(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("system-design",{"system-design/the-design-method-and-estimation":{"html":"\u003cp>System design is not drawing boxes. It is choosing which failure you can afford, and showing your reasoning. Strong designers follow the same order every time, so they never skip the step that would have exposed the flaw. This lesson gives that order and the arithmetic you need to size a system before you draw it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Follow six steps: clarify, estimate, sketch, deep-dive, failure modes, trade-offs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Turn users and behaviour into requests per second, storage and bandwidth\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pick the numbers that decide the design, and ignore the rest\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Write down assumptions so a reviewer can challenge them\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-six-steps\">The six steps\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Clarify.\u003c/strong> Who uses it, what must never be wrong, what may be slow, and what is the SLO? A design for a payment ledger and a design for a like counter are different even if the diagrams match.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Estimate.\u003c/strong> Requests per second, data size, growth, read to write ratio.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Sketch.\u003c/strong> The smallest design that meets the numbers: clients, entry point, service, store.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Deep-dive.\u003c/strong> Pick the one or two parts that carry the risk (the data store, the queue) and design them properly.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Failure modes.\u003c/strong> For every box ask what happens when it is slow, down, wrong or overloaded.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Trade-offs.\u003c/strong> Say what you gave up. Every design gives something up; naming it is the skill.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2 id=\"estimation-you-can-do-in-your-head\">Estimation you can do in your head\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Round hard. A day is about 100,000 seconds (86,400). So 1 million requests per day is about 10 per second. 100 million per day is about 1,000 per second.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Quantity\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Rule of thumb\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Seconds per day\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>86,400, call it 10^5\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Peak to average\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>2x to 10x; use 3x unless you know better\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>One modern server\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>thousands of simple requests per second, hundreds for heavy ones\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Memory read\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>around 100 nanoseconds\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>SSD random read\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>around 100 microseconds\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Same-region network round trip\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>around 0.5 to 1 millisecond\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cross-continent round trip\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>100 to 200 milliseconds\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Worked example: a service for 20 million daily users, each making 15 requests a day. That is 300 million requests a day, about 3,500 per second on average and about 10,000 per second at a 3x peak. Each response is 5 KB, so peak egress is about 50 MB per second, around 400 Mbit per second. Storage: if each user writes 1 KB a day, that is 20 GB a day, about 7 TB a year before replication. Three copies makes about 22 TB.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Those few lines already decide things: 10,000 per second needs several instances behind a balancer, not one; 22 TB a year does not fit one database node; the read to write ratio decides whether a cache pays.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"which-numbers-decide-the-design\">Which numbers decide the design\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Not every figure matters. Look for the number that crosses a threshold: one node&#39;s capacity, one network link, one partition&#39;s write limit. The design changes where a limit is crossed. If peak is 10,000 per second and one database handles 20,000 writes per second, you do not need sharding yet. Say so, and note at what growth you will.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"write-assumptions-down\">Write assumptions down\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Assumed 3x peak, 5 KB responses, 99.9 percent of reads can be 60 seconds stale.&quot; Each assumption is a place where the design can be wrong. Reviewers can then attack the assumption instead of guessing at it. Several real outages began as an assumption nobody wrote down. Pokemon GO launched in 2016 to 50 times the planned traffic and ten times the worst case estimate; the lesson is not that estimates fail, but that the design needs headroom and a way to scale when they do.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A service gets 50 million requests per day. Roughly how many per second on average, and what would you plan for at peak?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> About 500 per second on average (50 million divided by roughly 100,000 seconds); plan for around 1,500 per second at a 3x peak, then check that against the capacity of each tier.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Round to one significant figure. You are sizing, not billing.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Designs fail at the step skipped. Skip clarify and you build the wrong thing. Skip estimate and you build for the wrong size, either one node that melts or a sharded cluster nobody needs. Skip failure modes and the first outage writes them for you.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-launch-that-was-fifty-times-the-plan\">Incident: The launch that was fifty times the plan\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A game launched worldwide and traffic reached 50 times the initial target within days.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl top nodes | sort -k3 -nr | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl top nodes | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> -k3 -nr | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">gke-game-pool-7   7890m   98%   26Gi   91%\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">gke-game-pool-2   7810m   97%   25Gi   90%\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">gke-game-pool-9   7760m   97%   25Gi   88%\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The output above is a simulated illustration. In the real launch, Niantic and Google Cloud added tens of thousands of cores to the Kubernetes backend during the first days. What saved them was a design that could scale horizontally and a vendor who could provide capacity; the estimate itself was wrong by a wide margin. Read the case file for the sourced timeline.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Design a URL shortener for 100 million new links a month.&quot; Start by asking about read traffic, link lifetime and custom aliases. Estimate: 100 million a month is about 40 writes per second; reads are perhaps 100 times that, 4,000 per second, so caching matters far more than the write path.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Drawing before asking what the SLO is\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Precise arithmetic on guessed inputs (use round numbers)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Designing for the average instead of the peak\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never saying what the design gives up\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-six-steps","text":"The six steps"},{"id":"estimation-you-can-do-in-your-head","text":"Estimation you can do in your head"},{"id":"which-numbers-decide-the-design","text":"Which numbers decide the design"},{"id":"write-assumptions-down","text":"Write assumptions down"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-the-launch-that-was-fifty-times-the-plan","text":"Incident: The launch that was fifty times the plan"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Azure Well-Architected reliability","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/well-architected/reliability/"},{"t":"SRE book contents","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/table-of-contents/"}],"text":"System design is not drawing boxes. It is choosing which failure you can afford, and showing your reasoning. Strong designers follow the same order every time, so they never skip the step that would have exposed the flaw. This lesson gives that order and the arithmetic you need to size a system before you draw it. What you will learn Follow six steps: clarify, estimate, sketch, deep-dive, failure modes, trade-offs Turn users and behaviour into requests per second, storage and bandwidth Pick the numbers that decide the design, and ignore the rest Write down assumptions so a reviewer can challenge them The six steps Clarify. Who uses it, what must never be wrong, what may be slow, and what is the SLO? A design for a payment ledger and a design for a like counter are different even if the diagrams match. Estimate. Requests per second, data size, growth, read to write ratio. Sketch. The smallest design that meets the numbers: clients, entry point, service, store. Deep-dive. Pick the one or two parts that carry the risk (the data store, the queue) and design them properly. Failure modes. For every box ask what happens when it is slow, down, wrong or overloaded. Trade-offs. Say what you gave up. Every design gives something up; naming it is the skill. Estimation you can do in your head Round hard. A day is about 100,000 seconds (86,400). So 1 million requests per day is about 10 per second. 100 million per day is about 1,000 per second. Quantity Rule of thumb Seconds per day 86,400, call it 10^5 Peak to average 2x to 10x; use 3x unless you know better One modern server thousands of simple requests per second, hundreds for heavy ones Memory read around 100 nanoseconds SSD random read around 100 microseconds Same-region network round trip around 0.5 to 1 millisecond Cross-continent round trip 100 to 200 milliseconds Worked example: a service for 20 million daily users, each making 15 requests a day. That is 300 million requests a day, about 3,500 per second on average and about 10,000 per second at a 3x peak. Each response is 5 KB, so peak egress is about 50 MB per second, around 400 Mbit per second. Storage: if each user writes 1 KB a day, that is 20 GB a day, about 7 TB a year before replication. Three copies makes about 22 TB. Those few lines already decide things: 10,000 per second needs several instances behind a balancer, not one; 22 TB a year does not fit one database node; the read to write ratio decides whether a cache pays. Which numbers decide the design Not every figure matters. Look for the number that crosses a threshold: one node&#39;s capacity, one network link, one partition&#39;s write limit. The design changes where a limit is crossed. If peak is 10,000 per second and one database handles 20,000 writes per second, you do not need sharding yet. Say so, and note at what growth you will. Write assumptions down \"Assumed 3x peak, 5 KB responses, 99.9 percent of reads can be 60 seconds stale.\" Each assumption is a place where the design can be wrong. Reviewers can then attack the assumption instead of guessing at it. Several real outages began as an assumption nobody wrote down. Pokemon GO launched in 2016 to 50 times the planned traffic and ten times the worst case estimate; the lesson is not that estimates fail, but that the design needs headroom and a way to scale when they do. Predict first. A service gets 50 million requests per day. Roughly how many per second on average, and what would you plan for at peak? Your guess Answer: About 500 per second on average (50 million divided by roughly 100,000 seconds); plan for around 1,500 per second at a 3x peak, then check that against the capacity of each tier. Round to one significant figure. You are sizing, not billing. How it fails Designs fail at the step skipped. Skip clarify and you build the wrong thing. Skip estimate and you build for the wrong size, either one node that melts or a sharded cluster nobody needs. Skip failure modes and the first outage writes them for "},"system-design/caching-without-stampedes":{"html":"\u003cp>A cache trades freshness for speed and load. It is the cheapest way to make a read-heavy system fast, and the commonest way to make a recovery fail: when a cache goes cold, every request lands on the store it was protecting. Design the cache and the cold-cache case together.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choose a caching pattern: cache-aside, read-through, write-through, write-back\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Set TTLs and invalidate without serving wrong data for long\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Recognise and prevent stampedes, hot keys and cold starts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Decide when not to cache\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"patterns\">Patterns\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Cache-aside:\u003c/strong> the app reads the cache; on a miss it reads the database and fills the cache. Simple and common. \u003cstrong>Read-through:\u003c/strong> the cache itself loads on a miss. \u003cstrong>Write-through:\u003c/strong> writes go to cache and store together; reads are fresh, writes are slower. \u003cstrong>Write-back:\u003c/strong> writes go to the cache and flush later; fast, but data can be lost if the cache dies before flushing. Use write-back only when losing recent writes is acceptable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ttl-and-invalidation\">TTL and invalidation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A TTL bounds how stale data can be. It is also a promise: &quot;a user may see data up to this old&quot;. Pick it from the business rule, not from habit. Explicit invalidation (delete the key when the source changes) is fresher but fails in subtle ways: a delete that is lost leaves stale data until the TTL, so keep a TTL as a safety net even with invalidation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A classic race: reader A misses and reads old data from the database; writer updates the database and deletes the key; reader A then fills the cache with the old data. The cache is now stale until the TTL. Short TTLs on mutable data, or versioned keys, limit this.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-stampede\">The stampede\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A popular key expires. Within milliseconds, thousands of requests miss together and all query the database for the same row. The database slows, so the refill takes longer, so more requests pile in. Defences:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Request coalescing:\u003c/strong> one request refills, the others wait for it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Jittered TTLs:\u003c/strong> add random spread so keys do not expire together.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Serve stale while refreshing:\u003c/strong> return the old value and refresh in the background.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Early refresh:\u003c/strong> refresh a little before expiry for hot keys.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-cold-start\">The cold start\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>After a deploy, a cache restart or a failover, the cache is empty. If the database can only handle 10 percent of full traffic, a cold cache at full traffic is an outage. Plan for it: warm the cache before shifting traffic, ramp traffic slowly, and size the database for the miss rate you can reach, not only the steady state.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hot-keys-and-negative-caching\">Hot keys and negative caching\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>One key getting a large share of traffic overloads the single cache node that holds it. Replicate hot keys or keep a short local in-process cache in front. Also cache &quot;not found&quot; answers briefly, or a missing item will hit the database every time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-not-to-cache\">When not to cache\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Skip a cache when data must be exactly current (account balances shown for payment), when the hit rate would be low, or when the store is fast enough. A cache you cannot explain is another failure point.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A cache with a 60 second TTL on 10,000 popular keys is filled all at once at startup. What happens at the 60 second mark?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> All keys expire together, a burst of misses reaches the database at the same moment. Jitter the TTLs and coalesce refills.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Synchronised expiry is the stampede recipe.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Silently. Stale data appears wrong only to the user who saw it. A stampede shows as a database spike right after a cache event. Alert on hit rate, miss rate and database load together; a hit rate that drops while database load rises is the signal.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-recovery-that-needed-a-warm-cache\">Incident: Recovery that needed a warm cache\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a restart the cache was empty and the database fell over under the full read load.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"redis-cli info stats | grep keyspace\npsql -c &quot;select count(*) from pg_stat_activity where state='active'&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>redis-cli info stats | grep keyspace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">keyspace_hits:0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">keyspace_misses:482113\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;select count(*) from pg_stat_activity where state=&#x27;active&#x27;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> count\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   398\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This output is a simulated illustration of a common pattern, not one named outage. The database was sized for a 95 percent hit rate, so after the cache went empty it saw twenty times its normal read load. The team added request coalescing, warmed the cache from a replica before cutover, and ramped traffic in steps.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Your product page is cached for five minutes and a price change must show within seconds. What do you do?&quot; Invalidate on write, keep the TTL as a backstop, and consider versioned keys so a late fill cannot overwrite a newer value.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No jitter on TTLs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A database sized only for the warm-cache case\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using write-back for data you cannot lose\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No cache hit-rate alert\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"patterns","text":"Patterns"},{"id":"ttl-and-invalidation","text":"TTL and invalidation"},{"id":"the-stampede","text":"The stampede"},{"id":"the-cold-start","text":"The cold start"},{"id":"hot-keys-and-negative-caching","text":"Hot keys and negative caching"},{"id":"when-not-to-cache","text":"When not to cache"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-recovery-that-needed-a-warm-cache","text":"Incident: Recovery that needed a warm cache"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Caching challenges and strategies","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/caching-challenges-and-strategies/"},{"t":"Cache-aside pattern","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/cache-aside"}],"text":"A cache trades freshness for speed and load. It is the cheapest way to make a read-heavy system fast, and the commonest way to make a recovery fail: when a cache goes cold, every request lands on the store it was protecting. Design the cache and the cold-cache case together. What you will learn Choose a caching pattern: cache-aside, read-through, write-through, write-back Set TTLs and invalidate without serving wrong data for long Recognise and prevent stampedes, hot keys and cold starts Decide when not to cache Patterns Cache-aside: the app reads the cache; on a miss it reads the database and fills the cache. Simple and common. Read-through: the cache itself loads on a miss. Write-through: writes go to cache and store together; reads are fresh, writes are slower. Write-back: writes go to the cache and flush later; fast, but data can be lost if the cache dies before flushing. Use write-back only when losing recent writes is acceptable. TTL and invalidation A TTL bounds how stale data can be. It is also a promise: \"a user may see data up to this old\". Pick it from the business rule, not from habit. Explicit invalidation (delete the key when the source changes) is fresher but fails in subtle ways: a delete that is lost leaves stale data until the TTL, so keep a TTL as a safety net even with invalidation. A classic race: reader A misses and reads old data from the database; writer updates the database and deletes the key; reader A then fills the cache with the old data. The cache is now stale until the TTL. Short TTLs on mutable data, or versioned keys, limit this. The stampede A popular key expires. Within milliseconds, thousands of requests miss together and all query the database for the same row. The database slows, so the refill takes longer, so more requests pile in. Defences: Request coalescing: one request refills, the others wait for it. Jittered TTLs: add random spread so keys do not expire together. Serve stale while refreshing: return the old value and refresh in the background. Early refresh: refresh a little before expiry for hot keys. The cold start After a deploy, a cache restart or a failover, the cache is empty. If the database can only handle 10 percent of full traffic, a cold cache at full traffic is an outage. Plan for it: warm the cache before shifting traffic, ramp traffic slowly, and size the database for the miss rate you can reach, not only the steady state. Hot keys and negative caching One key getting a large share of traffic overloads the single cache node that holds it. Replicate hot keys or keep a short local in-process cache in front. Also cache \"not found\" answers briefly, or a missing item will hit the database every time. When not to cache Skip a cache when data must be exactly current (account balances shown for payment), when the hit rate would be low, or when the store is fast enough. A cache you cannot explain is another failure point. Predict first. A cache with a 60 second TTL on 10,000 popular keys is filled all at once at startup. What happens at the 60 second mark? Your guess Answer: All keys expire together, a burst of misses reaches the database at the same moment. Jitter the TTLs and coalesce refills. Synchronised expiry is the stampede recipe. How it fails Silently. Stale data appears wrong only to the user who saw it. A stampede shows as a database spike right after a cache event. Alert on hit rate, miss rate and database load together; a hit rate that drops while database load rises is the signal. Incident: Recovery that needed a warm cache Incident After a restart the cache was empty and the database fell over under the full read load. $ redis-cli info stats | grep keyspace keyspace_hits:0 keyspace_misses:482113 $ psql -c \"select count(*) from pg_stat_activity where state=&#x27;active&#x27;\" count ------- 398 This output is a simulated illustration of a common pattern, not one named outage. The database was sized for a 95 percent hit rate, so after the cache went empty it saw twe"},"system-design/queues-retries-and-idempotency":{"html":"\u003cp>Networks lose messages and services time out. The question is never whether a call will be repeated, but what happens when it is. Queues absorb bursts, retries recover from blips, and idempotency makes repeats harmless. Done carelessly, the same tools turn a small fault into a storm.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choose between a queue and a stream, and what ordering and delivery guarantees mean\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Set timeouts, retries, backoff and jitter so retries help instead of hurt\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Make operations idempotent with keys\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Apply backpressure and dead-letter queues\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"queues-and-streams\">Queues and streams\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>queue\u003c/strong> hands each message to one consumer and removes it after acknowledgement; good for work to be done once. A \u003cstrong>stream\u003c/strong> is an ordered log that many consumers read at their own pace and can replay; good for events and pipelines. Ordering is usually guaranteed only within a partition, so choose the partition key so related events stay together (for example the account id).\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Delivery guarantees:\u003c/strong> at-most-once (may lose), at-least-once (may repeat), exactly-once (in practice, at-least-once delivery plus idempotent handling). Assume at-least-once and design consumers to be safe on repeats.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"timeouts\">Timeouts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every call needs a timeout shorter than the caller&#39;s own timeout. Without one, a slow dependency ties up threads and connections until the caller fails too. Set timeouts from the dependency&#39;s real latency (for example just above its 99th percentile), and use a deadline that passes down the whole call chain so work is abandoned once the user has gone.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retries\">Retries\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Retry only errors that may succeed later (timeouts, 503, connection reset), not ones that never will (400, 404). Use exponential backoff with \u003cstrong>jitter\u003c/strong>: wait 100 ms, then up to 200, then up to 400, each randomised. Cap the attempts. Without jitter, thousands of clients retry at the same moments and hit the recovering service in waves.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Retry amplification:\u003c/strong> if each of three layers retries three times, one user request can become 27 calls to the bottom service during an incident, exactly when it is least able to cope. Retry at one layer, and use a \u003cstrong>retry budget\u003c/strong> (for example, retries may be at most 10 percent of requests).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"idempotency\">Idempotency\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An operation is idempotent if doing it twice has the same effect as once. Setting a value is; adding to a balance is not. Make non-idempotent operations safe with an \u003cstrong>idempotency key\u003c/strong>: the client sends a unique key, the server records the key with the result, and a repeat returns the stored result instead of acting again. Payment APIs use this so a retried charge does not bill twice. Store the key and the effect in the same transaction.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"backpressure-and-dead-letters\">Backpressure and dead letters\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If producers outrun consumers, queues grow without limit and latency climbs. Apply backpressure: bound the queue, reject or slow producers when it is full, and alert on queue age, not only length. Messages that fail repeatedly go to a \u003cstrong>dead-letter queue\u003c/strong> so one poison message does not block the rest; someone must watch it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A client times out calling a payment service and retries. The first call had actually succeeded. How do you avoid a double charge?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Send an idempotency key with the request; the server stores the key with the result and returns that result on the repeat instead of charging again.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The timeout tells you nothing about whether it worked.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Unbounded queues hide overload until memory is gone. Missing jitter creates retry waves. Layered retries multiply load. Consumers without idempotency double-apply work after a redelivery.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-when-retries-became-the-outage\">Incident: When retries became the outage\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A dependency slowed down and the clients that called it retried aggressively, keeping it overloaded long after the original fault.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -c &quot;retry attempt&quot; /var/log/api/app.log\ngrep -c &quot;request received&quot; /var/log/api/app.log\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;retry attempt&quot;\u003c/span> /var/log/api/app.log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">48211\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;request received&quot;\u003c/span> /var/log/api/app.log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">15870\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is a simulated illustration of the retry-storm pattern. About three attempts per request, summed across layers, kept the dependency saturated. No single public case is claimed here; the case files on overload and recovery show related effects. The fix was a retry budget, jittered backoff and retries at only one layer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;A queue consumer is slow and the backlog is growing. What do you check, and what do you change?&quot; Check consumer errors and retries, per-message time, partition skew and downstream latency; change by scaling consumers only if the downstream can take it, adding backpressure, and moving poison messages to a dead-letter queue.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Retrying at every layer\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retries with no jitter or cap\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming exactly-once delivery\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Alerting on queue length but not age\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"queues-and-streams","text":"Queues and streams"},{"id":"timeouts","text":"Timeouts"},{"id":"retries","text":"Retries"},{"id":"idempotency","text":"Idempotency"},{"id":"backpressure-and-dead-letters","text":"Backpressure and dead letters"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-when-retries-became-the-outage","text":"Incident: When retries became the outage"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Timeouts, retries and backoff with jitter","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/timeouts-retries-and-backoff-with-jitter/"},{"t":"Making retries safe with idempotent APIs","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/making-retries-safe-with-idempotent-APIs/"}],"text":"Networks lose messages and services time out. The question is never whether a call will be repeated, but what happens when it is. Queues absorb bursts, retries recover from blips, and idempotency makes repeats harmless. Done carelessly, the same tools turn a small fault into a storm. What you will learn Choose between a queue and a stream, and what ordering and delivery guarantees mean Set timeouts, retries, backoff and jitter so retries help instead of hurt Make operations idempotent with keys Apply backpressure and dead-letter queues Queues and streams A queue hands each message to one consumer and removes it after acknowledgement; good for work to be done once. A stream is an ordered log that many consumers read at their own pace and can replay; good for events and pipelines. Ordering is usually guaranteed only within a partition, so choose the partition key so related events stay together (for example the account id). Delivery guarantees: at-most-once (may lose), at-least-once (may repeat), exactly-once (in practice, at-least-once delivery plus idempotent handling). Assume at-least-once and design consumers to be safe on repeats. Timeouts Every call needs a timeout shorter than the caller&#39;s own timeout. Without one, a slow dependency ties up threads and connections until the caller fails too. Set timeouts from the dependency&#39;s real latency (for example just above its 99th percentile), and use a deadline that passes down the whole call chain so work is abandoned once the user has gone. Retries Retry only errors that may succeed later (timeouts, 503, connection reset), not ones that never will (400, 404). Use exponential backoff with jitter : wait 100 ms, then up to 200, then up to 400, each randomised. Cap the attempts. Without jitter, thousands of clients retry at the same moments and hit the recovering service in waves. Retry amplification: if each of three layers retries three times, one user request can become 27 calls to the bottom service during an incident, exactly when it is least able to cope. Retry at one layer, and use a retry budget (for example, retries may be at most 10 percent of requests). Idempotency An operation is idempotent if doing it twice has the same effect as once. Setting a value is; adding to a balance is not. Make non-idempotent operations safe with an idempotency key : the client sends a unique key, the server records the key with the result, and a repeat returns the stored result instead of acting again. Payment APIs use this so a retried charge does not bill twice. Store the key and the effect in the same transaction. Backpressure and dead letters If producers outrun consumers, queues grow without limit and latency climbs. Apply backpressure: bound the queue, reject or slow producers when it is full, and alert on queue age, not only length. Messages that fail repeatedly go to a dead-letter queue so one poison message does not block the rest; someone must watch it. Predict first. A client times out calling a payment service and retries. The first call had actually succeeded. How do you avoid a double charge? Your guess Answer: Send an idempotency key with the request; the server stores the key with the result and returns that result on the repeat instead of charging again. The timeout tells you nothing about whether it worked. How it fails Unbounded queues hide overload until memory is gone. Missing jitter creates retry waves. Layered retries multiply load. Consumers without idempotency double-apply work after a redelivery. Incident: When retries became the outage Incident A dependency slowed down and the clients that called it retried aggressively, keeping it overloaded long after the original fault. $ grep -c \"retry attempt\" /var/log/api/app.log 48211 $ grep -c \"request received\" /var/log/api/app.log 15870 This is a simulated illustration of the retry-storm pattern. About three attempts per request, summed across layers, kept the dependency saturated. No single public case is claimed "},"system-design/consistency-and-availability-in-practice":{"html":"\u003cp>When a network splits or a replica lags, a system must choose: answer with possibly stale data, or refuse. CAP names that choice. In daily work the sharper question is the trade between consistency and latency when nothing is broken. This lesson turns the theory into per-feature decisions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>State CAP and PACELC without the myths\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Describe strong, eventual, read-your-writes and causal consistency\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose a level per feature, not per system\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Spot replication lag as a source of user-visible bugs\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cap-stated-carefully\">CAP, stated carefully\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>During a network \u003cstrong>partition\u003c/strong>, a system cannot both stay \u003cstrong>consistent\u003c/strong> (every read sees the latest write) and stay \u003cstrong>available\u003c/strong> (every request gets a non-error answer). Partitions happen, so the real choice is what to give up while one is in progress. It is not a menu of any two of three at all times.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>PACELC\u003c/strong> adds the everyday case: if there is a Partition, choose Availability or Consistency; Else, choose Latency or Consistency. Even with a healthy network, waiting for replicas to confirm a write costs time. Most systems pay that cost somewhere.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"levels-you-will-meet\">Levels you will meet\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Strong (linearizable):\u003c/strong> reads see the latest completed write. Costs latency and availability.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Eventual:\u003c/strong> replicas converge given time; reads may be stale. Cheap and fast.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Read-your-writes:\u003c/strong> you see your own writes, others may lag. Fixes the most visible user bug.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Monotonic reads:\u003c/strong> you never see time go backwards between reads.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Causal:\u003c/strong> if B depends on A, everyone sees A first.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choose-per-feature\">Choose per feature\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A shopping site can use different levels at once. The cart: read-your-writes is enough. Stock count at checkout: strong, or reserve with a check at commit. The product description: eventual, cache freely. A bank balance after a transfer: strong. Ask for each piece of data: what is the cost of a wrong or stale answer, and what is the cost of an error or delay?\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"replication-lag\">Replication lag\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Read replicas lag. A user saves a profile, the page reloads from a replica, and the old profile shows. They save again, worse. Fixes: read from the primary for a short time after a write, route a user&#39;s reads to the same replica, or carry a version or timestamp token and wait until the replica has reached it. Measure lag and alert on it; lag also decides how much data a failover can lose.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"failover-and-what-it-sacrifices\">Failover and what it sacrifices\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Asynchronous replication keeps writes fast but a primary failure can lose the last writes. Synchronous replication avoids that loss but halts writes if the replica is unreachable. Many teams use one synchronous replica as the failover target. The GoCardless case in 2017 shows a design that had this intent but whose automatic promotion did not trigger, so the outage lasted until engineers promoted a node by hand.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A user updates their email and the next page still shows the old one, though the database has the new value. What is the likely cause and a cheap fix?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The read went to a lagging replica. Read from the primary briefly after a write, or use a version token (read-your-writes).\u003c/p>\u003cp>The data is fine; the read path is stale.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Eventual consistency fails as confusing, not as down: duplicated actions, lost updates when two writers race, double spends. Strong consistency fails as unavailable: writes block during a partition. Name which failure the feature can tolerate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-failover-that-did-not-fail-over\">Incident: The failover that did not fail over\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The primary database lost its disk array. The synchronous replica was healthy, but the cluster manager did not promote it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"crm status | head -5\ncrm resource promote ms_pgsql\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>crm status | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Online: [ db-a db-b ]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">db-a  (ocf::heartbeat:pgsql)  FAILED\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">db-b  (ocf::heartbeat:pgsql)  Started (not promoted)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>crm resource promote ms_pgsql\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This block is a simulated illustration of the pattern. In the real GoCardless incident on 10 October 2017, the API and dashboard were down about 1 hour 50 minutes until a manual promotion. The lesson for design: automatic failover is a feature to be tested on a schedule, not trusted because it exists. See the case file for sourced detail.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Users see their old data right after saving. Walk me through it.&quot; Replication lag or a cache; prove which by reading from the primary and checking cache keys; then pick read-your-writes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choosing one consistency level for the whole system\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading from replicas right after a write with no lag handling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never testing failover\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating CAP as three independent switches\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"cap-stated-carefully","text":"CAP, stated carefully"},{"id":"levels-you-will-meet","text":"Levels you will meet"},{"id":"choose-per-feature","text":"Choose per feature"},{"id":"replication-lag","text":"Replication lag"},{"id":"failover-and-what-it-sacrifices","text":"Failover and what it sacrifices"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-the-failover-that-did-not-fail-over","text":"Incident: The failover that did not fail over"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Consistency levels","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cosmos-db/consistency-levels"},{"t":"Managing critical state","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/managing-critical-state/"}],"text":"When a network splits or a replica lags, a system must choose: answer with possibly stale data, or refuse. CAP names that choice. In daily work the sharper question is the trade between consistency and latency when nothing is broken. This lesson turns the theory into per-feature decisions. What you will learn State CAP and PACELC without the myths Describe strong, eventual, read-your-writes and causal consistency Choose a level per feature, not per system Spot replication lag as a source of user-visible bugs CAP, stated carefully During a network partition , a system cannot both stay consistent (every read sees the latest write) and stay available (every request gets a non-error answer). Partitions happen, so the real choice is what to give up while one is in progress. It is not a menu of any two of three at all times. PACELC adds the everyday case: if there is a Partition, choose Availability or Consistency; Else, choose Latency or Consistency. Even with a healthy network, waiting for replicas to confirm a write costs time. Most systems pay that cost somewhere. Levels you will meet Strong (linearizable): reads see the latest completed write. Costs latency and availability. Eventual: replicas converge given time; reads may be stale. Cheap and fast. Read-your-writes: you see your own writes, others may lag. Fixes the most visible user bug. Monotonic reads: you never see time go backwards between reads. Causal: if B depends on A, everyone sees A first. Choose per feature A shopping site can use different levels at once. The cart: read-your-writes is enough. Stock count at checkout: strong, or reserve with a check at commit. The product description: eventual, cache freely. A bank balance after a transfer: strong. Ask for each piece of data: what is the cost of a wrong or stale answer, and what is the cost of an error or delay? Replication lag Read replicas lag. A user saves a profile, the page reloads from a replica, and the old profile shows. They save again, worse. Fixes: read from the primary for a short time after a write, route a user&#39;s reads to the same replica, or carry a version or timestamp token and wait until the replica has reached it. Measure lag and alert on it; lag also decides how much data a failover can lose. Failover and what it sacrifices Asynchronous replication keeps writes fast but a primary failure can lose the last writes. Synchronous replication avoids that loss but halts writes if the replica is unreachable. Many teams use one synchronous replica as the failover target. The GoCardless case in 2017 shows a design that had this intent but whose automatic promotion did not trigger, so the outage lasted until engineers promoted a node by hand. Predict first. A user updates their email and the next page still shows the old one, though the database has the new value. What is the likely cause and a cheap fix? Your guess Answer: The read went to a lagging replica. Read from the primary briefly after a write, or use a version token (read-your-writes). The data is fine; the read path is stale. How it fails Eventual consistency fails as confusing, not as down: duplicated actions, lost updates when two writers race, double spends. Strong consistency fails as unavailable: writes block during a partition. Name which failure the feature can tolerate. Incident: The failover that did not fail over Incident The primary database lost its disk array. The synchronous replica was healthy, but the cluster manager did not promote it. $ crm status | head -5 Online: [ db-a db-b ] db-a (ocf::heartbeat:pgsql) FAILED db-b (ocf::heartbeat:pgsql) Started (not promoted) $ crm resource promote ms_pgsql This block is a simulated illustration of the pattern. In the real GoCardless incident on 10 October 2017, the API and dashboard were down about 1 hour 50 minutes until a manual promotion. The lesson for design: automatic failover is a feature to be tested on a schedule, not trusted because it exists. See the case file for sourced de"},"system-design/quorum-leaders-and-split-brain":{"html":"\u003cp>Some jobs must have exactly one owner at a time: the database primary, the holder of a lock, the node that assigns work. Getting machines to agree on who that is, when messages are delayed and nodes pause, is the core problem of distributed systems. You will rarely implement consensus, but you must know how to use and operate it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Explain quorum, majority and why clusters use odd sizes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Describe leader election at the level of Raft style systems\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Define split brain and use fencing to prevent damage\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Know what happens when a consensus store is unhealthy\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"quorum\">Quorum\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>quorum\u003c/strong> is a majority: more than half. If writes need acknowledgement from a majority, any two majorities share at least one node, so a later read of a majority sees the latest write. With 3 nodes the quorum is 2 and one failure is tolerated. With 5 the quorum is 3 and two are tolerated. 4 nodes tolerate one failure, the same as 3, so use odd sizes. More nodes add fault tolerance but also latency, since more must acknowledge.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"leader-election\">Leader election\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Consensus systems such as etcd, ZooKeeper and Consul (using Raft or similar protocols) elect one leader. All writes go through it and are replicated to followers; a write is committed once a majority has it. If the leader stops sending heartbeats, followers wait a randomised timeout, one becomes a candidate, and wins with a majority of votes. Each leader period has a number called a term or epoch; messages from an old term are rejected.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Consequence: without a majority, the cluster cannot commit writes. A 3-node cluster that loses 2 nodes stops writing, by design. It chooses consistency over availability.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"split-brain\">Split brain\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Split brain is two nodes both acting as leader. It happens when a network partition, or a long pause, makes the old leader unaware it was replaced. A frozen process (garbage collection, a VM pause) wakes up and still believes it holds the lock. Two leaders write to the same data and corrupt it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fencing\">Fencing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The remedy is \u003cstrong>fencing\u003c/strong>: the resource itself refuses stale leaders. Give every leadership a monotonically increasing \u003cstrong>fencing token\u003c/strong>; the storage layer rejects any write whose token is lower than one it has already seen. Other forms: power off the old node (STONITH), revoke its network access, or use leases that expire and a store that checks them. A lease alone is not safe, because a paused process can outlive its lease; the token check is.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"operating-consensus-stores\">Operating consensus stores\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Treat etcd, ZooKeeper or Consul as critical infrastructure: run 3 or 5 members across failure domains, keep disks fast (they sync to disk on every commit), monitor leader changes and commit latency, take and test snapshots, and keep their load small. A flood of requests to the consensus store can make the whole platform stall. The 2021 Roblox outage involved a Consul cluster under heavy load and a streaming feature; see the case file for the sourced account.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A 5-node cluster loses 3 nodes in one zone. Can it commit writes?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> No. It has 2 of 5, which is not a majority. It stops accepting writes until a majority is restored, preferring consistency.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Quorum is more than half of the configured members.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Two leaders after a pause. A quorum lost because all members shared one zone. Slow disks causing constant re-elections. A cluster whose members were replaced faster than they could catch up.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-leader-that-did-not-know\">Incident: The leader that did not know\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A paused primary resumed after failover and kept writing, while the new primary also accepted writes.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -h old-primary -c &quot;select pg_is_in_recovery()&quot;\npsql -h new-primary -c &quot;select pg_is_in_recovery()&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -h old-primary -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;select pg_is_in_recovery()&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> pg_is_in_recovery\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-------------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> f\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -h new-primary -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;select pg_is_in_recovery()&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> pg_is_in_recovery\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-------------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> f\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is a simulated illustration of split brain, not one named outage. Both nodes report they are writable. The team had no fencing: the load balancer still sent some traffic to the old primary. They added a fencing step that cuts the old node off before promotion, and a check that only one writable primary exists.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;How do you stop two instances of a job both running?&quot; Use a lease from a consensus store plus a fencing token checked by the thing the job writes to; a lease alone cannot stop a paused process.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Even-sized or single-zone consensus clusters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trusting a lock lease without a fencing token\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting heavy application traffic on the consensus store\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never practising a member replacement\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"quorum","text":"Quorum"},{"id":"leader-election","text":"Leader election"},{"id":"split-brain","text":"Split brain"},{"id":"fencing","text":"Fencing"},{"id":"operating-consensus-stores","text":"Operating consensus stores"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-the-leader-that-did-not-know","text":"Incident: The leader that did not know"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Managing critical state","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/managing-critical-state/"},{"t":"etcd documentation","u":"https://etcd.io/docs/latest/"}],"text":"Some jobs must have exactly one owner at a time: the database primary, the holder of a lock, the node that assigns work. Getting machines to agree on who that is, when messages are delayed and nodes pause, is the core problem of distributed systems. You will rarely implement consensus, but you must know how to use and operate it. What you will learn Explain quorum, majority and why clusters use odd sizes Describe leader election at the level of Raft style systems Define split brain and use fencing to prevent damage Know what happens when a consensus store is unhealthy Quorum A quorum is a majority: more than half. If writes need acknowledgement from a majority, any two majorities share at least one node, so a later read of a majority sees the latest write. With 3 nodes the quorum is 2 and one failure is tolerated. With 5 the quorum is 3 and two are tolerated. 4 nodes tolerate one failure, the same as 3, so use odd sizes. More nodes add fault tolerance but also latency, since more must acknowledge. Leader election Consensus systems such as etcd, ZooKeeper and Consul (using Raft or similar protocols) elect one leader. All writes go through it and are replicated to followers; a write is committed once a majority has it. If the leader stops sending heartbeats, followers wait a randomised timeout, one becomes a candidate, and wins with a majority of votes. Each leader period has a number called a term or epoch; messages from an old term are rejected. Consequence: without a majority, the cluster cannot commit writes. A 3-node cluster that loses 2 nodes stops writing, by design. It chooses consistency over availability. Split brain Split brain is two nodes both acting as leader. It happens when a network partition, or a long pause, makes the old leader unaware it was replaced. A frozen process (garbage collection, a VM pause) wakes up and still believes it holds the lock. Two leaders write to the same data and corrupt it. Fencing The remedy is fencing : the resource itself refuses stale leaders. Give every leadership a monotonically increasing fencing token ; the storage layer rejects any write whose token is lower than one it has already seen. Other forms: power off the old node (STONITH), revoke its network access, or use leases that expire and a store that checks them. A lease alone is not safe, because a paused process can outlive its lease; the token check is. Operating consensus stores Treat etcd, ZooKeeper or Consul as critical infrastructure: run 3 or 5 members across failure domains, keep disks fast (they sync to disk on every commit), monitor leader changes and commit latency, take and test snapshots, and keep their load small. A flood of requests to the consensus store can make the whole platform stall. The 2021 Roblox outage involved a Consul cluster under heavy load and a streaming feature; see the case file for the sourced account. Predict first. A 5-node cluster loses 3 nodes in one zone. Can it commit writes? Your guess Answer: No. It has 2 of 5, which is not a majority. It stops accepting writes until a majority is restored, preferring consistency. Quorum is more than half of the configured members. How it fails Two leaders after a pause. A quorum lost because all members shared one zone. Slow disks causing constant re-elections. A cluster whose members were replaced faster than they could catch up. Incident: The leader that did not know Incident A paused primary resumed after failover and kept writing, while the new primary also accepted writes. $ psql -h old-primary -c \"select pg_is_in_recovery()\" pg_is_in_recovery ------------------- f $ psql -h new-primary -c \"select pg_is_in_recovery()\" pg_is_in_recovery ------------------- f This is a simulated illustration of split brain, not one named outage. Both nodes report they are writable. The team had no fencing: the load balancer still sent some traffic to the old primary. They added a fencing step that cuts the old node off before promotion, and a check that only one"},"system-design/clocks-time-and-ordering":{"html":"\u003cp>Programs treat time as a simple number. In a distributed system it is not. Clocks drift, jump and disagree, and the same instant is seen differently by two machines. Designs that assume otherwise lose writes, expire locks early and crash on a leap second.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Distinguish wall-clock time from monotonic time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Explain clock skew, jumps and leap seconds\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Order events with logical clocks and versions instead of timestamps\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Design timeouts and expiry that survive clock changes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"two-kinds-of-clock\">Two kinds of clock\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Wall-clock time\u003c/strong> is the calendar date and time, synchronised by NTP. It can jump forwards or backwards when corrected. \u003cstrong>Monotonic time\u003c/strong> only moves forward and is meant for measuring durations. Use the monotonic clock for timeouts and elapsed time, and wall-clock only for showing dates and for coarse expiry.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"skew-and-drift\">Skew and drift\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Two servers can differ by milliseconds or, if sync breaks, by seconds or more. Last-write-wins by timestamp then silently discards the newer write when it comes from the slower clock. Do not use machine timestamps to decide which of two concurrent writes is correct.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"leap-seconds-and-other-jumps\">Leap seconds and other jumps\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>At midnight UTC on 1 January 2017, a leap second was inserted. Cloudflare&#39;s custom DNS software computed a time difference that became negative, a value the code assumed could not happen, and a call that panics on negative input failed. Customers with CNAME records saw errors until about 06:45 UTC. Other time bugs: expired certificates, date rollovers, daylight saving transitions, and a Microsoft Exchange date check that failed at the start of 2022. The pattern: code that assumes time only moves forward, evenly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ordering-without-wall-clocks\">Ordering without wall clocks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Logical clocks (Lamport):\u003c/strong> a counter that increases with each event and each received message, giving an order consistent with cause and effect.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Version vectors:\u003c/strong> per-replica counters that detect true concurrency, so a system can keep both versions and ask for a merge.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Database sequence or log offset:\u003c/strong> let one authority assign order, as a stream partition does.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Hybrid logical clocks:\u003c/strong> physical time plus a counter, close to real time and still ordered.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Some databases bound clock uncertainty with special hardware and then wait out the uncertainty before committing. You do not need this, but know the idea: if you must use time for ordering, state the error bound and handle it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"practical-rules\">Practical rules\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Run time sync everywhere and alert when offset grows.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use monotonic clocks for durations and timeouts.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never compute with a time difference you assume is positive; clamp or handle it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Store UTC; convert for display only.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Test with the clock moved: forwards, backwards, a leap second, a daylight saving change, certificate expiry.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Track every certificate expiry date; they are clocks too.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Two replicas accept writes to the same key, and the system keeps the one with the later timestamp. One server's clock is 3 seconds slow. What goes wrong?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> A newer write from the slow server can carry an older timestamp and be discarded, losing data silently. Use version vectors or a single ordering authority instead.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Timestamps are not a safe tie-breaker across machines.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Silently, until the date arrives. A negative duration, an early expiry, a lock that two holders believe they own, a certificate that lapsed because nobody tracked it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-leap-second-and-a-negative-number\">Incident: A leap second and a negative number\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>At midnight UTC a time difference computed by the DNS software was negative and a function that rejects negative values panicked.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"dig +short app.example.com CNAME\njournalctl -u rrdns --since &quot;00:00&quot; | grep -i panic | head -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dig +short app.example.com CNAME\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">;; connection timed out; no servers could be reached\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>journalctl -u rrdns --since \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;00:00&quot;\u003c/span> | grep -i panic | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">panic: negative duration passed to random selection\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The console output is a simulated illustration; the real timeline and Cloudflare&#39;s own explanation are in the case file. The fix was to handle the negative value, and the lasting lesson is to treat time inputs as untrusted and to test timestamps that move backwards.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;How do you make sure only one worker processes a job when clocks differ?&quot; Do not depend on timestamps. Use a lock with a fencing token, or let a queue partition assign each job to a single consumer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Last-write-wins by wall-clock timestamp\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measuring durations with the wall clock\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming time differences are never negative\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No alert on clock offset or certificate expiry\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"two-kinds-of-clock","text":"Two kinds of clock"},{"id":"skew-and-drift","text":"Skew and drift"},{"id":"leap-seconds-and-other-jumps","text":"Leap seconds and other jumps"},{"id":"ordering-without-wall-clocks","text":"Ordering without wall clocks"},{"id":"practical-rules","text":"Practical rules"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-a-leap-second-and-a-negative-number","text":"Incident: A leap second and a negative number"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"How the leap second affected Cloudflare DNS","u":"https://blog.cloudflare.com/how-and-why-the-leap-second-affected-cloudflare-dns/"},{"t":"RFC 5905 Network Time Protocol v4","u":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5905"}],"text":"Programs treat time as a simple number. In a distributed system it is not. Clocks drift, jump and disagree, and the same instant is seen differently by two machines. Designs that assume otherwise lose writes, expire locks early and crash on a leap second. What you will learn Distinguish wall-clock time from monotonic time Explain clock skew, jumps and leap seconds Order events with logical clocks and versions instead of timestamps Design timeouts and expiry that survive clock changes Two kinds of clock Wall-clock time is the calendar date and time, synchronised by NTP. It can jump forwards or backwards when corrected. Monotonic time only moves forward and is meant for measuring durations. Use the monotonic clock for timeouts and elapsed time, and wall-clock only for showing dates and for coarse expiry. Skew and drift Two servers can differ by milliseconds or, if sync breaks, by seconds or more. Last-write-wins by timestamp then silently discards the newer write when it comes from the slower clock. Do not use machine timestamps to decide which of two concurrent writes is correct. Leap seconds and other jumps At midnight UTC on 1 January 2017, a leap second was inserted. Cloudflare&#39;s custom DNS software computed a time difference that became negative, a value the code assumed could not happen, and a call that panics on negative input failed. Customers with CNAME records saw errors until about 06:45 UTC. Other time bugs: expired certificates, date rollovers, daylight saving transitions, and a Microsoft Exchange date check that failed at the start of 2022. The pattern: code that assumes time only moves forward, evenly. Ordering without wall clocks Logical clocks (Lamport): a counter that increases with each event and each received message, giving an order consistent with cause and effect. Version vectors: per-replica counters that detect true concurrency, so a system can keep both versions and ask for a merge. Database sequence or log offset: let one authority assign order, as a stream partition does. Hybrid logical clocks: physical time plus a counter, close to real time and still ordered. Some databases bound clock uncertainty with special hardware and then wait out the uncertainty before committing. You do not need this, but know the idea: if you must use time for ordering, state the error bound and handle it. Practical rules Run time sync everywhere and alert when offset grows. Use monotonic clocks for durations and timeouts. Never compute with a time difference you assume is positive; clamp or handle it. Store UTC; convert for display only. Test with the clock moved: forwards, backwards, a leap second, a daylight saving change, certificate expiry. Track every certificate expiry date; they are clocks too. Predict first. Two replicas accept writes to the same key, and the system keeps the one with the later timestamp. One server's clock is 3 seconds slow. What goes wrong? Your guess Answer: A newer write from the slow server can carry an older timestamp and be discarded, losing data silently. Use version vectors or a single ordering authority instead. Timestamps are not a safe tie-breaker across machines. How it fails Silently, until the date arrives. A negative duration, an early expiry, a lock that two holders believe they own, a certificate that lapsed because nobody tracked it. Incident: A leap second and a negative number Incident At midnight UTC a time difference computed by the DNS software was negative and a function that rejects negative values panicked. $ dig +short app.example.com CNAME ;; connection timed out; no servers could be reached $ journalctl -u rrdns --since \"00:00\" | grep -i panic | head -2 panic: negative duration passed to random selection The console output is a simulated illustration; the real timeline and Cloudflare&#39;s own explanation are in the case file. The fix was to handle the negative value, and the lasting lesson is to treat time inputs as untrusted and to test timestamps that move backw"},"system-design/partitioning-sharding-and-consistent-hashing":{"html":"\u003cp>One node always has a ceiling: disk size, write throughput, memory, a file system limit. Partitioning splits data across nodes so each holds a share. It is the main way storage scales, and it is hard to change later, so the choices you make early (the key, the scheme, the number of partitions) matter more than the technology.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choose between range, hash and directory partitioning\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pick a partition key and spot hot keys and skew\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Explain consistent hashing and why it limits data movement\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Plan resharding and cross-partition queries\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-partition\">Why partition\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Reasons are size (data no longer fits), write rate (one primary cannot take the writes) and blast radius (a fault should reach only one partition). Replication copies the same data to more nodes for availability and read scale; it does not help writes or size. Partitioning divides data; replication copies it. Real systems do both: each partition has its own replicas.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"schemes\">Schemes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Range:\u003c/strong> keys A to F on node 1, G to M on node 2. Range scans are cheap, but sequential keys (timestamps, auto-increment ids) pile all new writes on the last range, a hot spot.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Hash:\u003c/strong> hash the key and take it modulo N, or map it to a range of hash values. Spreads load evenly; range scans must visit every partition.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Directory (lookup):\u003c/strong> a table says which partition owns each key or tenant. Flexible, and the directory becomes a critical dependency to cache and protect.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing-the-key\">Choosing the key\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A good key has many distinct values, spreads reads and writes evenly, and keeps data that is used together on the same partition. A tenant id keeps one customer&#39;s data together, which suits a cell design, but one very large tenant becomes a hot partition. A user id spreads well. A country code does not (a few huge values). Ask: what is the biggest value of this key, and what fraction of traffic does it carry?\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hot-keys-and-skew\">Hot keys and skew\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Even with a hash, one key can dominate: a celebrity account, a viral item, a default value used by millions. Remedies: add a random suffix to spread one key over several (and read all of them), cache the hot key in front, split the hot entity into smaller pieces, or give large tenants their own partitions. Monitor per-partition load, not only the average.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"modulo-is-a-trap-consistent-hashing-is-the-fix\">Modulo is a trap, consistent hashing is the fix\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>With \u003ccode>hash(key) mod N\u003c/code>, changing N from 10 to 11 moves about 90 percent of keys to different nodes, a storm of data movement and cache misses. \u003cstrong>Consistent hashing\u003c/strong> places nodes and keys on a ring; a key belongs to the next node clockwise. Adding or removing one node moves only about 1/N of the keys. To even out load, each physical node takes many positions on the ring (virtual nodes). Many caches and distributed stores use it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Another approach is a fixed, large number of logical partitions (for example 1,024) mapped to nodes. Moving a partition moves a whole unit, and the partition count never changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cross-partition-work\">Cross-partition work\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Queries that span partitions (joins, global secondary indexes, uniqueness checks, transactions) are slow or hard. Design so the common case touches one partition. Use a local index per partition and scatter-gather for rare global queries, accept eventual consistency for global indexes, and use sagas or outbox patterns rather than distributed transactions where you can.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"resharding\">Resharding\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Plan it before you need it: choose a scheme that splits without downtime (split a partition in two while it serves traffic), copy data in the background, switch with a version of the directory, verify, then delete. Waiting until the node is full leaves no room to copy.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A table is partitioned by creation timestamp ranges, one range per day. Writes are all for today. What happens, and what would you change?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Today's partition takes every write while older ones sit idle, a hot spot. Hash a high-cardinality key (such as user id) for writes, or add a hashed prefix, and keep time ranges for archival or scans only.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sequential keys concentrate load at one end.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A partition key chosen for convenience that skews. A modulo scheme that makes growth an outage. A directory that is a single point. A rebalance that saturates the network and slows production. A limit nobody watched until the single node hit it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-table-that-hit-a-size-limit\">Incident: A table that hit a size limit\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A single database table on one node grew until it reached a file size limit, and the service stopped.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ls -lh /var/lib/mysql/shop/bookmarks.ibd\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> -lh /var/lib/mysql/shop/bookmarks.ibd\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-rw-rw---- 1 mysql mysql 2.0T bookmarks.ibd\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">mysql&gt; insert into bookmarks values (...);\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ERROR 1114 (HY000): The table 'bookmarks' is full\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The console lines are a simulated illustration. In the real Instapaper incident on 9 February 2017, a MySQL table on Amazon RDS reached a 2 TB file size limit; the service was down for about 31 hours and archive access was limited for about five days. The design lesson is to track the distance to every single-node limit, and to partition or archive while there is room to copy. See the case file.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Design the storage for a service that will hold 50 TB of user events.&quot; Estimate rate and growth, choose a hashed user id as the key with a large fixed partition count, replicate each partition, handle hot users, and describe how you would add nodes without moving most of the data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Sequential partition keys\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>hash mod N\u003c/code> with no plan for growth\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring one huge tenant or key\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No alert on distance to a node limit\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"why-partition","text":"Why partition"},{"id":"schemes","text":"Schemes"},{"id":"choosing-the-key","text":"Choosing the key"},{"id":"hot-keys-and-skew","text":"Hot keys and skew"},{"id":"modulo-is-a-trap-consistent-hashing-is-the-fix","text":"Modulo is a trap, consistent hashing is the fix"},{"id":"cross-partition-work","text":"Cross-partition work"},{"id":"resharding","text":"Resharding"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-a-table-that-hit-a-size-limit","text":"Incident: A table that hit a size limit"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Workload isolation using shuffle sharding","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/workload-isolation-using-shuffle-sharding/"},{"t":"Sharding pattern","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/sharding"}],"text":"One node always has a ceiling: disk size, write throughput, memory, a file system limit. Partitioning splits data across nodes so each holds a share. It is the main way storage scales, and it is hard to change later, so the choices you make early (the key, the scheme, the number of partitions) matter more than the technology. What you will learn Choose between range, hash and directory partitioning Pick a partition key and spot hot keys and skew Explain consistent hashing and why it limits data movement Plan resharding and cross-partition queries Why partition Reasons are size (data no longer fits), write rate (one primary cannot take the writes) and blast radius (a fault should reach only one partition). Replication copies the same data to more nodes for availability and read scale; it does not help writes or size. Partitioning divides data; replication copies it. Real systems do both: each partition has its own replicas. Schemes Range: keys A to F on node 1, G to M on node 2. Range scans are cheap, but sequential keys (timestamps, auto-increment ids) pile all new writes on the last range, a hot spot. Hash: hash the key and take it modulo N, or map it to a range of hash values. Spreads load evenly; range scans must visit every partition. Directory (lookup): a table says which partition owns each key or tenant. Flexible, and the directory becomes a critical dependency to cache and protect. Choosing the key A good key has many distinct values, spreads reads and writes evenly, and keeps data that is used together on the same partition. A tenant id keeps one customer&#39;s data together, which suits a cell design, but one very large tenant becomes a hot partition. A user id spreads well. A country code does not (a few huge values). Ask: what is the biggest value of this key, and what fraction of traffic does it carry? Hot keys and skew Even with a hash, one key can dominate: a celebrity account, a viral item, a default value used by millions. Remedies: add a random suffix to spread one key over several (and read all of them), cache the hot key in front, split the hot entity into smaller pieces, or give large tenants their own partitions. Monitor per-partition load, not only the average. Modulo is a trap, consistent hashing is the fix With hash(key) mod N , changing N from 10 to 11 moves about 90 percent of keys to different nodes, a storm of data movement and cache misses. Consistent hashing places nodes and keys on a ring; a key belongs to the next node clockwise. Adding or removing one node moves only about 1/N of the keys. To even out load, each physical node takes many positions on the ring (virtual nodes). Many caches and distributed stores use it. Another approach is a fixed, large number of logical partitions (for example 1,024) mapped to nodes. Moving a partition moves a whole unit, and the partition count never changes. Cross-partition work Queries that span partitions (joins, global secondary indexes, uniqueness checks, transactions) are slow or hard. Design so the common case touches one partition. Use a local index per partition and scatter-gather for rare global queries, accept eventual consistency for global indexes, and use sagas or outbox patterns rather than distributed transactions where you can. Resharding Plan it before you need it: choose a scheme that splits without downtime (split a partition in two while it serves traffic), copy data in the background, switch with a version of the directory, verify, then delete. Waiting until the node is full leaves no room to copy. Predict first. A table is partitioned by creation timestamp ranges, one range per day. Writes are all for today. What happens, and what would you change? Your guess Answer: Today's partition takes every write while older ones sit idle, a hot spot. Hash a high-cardinality key (such as user id) for writes, or add a hashed prefix, and keep time ranges for archival or scans only. Sequential keys concentrate load at one end. How it fails A partition"},"system-design/storage-engines-and-replication-topologies":{"html":"\u003cp>You do not need to write a database, but you must know what is underneath one, because capacity limits, latency spikes and recovery times come from it. This lesson covers the two main storage engine families and the three ways to replicate, with an eye on how each fails.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Compare B-tree and LSM tree storage engines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Explain write-ahead logs, compaction and vacuum, and why they matter in operations\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose a replication topology and know its conflict risk\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Spot internal limits such as transaction ids and metadata growth\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"two-storage-engine-families\">Two storage engine families\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>B-tree\u003c/strong> engines (many relational databases) keep sorted pages on disk and update them in place. Reads are predictable and a point lookup touches a few pages. Writes modify pages, so each write costs random I/O, and a write-ahead log (WAL) records changes first so a crash can be recovered.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>LSM tree\u003c/strong> engines (many key-value and wide-column stores) append writes to a memory table and a log, flush sorted files, and merge them in the background (\u003cstrong>compaction\u003c/strong>). Writes are fast and sequential. Reads may check several files, helped by Bloom filters, and compaction competes with foreground traffic for disk. A burst of writes can build a compaction backlog and push read latency up.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Rule of thumb: LSM favours write-heavy workloads and has to be watched for compaction debt; B-tree favours read-heavy, update-in-place workloads and has to be watched for index bloat and vacuum.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"background-work-is-part-of-the-design\">Background work is part of the design\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Both families clean up behind the scenes: vacuum and autovacuum, compaction, checkpointing, log truncation, metadata cleanup. When they fall behind, nothing fails at first, then something fails abruptly. Monitor their lag directly. In PostgreSQL, a table whose old row versions are not vacuumed can eventually exhaust transaction ids and the database stops accepting writes to protect itself. Sentry&#39;s database did this in 2015 and a Mandrill shard in 2019, with outages of most of a working day and about 40.5 hours respectively. See the case files.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"replication-topologies\">Replication topologies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Leader and followers:\u003c/strong> one node takes writes and ships a log to followers. Simple and consistent. Failover needs a promotion and fencing, and asynchronous followers can lose recent writes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Multi-leader:\u003c/strong> several nodes accept writes, often one per region, and replicate to each other. Good for write latency and regional independence. The price is \u003cstrong>conflicts\u003c/strong>: two leaders change the same row. You need a rule (last write wins, merge, or application logic) and you must accept that some writes are altered or lost.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Leaderless (quorum):\u003c/strong> any replica accepts writes; a write succeeds when W of N replicas acknowledge, a read asks R, and R + W &gt; N gives overlap. High availability, tunable consistency, and repair work (read repair, anti-entropy) to converge.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"synchronous-and-asynchronous\">Synchronous and asynchronous\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Synchronous replication waits for a follower before acknowledging: no loss on failover, but latency, and a dead follower can block writes. Asynchronous acknowledges at once: fast, but the last writes can be lost. A common middle path is one synchronous follower plus asynchronous ones.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"metadata-and-hidden-growth\">Metadata and hidden growth\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Systems also keep metadata: segment lists, tiered storage indexes, file catalogues. If cleanup is not enabled, it grows quietly. In May 2024 Honeycomb found one Kafka broker reading far more from disk than its peers because tiered storage metadata had grown for years without cleanup; enabling the cleaner cut metadata by over 99 percent and broker start-up fell from over 20 minutes to under one minute. Track metadata size and start-up time as signals.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing\">Choosing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Ask about the access pattern (point lookups, scans, writes per second), the consistency each feature needs, the growth, and the operations your team can actually run. A familiar relational database with replicas and partitions solves more problems than a surprising number of teams expect.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A write-heavy store on an LSM engine shows rising read latency and disk busy at 100 percent, with traffic unchanged. What is the likely cause?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Compaction has fallen behind, so reads check many files and compaction competes for disk. Throttle writes, add I/O capacity or tune compaction, and alert on pending compactions.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Background debt shows up as foreground latency.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Vacuum or compaction lag. WAL or log segments filling a disk. A follower that falls so far behind it cannot catch up. Multi-leader conflicts resolved silently by last write wins. Metadata that grows until start-up takes an hour.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-database-that-refused-writes-to-protect-itself\">Incident: A database that refused writes to protect itself\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A database stopped accepting writes because it was nearing a hard internal limit after cleanup had not kept up.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ERROR:  database is not accepting commands to avoid wraparound data loss in database &quot;app&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">HINT:  Stop the postmaster and vacuum that database in single-user mode.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is a simulated rendering of the pattern, not a quotation. The real incidents are Sentry on 20 July 2015 and Mandrill on 4 February 2019; the case files give sources and timelines. The design response is to monitor the distance to the limit, keep cleanup tuned and tested, and keep the ability to take a table out of service quickly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Why might a write-heavy workload prefer an LSM engine, and what do you watch?&quot; Sequential writes and fast ingestion; watch compaction backlog, read amplification, space amplification and disk I/O.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating background cleanup as optional\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Multi-leader replication with no conflict plan\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One synchronous replica that blocks all writes when it dies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No alert on replica lag or metadata growth\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"two-storage-engine-families","text":"Two storage engine families"},{"id":"background-work-is-part-of-the-design","text":"Background work is part of the design"},{"id":"replication-topologies","text":"Replication topologies"},{"id":"synchronous-and-asynchronous","text":"Synchronous and asynchronous"},{"id":"metadata-and-hidden-growth","text":"Metadata and hidden growth"},{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-a-database-that-refused-writes-to-protect-itself","text":"Incident: A database that refused writes to protect itself"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL routine vacuuming","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/routine-vacuuming.html"},{"t":"Managing critical state","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/managing-critical-state/"}],"text":"You do not need to write a database, but you must know what is underneath one, because capacity limits, latency spikes and recovery times come from it. This lesson covers the two main storage engine families and the three ways to replicate, with an eye on how each fails. What you will learn Compare B-tree and LSM tree storage engines Explain write-ahead logs, compaction and vacuum, and why they matter in operations Choose a replication topology and know its conflict risk Spot internal limits such as transaction ids and metadata growth Two storage engine families B-tree engines (many relational databases) keep sorted pages on disk and update them in place. Reads are predictable and a point lookup touches a few pages. Writes modify pages, so each write costs random I/O, and a write-ahead log (WAL) records changes first so a crash can be recovered. LSM tree engines (many key-value and wide-column stores) append writes to a memory table and a log, flush sorted files, and merge them in the background ( compaction ). Writes are fast and sequential. Reads may check several files, helped by Bloom filters, and compaction competes with foreground traffic for disk. A burst of writes can build a compaction backlog and push read latency up. Rule of thumb: LSM favours write-heavy workloads and has to be watched for compaction debt; B-tree favours read-heavy, update-in-place workloads and has to be watched for index bloat and vacuum. Background work is part of the design Both families clean up behind the scenes: vacuum and autovacuum, compaction, checkpointing, log truncation, metadata cleanup. When they fall behind, nothing fails at first, then something fails abruptly. Monitor their lag directly. In PostgreSQL, a table whose old row versions are not vacuumed can eventually exhaust transaction ids and the database stops accepting writes to protect itself. Sentry&#39;s database did this in 2015 and a Mandrill shard in 2019, with outages of most of a working day and about 40.5 hours respectively. See the case files. Replication topologies Leader and followers: one node takes writes and ships a log to followers. Simple and consistent. Failover needs a promotion and fencing, and asynchronous followers can lose recent writes. Multi-leader: several nodes accept writes, often one per region, and replicate to each other. Good for write latency and regional independence. The price is conflicts : two leaders change the same row. You need a rule (last write wins, merge, or application logic) and you must accept that some writes are altered or lost. Leaderless (quorum): any replica accepts writes; a write succeeds when W of N replicas acknowledge, a read asks R, and R + W > N gives overlap. High availability, tunable consistency, and repair work (read repair, anti-entropy) to converge. Synchronous and asynchronous Synchronous replication waits for a follower before acknowledging: no loss on failover, but latency, and a dead follower can block writes. Asynchronous acknowledges at once: fast, but the last writes can be lost. A common middle path is one synchronous follower plus asynchronous ones. Metadata and hidden growth Systems also keep metadata: segment lists, tiered storage indexes, file catalogues. If cleanup is not enabled, it grows quietly. In May 2024 Honeycomb found one Kafka broker reading far more from disk than its peers because tiered storage metadata had grown for years without cleanup; enabling the cleaner cut metadata by over 99 percent and broker start-up fell from over 20 minutes to under one minute. Track metadata size and start-up time as signals. Choosing Ask about the access pattern (point lookups, scans, writes per second), the consistency each feature needs, the growth, and the operations your team can actually run. A familiar relational database with replicas and partitions solves more problems than a surprising number of teams expect. Predict first. A write-heavy store on an LSM engine shows rising read latency and disk busy a"},"system-design/backup-restore-and-object-storage-design":{"html":"\u003cp>A backup is a hypothesis that you can recover. It becomes a fact only when you restore it, and the failure that takes your data often takes the backups with it. This lesson covers how to design for recovery: what to copy, where, how long it takes, and how to prove it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Set backup frequency and retention from RPO and RTO\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Apply the 3-2-1 idea and keep copies independent of the failure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Test restores and measure real recovery time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use object storage well: durability, versioning, lifecycle and consistency\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"start-from-rpo-and-rto\">Start from RPO and RTO\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>RPO says how much data you can lose, so how often you must capture changes. RTO says how long you can be down, so how fast a restore must be. A 5 minute RPO needs continuous log shipping or point-in-time recovery, not a nightly dump. A 1 hour RTO for 2 TB needs a restore path that moves 2 TB in well under an hour: at 200 MB per second, 2 TB takes about 2.8 hours, so a nightly full restore alone fails the target and you need replicas, snapshots or a hot standby.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"kinds-of-copies\">Kinds of copies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Replicas:\u003c/strong> protect against node failure, but replicate deletes and corruption instantly.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Snapshots:\u003c/strong> fast and cheap, often in the same account and failure domain.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Backups with point-in-time recovery:\u003c/strong> a base copy plus a log of changes, so you can restore to a moment before a mistake.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Archives:\u003c/strong> long retention for compliance, slow to restore.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Replication is not backup. A script that deletes the wrong thing deletes it everywhere within seconds. In April 2022 a script given the wrong kind of identifier deleted 883 customer sites at Atlassian, and full restoration took until 18 April; the case file explains why restoring many individual sites was a long, careful process. Plan restores for the unit you could lose, not only for a whole database.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"independence\">Independence\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keep at least one copy that a single event cannot reach: another account, another region and, for important data, another provider. Protect it with separate credentials and write-once or versioned storage so an attacker or a bad script cannot erase it. In May 2024 UniSuper&#39;s cloud subscription was deleted by a provisioning misconfiguration that spanned both of its geographies; backups held with another provider were part of how it recovered over days. Whatever name you give it, the principle is the same: a backup in the same account and control plane as the data shares its fate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"test-restores\">Test restores\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Schedule regular restore tests, automatically if possible, to a clean environment. Measure the real time, check data integrity (row counts, checksums, an application smoke test), and record it against the RTO. Practise the unpleasant cases: restoring to a point in time, restoring a single table, restoring when the primary credentials are unavailable. A backup job that reports success proves only that a file was written. In August 2023 Toyota&#39;s backup system failed along with the main system after a disk space error, so there was no automatic switchover; the case file has the details.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"object-storage-by-design\">Object storage by design\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Object stores hold large amounts of data cheaply and durably, with high availability and simple APIs. Know their shape: objects are immutable blobs addressed by key, not files you edit in place; listing is slower than lookup; per-prefix request rates can matter; consistency models vary by provider, so read the documentation instead of assuming. Use features that match your need:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Versioning\u003c/strong> to recover from overwrites and deletes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Lifecycle rules\u003c/strong> to move data to cheaper classes and expire it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Object lock or write-once\u003c/strong> for protection against deletion\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Cross-region replication\u003c/strong> for disaster recovery, remembering it copies mistakes too\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Access policies and encryption\u003c/strong> with least privilege, and no public access by default\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Design keys to spread load and to be listable by prefix (for example tenant and date). Keep a catalogue of metadata elsewhere if you need rich queries.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cost-and-retention\">Cost and retention\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Retention is a cost and a risk: long retention of personal data is a liability. Choose per dataset how long to keep it and write it down. Count restore costs too, since retrieving from deep archive tiers can be slow and charged per request or per gigabyte.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> The nightly backup job reports success every day. A table is dropped by mistake at 14:00. What do you check first, and what might you find?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Whether a restore from that backup has ever been tested and whether point-in-time recovery exists. You may find the backup is a nightly dump that loses 14 hours of data, or that it cannot be restored within the RTO or at all.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Success of the job is not success of recovery.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Backups in the same failure domain. Backups that were never restored. Credentials needed for restore kept only in the system being restored. Retention that deleted the good copy before the corruption was noticed. A restore that takes 3x the RTO.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-restore-nobody-had-timed\">Incident: The restore nobody had timed\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a destructive change, the team restored from backup and found it would take far longer than the business could accept.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"pg_restore -j 8 --dbname=app /backups/app-2026-10-28.dump\necho &quot;RTO target: 60 minutes&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>pg_restore -j 8 --dbname=app /backups/app-2026-10-28.dump\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">... 2h 41m elapsed ...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;RTO target: 60 minutes&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">RTO target: 60 minutes\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is a simulated illustration. The real cases with related lessons are the Atlassian site deletion in 2022 and the UniSuper subscription deletion in 2024. The change that followed in a design like this is a warm standby or snapshots for fast recovery, point-in-time recovery for precision, and a scheduled timed restore.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;How do you design backups for a 5 TB database with RPO 5 minutes and RTO 1 hour?&quot; Continuous log shipping to an independent account, regular snapshots, a standby for fast failover, a monthly timed restore test, and monitoring on backup age and restore duration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating replicas as backups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backups in the same account and control plane\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never timing a restore\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No protection against deletion of the backups\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"start-from-rpo-and-rto","text":"Start from RPO and RTO"},{"id":"kinds-of-copies","text":"Kinds of copies"},{"id":"independence","text":"Independence"},{"id":"test-restores","text":"Test restores"},{"id":"object-storage-by-design","text":"Object storage by design"},{"id":"cost-and-retention","text":"Cost and retention"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-the-restore-nobody-had-timed","text":"Incident: The restore nobody had timed"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Reliability pillar: back up data","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/reliability-pillar/back-up-data.html"},{"t":"Disaster recovery options in the cloud","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/disaster-recovery-workloads-on-aws/disaster-recovery-options-in-the-cloud.html"}],"text":"A backup is a hypothesis that you can recover. It becomes a fact only when you restore it, and the failure that takes your data often takes the backups with it. This lesson covers how to design for recovery: what to copy, where, how long it takes, and how to prove it. What you will learn Set backup frequency and retention from RPO and RTO Apply the 3-2-1 idea and keep copies independent of the failure Test restores and measure real recovery time Use object storage well: durability, versioning, lifecycle and consistency Start from RPO and RTO RPO says how much data you can lose, so how often you must capture changes. RTO says how long you can be down, so how fast a restore must be. A 5 minute RPO needs continuous log shipping or point-in-time recovery, not a nightly dump. A 1 hour RTO for 2 TB needs a restore path that moves 2 TB in well under an hour: at 200 MB per second, 2 TB takes about 2.8 hours, so a nightly full restore alone fails the target and you need replicas, snapshots or a hot standby. Kinds of copies Replicas: protect against node failure, but replicate deletes and corruption instantly. Snapshots: fast and cheap, often in the same account and failure domain. Backups with point-in-time recovery: a base copy plus a log of changes, so you can restore to a moment before a mistake. Archives: long retention for compliance, slow to restore. Replication is not backup. A script that deletes the wrong thing deletes it everywhere within seconds. In April 2022 a script given the wrong kind of identifier deleted 883 customer sites at Atlassian, and full restoration took until 18 April; the case file explains why restoring many individual sites was a long, careful process. Plan restores for the unit you could lose, not only for a whole database. Independence Keep at least one copy that a single event cannot reach: another account, another region and, for important data, another provider. Protect it with separate credentials and write-once or versioned storage so an attacker or a bad script cannot erase it. In May 2024 UniSuper&#39;s cloud subscription was deleted by a provisioning misconfiguration that spanned both of its geographies; backups held with another provider were part of how it recovered over days. Whatever name you give it, the principle is the same: a backup in the same account and control plane as the data shares its fate. Test restores Schedule regular restore tests, automatically if possible, to a clean environment. Measure the real time, check data integrity (row counts, checksums, an application smoke test), and record it against the RTO. Practise the unpleasant cases: restoring to a point in time, restoring a single table, restoring when the primary credentials are unavailable. A backup job that reports success proves only that a file was written. In August 2023 Toyota&#39;s backup system failed along with the main system after a disk space error, so there was no automatic switchover; the case file has the details. Object storage by design Object stores hold large amounts of data cheaply and durably, with high availability and simple APIs. Know their shape: objects are immutable blobs addressed by key, not files you edit in place; listing is slower than lookup; per-prefix request rates can matter; consistency models vary by provider, so read the documentation instead of assuming. Use features that match your need: Versioning to recover from overwrites and deletes Lifecycle rules to move data to cheaper classes and expire it Object lock or write-once for protection against deletion Cross-region replication for disaster recovery, remembering it copies mistakes too Access policies and encryption with least privilege, and no public access by default Design keys to spread load and to be listable by prefix (for example tenant and date). Keep a catalogue of metadata elsewhere if you need rich queries. Cost and retention Retention is a cost and a risk: long retention of personal data is a liability. Choose per datas"},"system-design/failure-domains-cells-and-bulkheads":{"html":"\u003cp>A big shared system fails big. The way to bound the damage is to decide in advance what can fail together and keep the rest apart. A failure domain is the set of things one cause can take down. Your design is mostly a list of failure domains and the walls between them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Name failure domains: process, host, rack, zone, region, provider, change\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Build cells and bulkheads so a fault cannot spread\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Spot shared dependencies that silently join domains\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Size a cell and route users to one\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"failure-domains\">Failure domains\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A process crash, a host, a rack, a power feed, a data centre or availability zone, a region, a cloud provider. There are also non-physical domains: a deploy that goes to everything at once, a shared configuration file, a shared certificate, a shared control plane. The last group causes more large outages than lightning does. Spreading across zones helps against a data centre fault and does nothing against a bad configuration pushed to all zones at once.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cells\">Cells\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>cell\u003c/strong> is a complete, independent copy of the service for a slice of users. Each cell has its own compute, queues and data. A router sends each customer to exactly one cell. If a cell fails, only its share of users is affected: with 10 cells, a cell fault reaches about 10 percent. Cells also give a safe place to roll out: update one cell, watch, then the next. Capacity grows by adding cells rather than making one cell larger, so the biggest thing you operate stays a known, tested size.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Cell design rules: cells do not call each other; the router is thin and highly reliable (it is the shared part, so keep it simple); a customer&#39;s data lives in one cell; keep a hard maximum size and test at it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"bulkheads\">Bulkheads\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Named after ship compartments. Inside one service, separate resources so one noisy part cannot drain the rest: a separate thread or connection pool per dependency, separate queues for critical and bulk work, separate rate limits per tenant. Without bulkheads, one slow dependency uses all threads and the whole service stalls, including paths that never needed it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hidden-shared-dependencies\">Hidden shared dependencies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cells and zones are undone by what they share: one database, one DNS provider, one identity service, one configuration store, one deploy pipeline. Draw the dependency graph and mark each node shared or per-cell. Every shared node is a place where cells fail together. In 2021 Roblox&#39;s Consul cluster was shared by many services and the outage lasted 73 hours; in 2025 Cloudflare Workers KV relied on a third-party storage provider, and many products failed with it. See the case files.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"static-stability\">Static stability\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A cell should keep running when its control plane is gone. Pre-provision capacity, cache configuration locally and avoid needing an API call to keep serving. Recovery should not depend on the thing that is broken.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You run 20 identical stateless instances behind one load balancer and one database. What are your failure domains?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Effectively one. The shared database and the load balancer fail all 20 together, and a bad deploy reaches all of them. Splitting instances alone adds capacity, not isolation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Count what is shared, not how many copies you have.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cells fail when the router fails, when a &quot;global&quot; dependency sneaks in, or when a deploy hits every cell at once. Bulkheads fail when pools are sized so large they still exhaust the host.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-three-zones-one-bad-config\">Incident: Three zones, one bad config\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A service ran in three zones for resilience. A configuration change was applied to all three at once and all three crashed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"for z in a b c; do kubectl --context zone-$z get pods -l app=api | grep -c CrashLoop; done\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> z \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> a b c; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span> kubectl --context zone-\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$z\u003c/span> get pods -l app=api | grep -c CrashLoop; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">done\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">24\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">24\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">24\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is a simulated illustration. Real cases with the same shape include Cloudflare&#39;s November 2025 bot management feature file, which reached the whole proxy fleet, and Microsoft Azure Front Door in October 2025. Spreading across zones did not help because the change was the shared domain. A staged rollout cell by cell would have limited it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Design for 99.99 percent availability.&quot; Start with cells and independent deployment, then ask what is shared and how changes are staged. Zones alone do not reach it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Counting replicas and calling it isolation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cells that call each other\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One global config or control plane everywhere at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No hard limit on cell size\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"failure-domains","text":"Failure domains"},{"id":"cells","text":"Cells"},{"id":"bulkheads","text":"Bulkheads"},{"id":"hidden-shared-dependencies","text":"Hidden shared dependencies"},{"id":"static-stability","text":"Static stability"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-three-zones-one-bad-config","text":"Incident: Three zones, one bad config"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Static stability using Availability Zones","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/static-stability-using-availability-zones/"},{"t":"Bulkhead pattern","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/bulkhead"}],"text":"A big shared system fails big. The way to bound the damage is to decide in advance what can fail together and keep the rest apart. A failure domain is the set of things one cause can take down. Your design is mostly a list of failure domains and the walls between them. What you will learn Name failure domains: process, host, rack, zone, region, provider, change Build cells and bulkheads so a fault cannot spread Spot shared dependencies that silently join domains Size a cell and route users to one Failure domains A process crash, a host, a rack, a power feed, a data centre or availability zone, a region, a cloud provider. There are also non-physical domains: a deploy that goes to everything at once, a shared configuration file, a shared certificate, a shared control plane. The last group causes more large outages than lightning does. Spreading across zones helps against a data centre fault and does nothing against a bad configuration pushed to all zones at once. Cells A cell is a complete, independent copy of the service for a slice of users. Each cell has its own compute, queues and data. A router sends each customer to exactly one cell. If a cell fails, only its share of users is affected: with 10 cells, a cell fault reaches about 10 percent. Cells also give a safe place to roll out: update one cell, watch, then the next. Capacity grows by adding cells rather than making one cell larger, so the biggest thing you operate stays a known, tested size. Cell design rules: cells do not call each other; the router is thin and highly reliable (it is the shared part, so keep it simple); a customer&#39;s data lives in one cell; keep a hard maximum size and test at it. Bulkheads Named after ship compartments. Inside one service, separate resources so one noisy part cannot drain the rest: a separate thread or connection pool per dependency, separate queues for critical and bulk work, separate rate limits per tenant. Without bulkheads, one slow dependency uses all threads and the whole service stalls, including paths that never needed it. Hidden shared dependencies Cells and zones are undone by what they share: one database, one DNS provider, one identity service, one configuration store, one deploy pipeline. Draw the dependency graph and mark each node shared or per-cell. Every shared node is a place where cells fail together. In 2021 Roblox&#39;s Consul cluster was shared by many services and the outage lasted 73 hours; in 2025 Cloudflare Workers KV relied on a third-party storage provider, and many products failed with it. See the case files. Static stability A cell should keep running when its control plane is gone. Pre-provision capacity, cache configuration locally and avoid needing an API call to keep serving. Recovery should not depend on the thing that is broken. Predict first. You run 20 identical stateless instances behind one load balancer and one database. What are your failure domains? Your guess Answer: Effectively one. The shared database and the load balancer fail all 20 together, and a bad deploy reaches all of them. Splitting instances alone adds capacity, not isolation. Count what is shared, not how many copies you have. How it fails Cells fail when the router fails, when a \"global\" dependency sneaks in, or when a deploy hits every cell at once. Bulkheads fail when pools are sized so large they still exhaust the host. Incident: Three zones, one bad config Incident A service ran in three zones for resilience. A configuration change was applied to all three at once and all three crashed. $ for z in a b c; do kubectl --context zone- $z get pods -l app=api | grep -c CrashLoop; done 24 24 24 This is a simulated illustration. Real cases with the same shape include Cloudflare&#39;s November 2025 bot management feature file, which reached the whole proxy fleet, and Microsoft Azure Front Door in October 2025. Spreading across zones did not help because the change was the shared domain. A staged rollout cell by cell would have li"},"system-design/blast-radius-and-shuffle-sharding":{"html":"\u003cp>Blast radius is the share of users or data a single fault can reach. Good designs set a target for it and then show how each fault stays inside it. A poison request, a noisy tenant or a bad deploy should hurt a few, not everyone.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Define blast radius and set a target\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reduce it with staged rollout, cells and sharding\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Explain shuffle sharding and compute its effect\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Handle the poison request and the noisy tenant\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"blast-radius-as-a-number\">Blast radius as a number\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For each kind of fault write the share affected: a crashed host, a bad deploy, a poisoned input, a lost zone, a bad config. If a bad deploy reaches 100 percent, no amount of zone redundancy gives you a four-nines design. Targets guide choices: a rollout in four stages of 1, 5, 25 and 100 percent limits an undetected bad build to 1 percent for the first detection window.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sharding-to-shrink-it\">Sharding to shrink it\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If customers are divided among N shards, one customer&#39;s fault reaches 1/N of customers. But sharding by customer id alone has a flaw: the unlucky customers sharing the shard with a bad one are always the same.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"shuffle-sharding\">Shuffle sharding\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Instead of mapping a customer to one shard, assign each customer a small random subset of workers, for example 2 out of 8. A poison customer takes down only the workers in its subset. Another customer is hurt only if their whole subset overlaps, and even then they still have a surviving worker if the subsets differ.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Example: 8 workers, each customer gets 2. The number of distinct pairs is 28 (8 choose 2). A given other customer shares both workers with the bad customer with probability 1/28, about 3.6 percent, and loses only one worker with some probability but still has one left in most cases. With plain 4 shards of 2 workers, a bad customer would take out one quarter of customers entirely. Larger fleets give much better numbers: with 100 workers and subsets of 5, there are about 75 million combinations. Clients that retry across their subset will route around the damage.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Shuffle sharding needs clients, or a router, that retry on another worker in their subset, and it assumes faults follow the customer (a poison request, a heavy tenant), not the infrastructure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"poison-requests\">Poison requests\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A request that crashes the worker that handles it, and which clients then retry onto other workers, spreads like a fire. Defences: shuffle sharding, crash isolation, request validation at the edge, limits on retries, and a &quot;quarantine&quot; that stops a repeatedly failing request from being retried at all. A new unprotected code path with blank policy fields crashed Google&#39;s Service Control worldwide in June 2025; see the case file.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"noisy-tenants\">Noisy tenants\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>One tenant using too much hurts others unless there are per-tenant limits, quotas and fair queueing. Quotas that are too coarse fail others; quotas wrong in the other direction fail the tenant. Google&#39;s 2020 authentication outage came from an automated quota system that set the quota of a core service to zero because an older system reported its usage as zero. Automated limits need sanity checks and slow, staged application.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rollout-as-blast-radius-control\">Rollout as blast radius control\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Stage by cell, region or percentage; watch a bake time with real health signals; stop automatically on regression. The first stage should be small enough to survive being wrong and large enough to carry real traffic.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> 100 workers, each customer gets a random set of 5. One customer sends a poison request that kills any worker it reaches. How many workers can it take out, and is every other customer affected?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> At most 5 workers. Other customers lose workers only if their subsets overlap, and few overlap fully, so most keep service by retrying on the rest of their subset.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Subset size sets the damage, overlap sets who else is hurt.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Shuffle sharding fails when faults are in shared infrastructure (a bad deploy to all workers) or when clients do not retry across the subset. Staged rollouts fail when the bake time is too short to catch slow failures, or when health checks measure the wrong thing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-one-request-crashed-the-fleet\">Incident: One request crashed the fleet\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A change with blank fields reached binaries that had a new, unprotected feature. A null pointer caused a crash loop everywhere.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud logging read 'severity&gt;=ERROR' --limit 3 --format='value(textPayload)'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud logging \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">read\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;severity&gt;=ERROR&#x27;\u003c/span> --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">limit\u003c/span> 3 --format=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;value(textPayload)&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">panic: nil pointer dereference in policy check\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">panic: nil pointer dereference in policy check\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">panic: nil pointer dereference in policy check\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The console lines are a simulated illustration. In the real 12 June 2025 incident the replicated policy data reached all regions within seconds, and the fleet crash-looped; a feature flag and staged rollout of the binary feature would have bounded it. See the case file for the sourced timeline and Google&#39;s remediation list.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;How do you stop one tenant taking down a shared service?&quot; Per-tenant quotas and fair queueing, shuffle sharding for isolation, and automatic limits on retries from that tenant.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Percentage rollouts with no bake time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Global data replication of configuration within seconds with no staging\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Per-tenant limits missing or applied without sanity checks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming sharding isolates faults that live in shared code\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"blast-radius-as-a-number","text":"Blast radius as a number"},{"id":"sharding-to-shrink-it","text":"Sharding to shrink it"},{"id":"shuffle-sharding","text":"Shuffle sharding"},{"id":"poison-requests","text":"Poison requests"},{"id":"noisy-tenants","text":"Noisy tenants"},{"id":"rollout-as-blast-radius-control","text":"Rollout as blast radius control"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-one-request-crashed-the-fleet","text":"Incident: One request crashed the fleet"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Workload isolation using shuffle sharding","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/workload-isolation-using-shuffle-sharding/"},{"t":"Canarying releases","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/canarying-releases/"}],"text":"Blast radius is the share of users or data a single fault can reach. Good designs set a target for it and then show how each fault stays inside it. A poison request, a noisy tenant or a bad deploy should hurt a few, not everyone. What you will learn Define blast radius and set a target Reduce it with staged rollout, cells and sharding Explain shuffle sharding and compute its effect Handle the poison request and the noisy tenant Blast radius as a number For each kind of fault write the share affected: a crashed host, a bad deploy, a poisoned input, a lost zone, a bad config. If a bad deploy reaches 100 percent, no amount of zone redundancy gives you a four-nines design. Targets guide choices: a rollout in four stages of 1, 5, 25 and 100 percent limits an undetected bad build to 1 percent for the first detection window. Sharding to shrink it If customers are divided among N shards, one customer&#39;s fault reaches 1/N of customers. But sharding by customer id alone has a flaw: the unlucky customers sharing the shard with a bad one are always the same. Shuffle sharding Instead of mapping a customer to one shard, assign each customer a small random subset of workers, for example 2 out of 8. A poison customer takes down only the workers in its subset. Another customer is hurt only if their whole subset overlaps, and even then they still have a surviving worker if the subsets differ. Example: 8 workers, each customer gets 2. The number of distinct pairs is 28 (8 choose 2). A given other customer shares both workers with the bad customer with probability 1/28, about 3.6 percent, and loses only one worker with some probability but still has one left in most cases. With plain 4 shards of 2 workers, a bad customer would take out one quarter of customers entirely. Larger fleets give much better numbers: with 100 workers and subsets of 5, there are about 75 million combinations. Clients that retry across their subset will route around the damage. Shuffle sharding needs clients, or a router, that retry on another worker in their subset, and it assumes faults follow the customer (a poison request, a heavy tenant), not the infrastructure. Poison requests A request that crashes the worker that handles it, and which clients then retry onto other workers, spreads like a fire. Defences: shuffle sharding, crash isolation, request validation at the edge, limits on retries, and a \"quarantine\" that stops a repeatedly failing request from being retried at all. A new unprotected code path with blank policy fields crashed Google&#39;s Service Control worldwide in June 2025; see the case file. Noisy tenants One tenant using too much hurts others unless there are per-tenant limits, quotas and fair queueing. Quotas that are too coarse fail others; quotas wrong in the other direction fail the tenant. Google&#39;s 2020 authentication outage came from an automated quota system that set the quota of a core service to zero because an older system reported its usage as zero. Automated limits need sanity checks and slow, staged application. Rollout as blast radius control Stage by cell, region or percentage; watch a bake time with real health signals; stop automatically on regression. The first stage should be small enough to survive being wrong and large enough to carry real traffic. Predict first. 100 workers, each customer gets a random set of 5. One customer sends a poison request that kills any worker it reaches. How many workers can it take out, and is every other customer affected? Your guess Answer: At most 5 workers. Other customers lose workers only if their subsets overlap, and few overlap fully, so most keep service by retrying on the rest of their subset. Subset size sets the damage, overlap sets who else is hurt. How it fails Shuffle sharding fails when faults are in shared infrastructure (a bad deploy to all workers) or when clients do not retry across the subset. Staged rollouts fail when the bake time is too short to catch slow failures, or when"},"system-design/graceful-degradation-and-circuit-breakers":{"html":"\u003cp>Every dependency eventually fails. A resilient service decides in advance what it will still do without it. Graceful degradation means serving a reduced but useful result. Circuit breakers stop a service from wasting its own capacity on a dependency that is not answering.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Rank features as critical, important or optional\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Design fallbacks: cached, default, partial or queued\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Configure a circuit breaker and understand its states\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Avoid fallbacks that fail worse than the failure\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rank-what-matters\">Rank what matters\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For a shop: checkout and login are critical; search and the product page are important; recommendations, reviews and the loyalty banner are optional. If recommendations are down, show the page without them. This only works if the page is built so a missing part does not break the whole page, and if you decide it before the incident.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fallback-options\">Fallback options\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Serve stale:\u003c/strong> return a cached copy, marked as possibly old.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Default value:\u003c/strong> a generic list instead of a personal one.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Partial result:\u003c/strong> the answer from the shards that responded, with a flag.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Queue for later:\u003c/strong> accept the write and process it when the dependency is back.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Feature off:\u003c/strong> hide the feature and say so.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>A fallback is code that runs rarely, so it is likely to be wrong. Test it routinely by switching dependencies off in a staging environment and, when safe, in production during a game day.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"circuit-breakers\">Circuit breakers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A breaker wraps calls to a dependency and has three states. \u003cstrong>Closed:\u003c/strong> calls pass; failures are counted. \u003cstrong>Open:\u003c/strong> after too many failures or slow calls, calls fail immediately without touching the dependency, for a cool-down time. \u003cstrong>Half-open:\u003c/strong> after the cool-down, a few trial calls go through; success closes the breaker, failure reopens it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>This protects both sides: the caller stops tying up threads waiting on timeouts, and the dependency gets quiet time to recover. Combine with a timeout (so slowness counts as failure), a bulkhead pool for that dependency, and a fallback for the open state.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Tune on rates, not single failures: for example open when more than 50 percent of the last 20 calls failed or took longer than 2 seconds. Too sensitive and it opens on noise; too sluggish and it never helps.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fail-open-or-closed\">Fail open or closed\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When a safety check cannot be reached, do you allow or deny? A spam filter may fail open (allow mail). A payment fraud check or an authorisation check usually fails closed. Decide per check, write it down, and test it. Cloudflare&#39;s 2025 outage came from a component that crashed on an oversized feature file instead of falling back to the previous good file; a safe fallback would have been to keep the last valid configuration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"shedding-optional-work\">Shedding optional work\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Under load, drop optional work first: analytics events, background refresh, expensive personalisation. Give each request a priority so overload hits the least important first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recovery\">Recovery\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Do not fail back all at once. When a dependency returns, ramp traffic, keep the breaker half-open for a while, and avoid synchronized retries.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A recommendations service is timing out at 5 seconds. The product page calls it synchronously. What happens to the page, and what should change?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Every page view waits 5 seconds and holds a thread, so the page slows or fails. Use a short timeout, a circuit breaker, and fall back to a default list or hide the section.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The optional part must not own the critical path.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Fallbacks that call the same failing dependency. Breakers that open for everyone because the failures were from one tenant. A degraded mode nobody has tested. Health checks that mark everything unhealthy at once and remove all capacity.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-health-check-that-removed-everything\">Incident: A health check that removed everything\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A dependency failed. The health check included it, so every instance reported unhealthy and the load balancer sent traffic to none.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\\n' http://api-1:8080/healthz\ncurl -s http://api-1:8080/healthz\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s -o /dev/null -w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;%{http_code}\\n&#x27;\u003c/span> http://api-1:8080/healthz\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">503\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s http://api-1:8080/healthz\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;db&quot;:&quot;ok&quot;,&quot;recommendations&quot;:&quot;timeout&quot;,&quot;status&quot;:&quot;unhealthy&quot;}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is a simulated illustration of a common pattern. A health check should answer whether this instance can serve its core job, not whether every optional dependency is fine. After the change, readiness checked only the database and the page degraded when recommendations were down.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Your service depends on five others. One is down. What do you do?&quot; Check which feature depends on it, apply the planned fallback, confirm breakers are open to protect threads, and keep health checks about the instance itself.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Putting optional dependencies in the readiness check\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A fallback that calls the failing service\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Breakers with no half-open recovery\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never rehearsing the degraded mode\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"rank-what-matters","text":"Rank what matters"},{"id":"fallback-options","text":"Fallback options"},{"id":"circuit-breakers","text":"Circuit breakers"},{"id":"fail-open-or-closed","text":"Fail open or closed"},{"id":"shedding-optional-work","text":"Shedding optional work"},{"id":"recovery","text":"Recovery"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-a-health-check-that-removed-everything","text":"Incident: A health check that removed everything"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Circuit breaker pattern","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/circuit-breaker"},{"t":"Avoiding fallback in distributed systems","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/avoiding-fallback-in-distributed-systems/"}],"text":"Every dependency eventually fails. A resilient service decides in advance what it will still do without it. Graceful degradation means serving a reduced but useful result. Circuit breakers stop a service from wasting its own capacity on a dependency that is not answering. What you will learn Rank features as critical, important or optional Design fallbacks: cached, default, partial or queued Configure a circuit breaker and understand its states Avoid fallbacks that fail worse than the failure Rank what matters For a shop: checkout and login are critical; search and the product page are important; recommendations, reviews and the loyalty banner are optional. If recommendations are down, show the page without them. This only works if the page is built so a missing part does not break the whole page, and if you decide it before the incident. Fallback options Serve stale: return a cached copy, marked as possibly old. Default value: a generic list instead of a personal one. Partial result: the answer from the shards that responded, with a flag. Queue for later: accept the write and process it when the dependency is back. Feature off: hide the feature and say so. A fallback is code that runs rarely, so it is likely to be wrong. Test it routinely by switching dependencies off in a staging environment and, when safe, in production during a game day. Circuit breakers A breaker wraps calls to a dependency and has three states. Closed: calls pass; failures are counted. Open: after too many failures or slow calls, calls fail immediately without touching the dependency, for a cool-down time. Half-open: after the cool-down, a few trial calls go through; success closes the breaker, failure reopens it. This protects both sides: the caller stops tying up threads waiting on timeouts, and the dependency gets quiet time to recover. Combine with a timeout (so slowness counts as failure), a bulkhead pool for that dependency, and a fallback for the open state. Tune on rates, not single failures: for example open when more than 50 percent of the last 20 calls failed or took longer than 2 seconds. Too sensitive and it opens on noise; too sluggish and it never helps. Fail open or closed When a safety check cannot be reached, do you allow or deny? A spam filter may fail open (allow mail). A payment fraud check or an authorisation check usually fails closed. Decide per check, write it down, and test it. Cloudflare&#39;s 2025 outage came from a component that crashed on an oversized feature file instead of falling back to the previous good file; a safe fallback would have been to keep the last valid configuration. Shedding optional work Under load, drop optional work first: analytics events, background refresh, expensive personalisation. Give each request a priority so overload hits the least important first. Recovery Do not fail back all at once. When a dependency returns, ramp traffic, keep the breaker half-open for a while, and avoid synchronized retries. Predict first. A recommendations service is timing out at 5 seconds. The product page calls it synchronously. What happens to the page, and what should change? Your guess Answer: Every page view waits 5 seconds and holds a thread, so the page slows or fails. Use a short timeout, a circuit breaker, and fall back to a default list or hide the section. The optional part must not own the critical path. How it fails Fallbacks that call the same failing dependency. Breakers that open for everyone because the failures were from one tenant. A degraded mode nobody has tested. Health checks that mark everything unhealthy at once and remove all capacity. Incident: A health check that removed everything Incident A dependency failed. The health check included it, so every instance reported unhealthy and the load balancer sent traffic to none. $ curl -s -o /dev/null -w &#x27;%{http_code}\\n&#x27; http://api-1:8080/healthz 503 $ curl -s http://api-1:8080/healthz {\"db\":\"ok\",\"recommendations\":\"timeout\",\"status\":\"unheal"},"system-design/rate-limiting-and-load-shedding":{"html":"\u003cp>Overload is not rare. It comes from launches, retry storms, a single heavy customer, a slow dependency and recovery itself. A service that accepts everything delivers nothing: queues grow, latency exceeds timeouts and every request fails. The aim is to serve fewer requests well.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choose a rate limit algorithm and where to enforce it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Share capacity fairly between tenants\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Shed load by priority and use admission control\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Return signals that tell clients to back off\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-overload-collapses\">Why overload collapses\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When arrival exceeds capacity, queues grow. Wait time passes the client&#39;s timeout, so the work is done for a client who has already given up, and the client retries, adding load. Goodput falls toward zero even though servers are busy. This is congestion collapse. Limiting early, before expensive work, keeps goodput high.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"algorithms\">Algorithms\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Fixed window:\u003c/strong> count per minute; simple, but allows a burst of double at the window edge.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Sliding window:\u003c/strong> smoother, more memory.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Token bucket:\u003c/strong> tokens refill at a steady rate up to a burst size; each request spends one. Allows controlled bursts and is the common default.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Leaky bucket:\u003c/strong> requests drain at a constant rate, smoothing output.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Concurrency limit:\u003c/strong> cap in-flight requests rather than rate. Often better for protecting a service, because slow requests hold capacity longer.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-to-enforce\">Where to enforce\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>At the edge or gateway (cheap, early), per service (protects itself), and per dependency (protects the callee). A distributed limiter needs shared state, which costs latency and can fail, so decide what happens if the limiter store is down: usually fail open with a local limit as a floor. Docker Hub&#39;s pull limits are a public example of limits applied by source address, which surprise teams whose CI jobs share one IP; read the current documentation for the exact numbers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fairness\">Fairness\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Per-tenant limits stop one customer taking everything. Weighted fair queueing gives each tenant a share and lets idle share be used by others. Pick the key carefully: per IP punishes shared networks, per API key is cleaner.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"load-shedding\">Load shedding\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When over capacity, reject deliberately: drop the lowest priority first (batch, prefetch, analytics), and reject new work rather than abandon work half done. Reject quickly and cheaply; a rejection that costs as much as the work defeats the point. Prefer shedding by queue age: drop requests that have waited longer than the client will wait.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"admission-control-and-clients\">Admission control and clients\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Limit concurrency at the entrance based on measured latency, so the service accepts only what it can finish in time. Tell clients: return HTTP 429 (too many requests) or 503 with a Retry-After header, and have clients back off with jitter. A client that ignores the signal needs a hard limit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"capacity-and-autoscaling\">Capacity and autoscaling\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Autoscaling is slow compared with a spike, and new instances start cold (empty caches, no connections). Do not use it as the only protection. Keep headroom, scale on leading signals, and let limits hold the line while scale-out catches up.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A service handles 1,000 requests a second. Traffic jumps to 3,000 and requests start taking longer than the 2 second client timeout. Why does adding retries make it worse, and what do you do?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Retries add more load while the service is already doing work for clients that gave up. Shed excess load early with a concurrency limit, return 429 or 503 with Retry-After, and make clients back off with jitter.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Serving fewer requests well beats serving all of them badly.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A limiter that rejects good traffic because the key is wrong. A limiter store outage that blocks everything. Shedding that rejects critical requests first. A limit set once and never revisited as capacity changed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-quota-that-went-to-zero\">Incident: A quota that went to zero\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An automated quota system reduced a core service&#39;s quota to zero because the old system reported its usage as zero.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gcloud service-usage quota describe --service=identity --limit=requests\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>gcloud service-usage quota describe --service=identity --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">limit\u003c/span>=requests\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">limit: 0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">used: 0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">status: LIMIT_REACHED\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The console output is a simulated illustration. In the real 14 December 2020 outage at Google, authentication failed for almost every Google service for about 50 minutes. The lesson is that limits are themselves a dependency that can fail: automated limits need sanity checks, staged change and a floor below which they cannot drop. See the case file.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Design a rate limiter for an API gateway.&quot; Ask for the key and the limits, choose a token bucket, keep counters in a fast shared store with local fallback, return 429 with Retry-After, and say what happens when the store is down.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Limits only at the edge, none per dependency\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Counting requests when concurrency is the real constraint\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No Retry-After, so clients retry immediately\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Autoscaling as the only defence\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"why-overload-collapses","text":"Why overload collapses"},{"id":"algorithms","text":"Algorithms"},{"id":"where-to-enforce","text":"Where to enforce"},{"id":"fairness","text":"Fairness"},{"id":"load-shedding","text":"Load shedding"},{"id":"admission-control-and-clients","text":"Admission control and clients"},{"id":"capacity-and-autoscaling","text":"Capacity and autoscaling"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-a-quota-that-went-to-zero","text":"Incident: A quota that went to zero"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Using load shedding to avoid overload","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/using-load-shedding-to-avoid-overload/"},{"t":"Handling overload","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/handling-overload/"}],"text":"Overload is not rare. It comes from launches, retry storms, a single heavy customer, a slow dependency and recovery itself. A service that accepts everything delivers nothing: queues grow, latency exceeds timeouts and every request fails. The aim is to serve fewer requests well. What you will learn Choose a rate limit algorithm and where to enforce it Share capacity fairly between tenants Shed load by priority and use admission control Return signals that tell clients to back off Why overload collapses When arrival exceeds capacity, queues grow. Wait time passes the client&#39;s timeout, so the work is done for a client who has already given up, and the client retries, adding load. Goodput falls toward zero even though servers are busy. This is congestion collapse. Limiting early, before expensive work, keeps goodput high. Algorithms Fixed window: count per minute; simple, but allows a burst of double at the window edge. Sliding window: smoother, more memory. Token bucket: tokens refill at a steady rate up to a burst size; each request spends one. Allows controlled bursts and is the common default. Leaky bucket: requests drain at a constant rate, smoothing output. Concurrency limit: cap in-flight requests rather than rate. Often better for protecting a service, because slow requests hold capacity longer. Where to enforce At the edge or gateway (cheap, early), per service (protects itself), and per dependency (protects the callee). A distributed limiter needs shared state, which costs latency and can fail, so decide what happens if the limiter store is down: usually fail open with a local limit as a floor. Docker Hub&#39;s pull limits are a public example of limits applied by source address, which surprise teams whose CI jobs share one IP; read the current documentation for the exact numbers. Fairness Per-tenant limits stop one customer taking everything. Weighted fair queueing gives each tenant a share and lets idle share be used by others. Pick the key carefully: per IP punishes shared networks, per API key is cleaner. Load shedding When over capacity, reject deliberately: drop the lowest priority first (batch, prefetch, analytics), and reject new work rather than abandon work half done. Reject quickly and cheaply; a rejection that costs as much as the work defeats the point. Prefer shedding by queue age: drop requests that have waited longer than the client will wait. Admission control and clients Limit concurrency at the entrance based on measured latency, so the service accepts only what it can finish in time. Tell clients: return HTTP 429 (too many requests) or 503 with a Retry-After header, and have clients back off with jitter. A client that ignores the signal needs a hard limit. Capacity and autoscaling Autoscaling is slow compared with a spike, and new instances start cold (empty caches, no connections). Do not use it as the only protection. Keep headroom, scale on leading signals, and let limits hold the line while scale-out catches up. Predict first. A service handles 1,000 requests a second. Traffic jumps to 3,000 and requests start taking longer than the 2 second client timeout. Why does adding retries make it worse, and what do you do? Your guess Answer: Retries add more load while the service is already doing work for clients that gave up. Shed excess load early with a concurrency limit, return 429 or 503 with Retry-After, and make clients back off with jitter. Serving fewer requests well beats serving all of them badly. How it fails A limiter that rejects good traffic because the key is wrong. A limiter store outage that blocks everything. Shedding that rejects critical requests first. A limit set once and never revisited as capacity changed. Incident: A quota that went to zero Incident An automated quota system reduced a core service&#39;s quota to zero because the old system reported its usage as zero. $ gcloud service-usage quota describe --service=identity -- limit =requests limit: 0 used: 0 status: LIMIT_R"},"system-design/multi-region-and-global-traffic":{"html":"\u003cp>A second region protects against a region failing. It also doubles the things that can go wrong: data in two places, traffic steering, and a failover path that is rarely used. Multi-region is a trade, and it only helps if you can show it works.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Define RTO and RPO and map them to a pattern\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Compare backup and restore, pilot light, warm standby and active-active\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Handle data across regions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Steer traffic with DNS, anycast and health checks\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rto-and-rpo\">RTO and RPO\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>RTO\u003c/strong> (recovery time objective) is how long you can be down. \u003cstrong>RPO\u003c/strong> (recovery point objective) is how much data you can lose, measured in time. A target of RTO 1 hour and RPO 5 minutes rules out nightly backups alone. State both, per service, with the business owner.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"patterns\">Patterns\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Backup and restore:\u003c/strong> hours of RTO, data loss up to the last backup. Cheapest.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Pilot light:\u003c/strong> core data replicated, compute off until needed. Minutes to hours.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Warm standby:\u003c/strong> a scaled-down copy running, scaled up on failover. Minutes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Active-active:\u003c/strong> both regions serve traffic. Seconds, highest cost and complexity.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Active-passive is simpler, but the standby is untested by real traffic, so failover often fails when first used. Active-active tests both paths all the time, but writes need an answer: one write region per customer (sticky home region), a store with multi-region writes and a conflict rule, or accept stale reads in the second region. Latency across regions (100 to 200 milliseconds between continents) rules out synchronous writes on user paths.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"data-is-the-hard-part\">Data is the hard part\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Asynchronous replication risks losing the last seconds on failover (RPO above zero). Synchronous across regions costs latency on each write. Pick per dataset. After a failover, decide how to fail back, and how to reconcile writes accepted during the split. Practise it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"steering-traffic\">Steering traffic\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>DNS (GeoDNS, weighted, health-checked):\u003c/strong> simple; limited by TTL and caches that ignore it, so failover takes minutes. Keep records low in TTL if you depend on it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Anycast:\u003c/strong> many locations announce the same IP; routing sends users to a nearby one; a failed site withdraws its route. Fast, but it ties you to routing and can send a user&#39;s packets to different sites mid-connection.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Global load balancers:\u003c/strong> health-check regions and shift traffic.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Make sure the steering layer is not the single point of failure. The Facebook outage on 4 October 2021 removed routes to its DNS servers, so the name stopped resolving anywhere. Dyn&#39;s DNS attack in 2016 took down many sites that used one DNS provider. Using two independent DNS providers is a standard mitigation. Akamai&#39;s edge DNS bug in 2021 shows a vendor change can fail globally in minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"region-wide-dependencies\">Region-wide dependencies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Some services are effectively global in one place. A region can be &quot;up&quot; while the identity or control-plane API it needs runs elsewhere. The October 2025 AWS DynamoDB DNS fault in us-east-1 left many AWS services failing for about 15 hours, which shows how much can hang on one regional endpoint. Map which calls leave your region and test with that region blocked.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prove-it\">Prove it\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run failover drills on a schedule. Verify capacity: the surviving region must take 100 percent of load (or the load you promise), not 50. Track the time from decision to full traffic and compare it with the RTO.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Two regions each run at 60 percent of capacity and you call it active-active for high availability. One region fails. What happens?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The survivor needs 120 percent of its capacity, so it overloads. Active-active at N regions needs headroom so the survivors can absorb the lost share, or load shedding to protect the core.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Availability is capacity plus design.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A standby that was never exercised. A runbook that depends on the region that failed. DNS caches pinning users to a dead region. Replication lag larger than the RPO. Credentials or configs present only in the primary.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-name-that-stopped-resolving\">Incident: The name that stopped resolving\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A change on the internal backbone withdrew the routes to the company&#39;s DNS servers. The domain stopped resolving and internal tools that used the same network were also unreachable.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"dig +short example-social.com NS\ntraceroute -n 203.0.113.53\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dig +short example-social.com NS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">;; connection timed out; no servers could be reached\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>traceroute -n 203.0.113.53\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 1  10.0.0.1  0.4 ms\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 2  *\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The console output is a simulated illustration. In the real 4 October 2021 outage the services were down about six hours; the case file uses Cloudflare&#39;s outside view as its source. The design lesson: keep the means to fix a failure, such as out-of-band access and status communication, off the system that is failing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Design a service for 99.99 percent across regions.&quot; Cells in each region, active-active with a home region per customer, health-checked traffic steering with two DNS providers, staged deploys per region, tested failover with survivor capacity, and a clear RPO per dataset.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No capacity in the surviving region\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One DNS provider\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A failover path used only in drills\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A recovery plan hosted on the failing system\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"rto-and-rpo","text":"RTO and RPO"},{"id":"patterns","text":"Patterns"},{"id":"data-is-the-hard-part","text":"Data is the hard part"},{"id":"steering-traffic","text":"Steering traffic"},{"id":"region-wide-dependencies","text":"Region-wide dependencies"},{"id":"prove-it","text":"Prove it"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-the-name-that-stopped-resolving","text":"Incident: The name that stopped resolving"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Disaster recovery options in the cloud","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/disaster-recovery-workloads-on-aws/disaster-recovery-options-in-the-cloud.html"},{"t":"Geode pattern","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/geode"}],"text":"A second region protects against a region failing. It also doubles the things that can go wrong: data in two places, traffic steering, and a failover path that is rarely used. Multi-region is a trade, and it only helps if you can show it works. What you will learn Define RTO and RPO and map them to a pattern Compare backup and restore, pilot light, warm standby and active-active Handle data across regions Steer traffic with DNS, anycast and health checks RTO and RPO RTO (recovery time objective) is how long you can be down. RPO (recovery point objective) is how much data you can lose, measured in time. A target of RTO 1 hour and RPO 5 minutes rules out nightly backups alone. State both, per service, with the business owner. Patterns Backup and restore: hours of RTO, data loss up to the last backup. Cheapest. Pilot light: core data replicated, compute off until needed. Minutes to hours. Warm standby: a scaled-down copy running, scaled up on failover. Minutes. Active-active: both regions serve traffic. Seconds, highest cost and complexity. Active-passive is simpler, but the standby is untested by real traffic, so failover often fails when first used. Active-active tests both paths all the time, but writes need an answer: one write region per customer (sticky home region), a store with multi-region writes and a conflict rule, or accept stale reads in the second region. Latency across regions (100 to 200 milliseconds between continents) rules out synchronous writes on user paths. Data is the hard part Asynchronous replication risks losing the last seconds on failover (RPO above zero). Synchronous across regions costs latency on each write. Pick per dataset. After a failover, decide how to fail back, and how to reconcile writes accepted during the split. Practise it. Steering traffic DNS (GeoDNS, weighted, health-checked): simple; limited by TTL and caches that ignore it, so failover takes minutes. Keep records low in TTL if you depend on it. Anycast: many locations announce the same IP; routing sends users to a nearby one; a failed site withdraws its route. Fast, but it ties you to routing and can send a user&#39;s packets to different sites mid-connection. Global load balancers: health-check regions and shift traffic. Make sure the steering layer is not the single point of failure. The Facebook outage on 4 October 2021 removed routes to its DNS servers, so the name stopped resolving anywhere. Dyn&#39;s DNS attack in 2016 took down many sites that used one DNS provider. Using two independent DNS providers is a standard mitigation. Akamai&#39;s edge DNS bug in 2021 shows a vendor change can fail globally in minutes. Region-wide dependencies Some services are effectively global in one place. A region can be \"up\" while the identity or control-plane API it needs runs elsewhere. The October 2025 AWS DynamoDB DNS fault in us-east-1 left many AWS services failing for about 15 hours, which shows how much can hang on one regional endpoint. Map which calls leave your region and test with that region blocked. Prove it Run failover drills on a schedule. Verify capacity: the surviving region must take 100 percent of load (or the load you promise), not 50. Track the time from decision to full traffic and compare it with the RTO. Predict first. Two regions each run at 60 percent of capacity and you call it active-active for high availability. One region fails. What happens? Your guess Answer: The survivor needs 120 percent of its capacity, so it overloads. Active-active at N regions needs headroom so the survivors can absorb the lost share, or load shedding to protect the core. Availability is capacity plus design. How it fails A standby that was never exercised. A runbook that depends on the region that failed. DNS caches pinning users to a dead region. Replication lag larger than the RPO. Credentials or configs present only in the primary. Incident: The name that stopped resolving Incident A change on the internal backbone withdrew the routes "},"system-design/config-and-control-plane-change-safety":{"html":"\u003cp>Many of the largest recent outages were not hardware failures or attacks. They were a change: a configuration file, a routing update, a policy, a feature list. Changes to data are often pushed faster and wider than code, with fewer checks. Design the change path as carefully as the serving path.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Explain why configuration is code with a bigger blast radius\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Validate, stage and canary configuration changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep a last known good copy and a fast rollback\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Separate the control plane from the data plane\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"config-is-code\">Config is code\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A configuration value can crash a binary, redirect traffic or disable security as surely as a code change. Yet it is often edited by hand, pushed everywhere at once and reviewed less. Give config the same path as code: version control, review, tests, staged rollout, rollback.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"validate-before-you-push\">Validate before you push\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Schema and bounds:\u003c/strong> types, ranges, size limits. A file that doubles in size should be rejected before it reaches the proxy.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Semantic checks:\u003c/strong> does it parse in the real consumer, in a test harness that loads it like production?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Diff size:\u003c/strong> a change that rewrites half the file is suspicious; require extra review or approval.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Compatibility:\u003c/strong> old binaries must tolerate new data and new binaries old data; a new field read by a new feature must be safe when blank.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stage-the-rollout\">Stage the rollout\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Apply to a small cell or percentage, wait a bake time, check real health signals (error rate, latency, crash count), then proceed. Make the rollout automatic and stoppable. Avoid &quot;global replication in seconds&quot;: that is an excellent way to deliver a bad value to everyone before a person can react.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"last-known-good-and-fail-static\">Last known good and fail static\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When a consumer loads a bad config, it should keep running with the previous valid one and raise an alert, not crash. Fail static: keep serving with the last good data. Pair with a fast rollback that does not depend on the broken system.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"control-plane-versus-data-plane\">Control plane versus data plane\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>data plane\u003c/strong> handles user traffic; the \u003cstrong>control plane\u003c/strong> changes configuration and scale. A good design lets the data plane keep working if the control plane is down or wrong. On 2 November 2023 Cloudflare&#39;s control plane and analytics primary site lost power; customers could not change settings, and logs were lost for about two days, yet traffic mostly flowed. The reverse failure is worse: a control plane that pushes a bad value to the data plane everywhere.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"real-cases\">Real cases\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Cloudflare, 18 November 2025: a database permissions change made a query return duplicate rows; the bot-management feature file doubled in size, passed a hard limit in the proxy and crashed it on every machine that loaded it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Microsoft Azure Front Door, 29 October 2025: incompatible metadata passed health checks, rolled out globally and triggered a latent bug in the data plane; recovery took about 4.5 hours.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Google Service Control, 12 June 2025: a policy with blank fields met an unprotected new feature.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Akamai edge DNS, 22 July 2021: a configuration update triggered a component bug; rolled back after about an hour.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Facebook, 4 October 2021: a backbone configuration change withdrew routes.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Read each case file for the sourced details.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"safe-change-checklist\">Safe-change checklist\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Can this change be previewed against production traffic or data?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What is the smallest first stage and the stop signal?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What is the previous good state and how long does rollback take?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What happens if the consumer receives garbage?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is the person fixing it dependent on the thing that is broken?\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A feature file generated by a query is pushed to every proxy every 5 minutes. A change makes the query return twice as many rows. What protections would have limited the damage?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> A size and shape check before publishing, staged rollout with a bake time, consumers that keep the last good file when a new one is invalid, and a kill switch. Any one of these would have limited the blast.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The generator and the consumer both need guards.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Validation exists but tests a different parser. Staging exists but the pipeline has a &quot;fast path&quot; used in emergencies. Rollback needs the control plane that is down. The change tool and the monitoring share the same dependency.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-file-that-doubled\">Incident: A file that doubled\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A generated configuration file doubled in size, exceeded a fixed limit in the proxy and the proxy crashed wherever it loaded it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ls -l /etc/proxy/features.json\njournalctl -u proxy -n 2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> -l /etc/proxy/features.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 2411873 features.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>journalctl -u proxy -n 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">proxy: feature count 400 exceeds limit 200\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">proxy: fatal: aborting\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This console output is a simulated illustration. The real 18 November 2025 Cloudflare incident is in the case file with its sourced timeline and remediation list. A pre-publish size check, a fail-static loader and a global kill switch are the generic design answers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;How would you make config pushes safe?&quot; Version and review, validate with the real parser, canary by cell, automatic halt on error rate, last known good, and a rollback that works when the control plane is down.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Pushing config to all regions at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A fast path that skips staging\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Consumers that crash on bad input\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rollback depending on the failed system\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"config-is-code","text":"Config is code"},{"id":"validate-before-you-push","text":"Validate before you push"},{"id":"stage-the-rollout","text":"Stage the rollout"},{"id":"last-known-good-and-fail-static","text":"Last known good and fail static"},{"id":"control-plane-versus-data-plane","text":"Control plane versus data plane"},{"id":"real-cases","text":"Real cases"},{"id":"safe-change-checklist","text":"Safe-change checklist"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-a-file-that-doubled","text":"Incident: A file that doubled"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Automating safe, hands-off deployments","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/automating-safe-hands-off-deployments/"},{"t":"Cloudflare outage on 18 November 2025","u":"https://blog.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-outage-on-november-18-2025/"}],"text":"Many of the largest recent outages were not hardware failures or attacks. They were a change: a configuration file, a routing update, a policy, a feature list. Changes to data are often pushed faster and wider than code, with fewer checks. Design the change path as carefully as the serving path. What you will learn Explain why configuration is code with a bigger blast radius Validate, stage and canary configuration changes Keep a last known good copy and a fast rollback Separate the control plane from the data plane Config is code A configuration value can crash a binary, redirect traffic or disable security as surely as a code change. Yet it is often edited by hand, pushed everywhere at once and reviewed less. Give config the same path as code: version control, review, tests, staged rollout, rollback. Validate before you push Schema and bounds: types, ranges, size limits. A file that doubles in size should be rejected before it reaches the proxy. Semantic checks: does it parse in the real consumer, in a test harness that loads it like production? Diff size: a change that rewrites half the file is suspicious; require extra review or approval. Compatibility: old binaries must tolerate new data and new binaries old data; a new field read by a new feature must be safe when blank. Stage the rollout Apply to a small cell or percentage, wait a bake time, check real health signals (error rate, latency, crash count), then proceed. Make the rollout automatic and stoppable. Avoid \"global replication in seconds\": that is an excellent way to deliver a bad value to everyone before a person can react. Last known good and fail static When a consumer loads a bad config, it should keep running with the previous valid one and raise an alert, not crash. Fail static: keep serving with the last good data. Pair with a fast rollback that does not depend on the broken system. Control plane versus data plane The data plane handles user traffic; the control plane changes configuration and scale. A good design lets the data plane keep working if the control plane is down or wrong. On 2 November 2023 Cloudflare&#39;s control plane and analytics primary site lost power; customers could not change settings, and logs were lost for about two days, yet traffic mostly flowed. The reverse failure is worse: a control plane that pushes a bad value to the data plane everywhere. Real cases Cloudflare, 18 November 2025: a database permissions change made a query return duplicate rows; the bot-management feature file doubled in size, passed a hard limit in the proxy and crashed it on every machine that loaded it. Microsoft Azure Front Door, 29 October 2025: incompatible metadata passed health checks, rolled out globally and triggered a latent bug in the data plane; recovery took about 4.5 hours. Google Service Control, 12 June 2025: a policy with blank fields met an unprotected new feature. Akamai edge DNS, 22 July 2021: a configuration update triggered a component bug; rolled back after about an hour. Facebook, 4 October 2021: a backbone configuration change withdrew routes. Read each case file for the sourced details. Safe-change checklist Can this change be previewed against production traffic or data? What is the smallest first stage and the stop signal? What is the previous good state and how long does rollback take? What happens if the consumer receives garbage? Is the person fixing it dependent on the thing that is broken? Predict first. A feature file generated by a query is pushed to every proxy every 5 minutes. A change makes the query return twice as many rows. What protections would have limited the damage? Your guess Answer: A size and shape check before publishing, staged rollout with a bake time, consumers that keep the last good file when a new one is invalid, and a kill switch. Any one of these would have limited the blast. The generator and the consumer both need guards. How it fails Validation exists but tests a different parser. Staging exists but"},"system-design/hidden-dependencies-and-single-points":{"html":"\u003cp>A diagram shows the dependencies you remember. Outages come from the ones you forgot: the certificate authority, the DNS provider, the identity service, the status page hosted on the same cloud account, the chat tool you coordinate in. This lesson is a method to find them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Build a dependency map including runtime, build, deploy and recovery paths\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Find single points of failure and hard cycles\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Handle third-party and shared platform dependencies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep your recovery tools independent of the system they recover\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"four-dependency-graphs\">Four dependency graphs\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Serving:\u003c/strong> what a user request touches: DNS, CDN, gateway, services, databases, identity, payments.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Build and deploy:\u003c/strong> source host, registry, CI, secrets, artifact store, deploy tool. Docker Hub pull limits blocked builds and clusters that pulled from shared addresses.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Operate:\u003c/strong> metrics, logs, alerting, paging, on-call chat, runbooks, access (VPN, SSO).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Recover:\u003c/strong> backups and their keys, access to restore, DNS to fail over, the status page.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Teams usually map the first and forget the other three.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-to-find-hidden-ones\">How to find hidden ones\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Trace one request with distributed tracing and list every hostname called.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Read egress logs and DNS logs; each external name is a dependency.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ask for each: if this is down, what do we lose, and for how long? Rank by impact.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Block each dependency in staging, one by one, and watch.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>List who holds the credentials needed to recover, and where they are stored.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cycles\">Cycles\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A cycle is when A needs B to start and B needs A. Examples: a cluster that pulls its images from a registry running on that cluster; a secrets store that needs a secret to unlock; a DNS service whose own name must resolve through itself; monitoring that depends on the cluster it monitors. Cycles turn a small fault into a full outage because recovery requires the broken thing. Roblox&#39;s 2021 outage was lengthened when monitoring depended on the same Consul cluster that was struggling.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"single-points-of-failure\">Single points of failure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>One of anything: one DNS provider, one certificate expiry date, one engineer with the key, one account, one region, one person who knows the process. Each needs either removal (a second independent path), or acceptance written down with a reason. Expired certificates are a common single point: Microsoft Teams in 2020 and O2 and Ericsson in 2018 are public cases; track expiry dates and automate renewal.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"third-parties\">Third parties\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You inherit the failures of vendors. Cloudflare Workers KV on 12 June 2025 failed when a third-party storage provider had its own outage, and many Cloudflare products went with it. For each vendor ask: what is the contract SLA, what is our fallback, and can we operate without it for an hour, a day?\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-recovery-independent\">Keep recovery independent\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Host the status page, the runbooks and the on-call tools outside the platform they describe. Keep break-glass access that works when SSO is down. Keep copies of the critical recovery data in a separate account or provider. Test that you can still reach them with the main system off.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Your status page is hosted on the same cloud account and DNS as your product. What happens during a full outage?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Customers cannot reach the status page either, so you cannot communicate. Host it on independent infrastructure and DNS.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The thing you use to explain an outage must survive the outage.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A map that is a one-off drawing and is stale in a month. A dependency noted but never tested. A break-glass account nobody has tried this year.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-monitoring-that-went-down-with-the-cluster\">Incident: Monitoring that went down with the cluster\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The monitoring system ran on the platform it watched. When the platform degraded, engineers lost their view of it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s -m 5 https://metrics.internal/api/health\nkubectl get nodes\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s -m 5 https://metrics.internal/api/health\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">curl: (28) Operation timed out after 5001 milliseconds\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Unable to connect to the server: net/http: TLS handshake timeout\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is a simulated illustration. The Roblox 73-hour case file describes how monitoring that depended on the same cluster made diagnosis harder. The design response is an independent, simple monitoring path for the core platform and an out-of-band way to reach it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Walk me through hidden dependencies in a service you ran.&quot; List one in each of the four graphs, the test you ran to find it and what you changed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Mapping only the serving path\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Status page and runbooks on the failing system\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Untracked certificate expiry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Vendors with no fallback plan\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"four-dependency-graphs","text":"Four dependency graphs"},{"id":"how-to-find-hidden-ones","text":"How to find hidden ones"},{"id":"cycles","text":"Cycles"},{"id":"single-points-of-failure","text":"Single points of failure"},{"id":"third-parties","text":"Third parties"},{"id":"keep-recovery-independent","text":"Keep recovery independent"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-monitoring-that-went-down-with-the-cluster","text":"Incident: Monitoring that went down with the cluster"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Addressing cascading failures","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/addressing-cascading-failures/"},{"t":"Avoiding fallback in distributed systems","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/avoiding-fallback-in-distributed-systems/"}],"text":"A diagram shows the dependencies you remember. Outages come from the ones you forgot: the certificate authority, the DNS provider, the identity service, the status page hosted on the same cloud account, the chat tool you coordinate in. This lesson is a method to find them. What you will learn Build a dependency map including runtime, build, deploy and recovery paths Find single points of failure and hard cycles Handle third-party and shared platform dependencies Keep your recovery tools independent of the system they recover Four dependency graphs Serving: what a user request touches: DNS, CDN, gateway, services, databases, identity, payments. Build and deploy: source host, registry, CI, secrets, artifact store, deploy tool. Docker Hub pull limits blocked builds and clusters that pulled from shared addresses. Operate: metrics, logs, alerting, paging, on-call chat, runbooks, access (VPN, SSO). Recover: backups and their keys, access to restore, DNS to fail over, the status page. Teams usually map the first and forget the other three. How to find hidden ones Trace one request with distributed tracing and list every hostname called. Read egress logs and DNS logs; each external name is a dependency. Ask for each: if this is down, what do we lose, and for how long? Rank by impact. Block each dependency in staging, one by one, and watch. List who holds the credentials needed to recover, and where they are stored. Cycles A cycle is when A needs B to start and B needs A. Examples: a cluster that pulls its images from a registry running on that cluster; a secrets store that needs a secret to unlock; a DNS service whose own name must resolve through itself; monitoring that depends on the cluster it monitors. Cycles turn a small fault into a full outage because recovery requires the broken thing. Roblox&#39;s 2021 outage was lengthened when monitoring depended on the same Consul cluster that was struggling. Single points of failure One of anything: one DNS provider, one certificate expiry date, one engineer with the key, one account, one region, one person who knows the process. Each needs either removal (a second independent path), or acceptance written down with a reason. Expired certificates are a common single point: Microsoft Teams in 2020 and O2 and Ericsson in 2018 are public cases; track expiry dates and automate renewal. Third parties You inherit the failures of vendors. Cloudflare Workers KV on 12 June 2025 failed when a third-party storage provider had its own outage, and many Cloudflare products went with it. For each vendor ask: what is the contract SLA, what is our fallback, and can we operate without it for an hour, a day? Keep recovery independent Host the status page, the runbooks and the on-call tools outside the platform they describe. Keep break-glass access that works when SSO is down. Keep copies of the critical recovery data in a separate account or provider. Test that you can still reach them with the main system off. Predict first. Your status page is hosted on the same cloud account and DNS as your product. What happens during a full outage? Your guess Answer: Customers cannot reach the status page either, so you cannot communicate. Host it on independent infrastructure and DNS. The thing you use to explain an outage must survive the outage. How it fails A map that is a one-off drawing and is stale in a month. A dependency noted but never tested. A break-glass account nobody has tried this year. Incident: Monitoring that went down with the cluster Incident The monitoring system ran on the platform it watched. When the platform degraded, engineers lost their view of it. $ curl -s -m 5 https://metrics.internal/api/health curl: (28) Operation timed out after 5001 milliseconds $ kubectl get nodes Unable to connect to the server: net/http: TLS handshake timeout This is a simulated illustration. The Roblox 73-hour case file describes how monitoring that depended on the same cluster made diagnosis harder. The design re"},"system-design/observability-by-design":{"html":"\u003cp>Observability is not a tool you add after launch. It is a property you design in: can an on-call engineer, at 3 a.m., tell what is wrong, how many users are affected and where to look, using only what the system emits? The design question is what to emit, where to keep it, and what happens to your view when the system is down.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Put SLIs and SLOs into the design, not after it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose signals: golden signals, RED and USE, and which to alert on\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Design traces, logs and metrics so they connect\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep monitoring independent and cost under control\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"start-with-the-sli\">Start with the SLI\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For each user journey, pick a small number of service level indicators measured as close to the user as practical: the share of requests that succeed and are fast enough, the freshness of data, the share of jobs finished on time. An SLI measured at the load balancer sees more than one measured inside the service, because a crashed service reports nothing. Write the SLO next to the design and make sure the design can measure it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-signals\">The signals\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Golden signals:\u003c/strong> latency, traffic, errors, saturation.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>RED\u003c/strong> for services: rate, errors, duration.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>USE\u003c/strong> for resources: utilisation, saturation, errors.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Alert on symptoms users feel (error rate and latency against the SLO, with burn-rate alerts), not on every cause. Use cause metrics (CPU, queue age, connection pool use, replica lag, cache hit rate) for dashboards and diagnosis. Saturation signals that predict trouble are the most valuable: queue age, thread pool use, disk and file descriptor use, distance to a hard limit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"metrics-logs-and-traces-together\">Metrics, logs and traces together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Metrics are cheap and aggregate: use them to detect and to size the problem. Logs are detailed and expensive: use structured fields (request id, tenant, version) so they can be searched. Traces follow one request across services: use them to find where time or errors come from. Connect them by a shared request or trace id in all three, so you can move from a spike on a graph to an example trace to the log lines.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Control cardinality: a label with millions of values (user id, full URL) can overwhelm a metrics system. Put high-cardinality detail in logs and traces, and sample traces sensibly, keeping all errors and slow requests.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"design-for-the-blast-radius\">Design for the blast radius\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Label metrics by cell, region, version and tenant tier so you can see whether a fault is bounded, and compare canary against baseline. A rollout without a per-version error rate cannot be halted automatically. Include change events (deploys, config pushes, flag flips) on the same timeline, because most incidents begin with a change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"monitoring-that-survives\">Monitoring that survives\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Do not run your only monitoring on the platform it watches. When the platform degrades you lose your view exactly when you need it. Keep a small, simple, independent path for the core signals: external probes from outside your network, a separate metrics store for critical alerts, a separate paging path, and a status page on independent infrastructure. In 2021 Roblox&#39;s monitoring depended on the same Consul cluster that was struggling, which made diagnosis harder; in November 2023 a power failure at the primary site for Cloudflare&#39;s control plane and analytics meant customers could not change settings and logs were lost for about two days. See the case files.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"alert-quality\">Alert quality\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every page should be urgent, actionable and linked to a runbook. Review pages weekly and fix or delete noisy alerts. Test alerts: simulate the failure and confirm that the right person is paged in the expected time. An alert that has never fired is untested.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cost-of-telemetry\">Cost of telemetry\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Telemetry can cost as much as the service. Set retention by value: high-resolution metrics for days, downsampled for months, logs hot for days and cold for compliance. Sample traces, drop debug logs by default, and watch the growth in ingestion volume as a signal in itself.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Your error-rate alert is calculated from application logs. The application crashes at start-up and writes no logs. What does the alert show?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Nothing: no logs means no errors counted, so the alert stays green during a total outage. Measure at the edge or with an external probe, and alert on absence of traffic as well as on error rate.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A crashed service cannot report its own failure.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Dashboards with fifty graphs and no SLO. Alerts on causes that do not matter. Traces without ids in the logs. Telemetry on the failing platform. A label that explodes cardinality and takes the metrics store down during the incident.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-blind-during-the-outage\">Incident: Blind during the outage\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Engineers could not see the state of the platform because the monitoring system ran on it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s -m 5 https://grafana.internal/api/health\ncurl -s -m 5 https://probe.example-external.net/check?target=api\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s -m 5 https://grafana.internal/api/health\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">curl: (28) Operation timed out after 5001 milliseconds\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s -m 5 https://probe.example-external.net/check?target=api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;target&quot;:&quot;api&quot;,&quot;status&quot;:&quot;fail&quot;,&quot;from&quot;:&quot;external&quot;}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is a simulated illustration. The external probe still answers when internal monitoring does not, which is the point of an independent path. The Roblox 73-hour case file describes monitoring that shared the struggling cluster; the design response is a small, independent set of probes and alerts for the core signals.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;What do you instrument in a new service before launch?&quot; SLIs at the edge with an SLO, golden signals per route, per-version and per-cell labels, trace and request ids across logs, saturation signals for each limit, change events, and an external probe, plus a dashboard and runbook for each alert.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Measuring only from inside the service\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Alerting on causes instead of symptoms\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>High-cardinality metric labels\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Monitoring that depends on the monitored platform\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"start-with-the-sli","text":"Start with the SLI"},{"id":"the-signals","text":"The signals"},{"id":"metrics-logs-and-traces-together","text":"Metrics, logs and traces together"},{"id":"design-for-the-blast-radius","text":"Design for the blast radius"},{"id":"monitoring-that-survives","text":"Monitoring that survives"},{"id":"alert-quality","text":"Alert quality"},{"id":"cost-of-telemetry","text":"Cost of telemetry"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-blind-during-the-outage","text":"Incident: Blind during the outage"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Monitoring distributed systems","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/"},{"t":"OpenTelemetry signals","u":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/"}],"text":"Observability is not a tool you add after launch. It is a property you design in: can an on-call engineer, at 3 a.m., tell what is wrong, how many users are affected and where to look, using only what the system emits? The design question is what to emit, where to keep it, and what happens to your view when the system is down. What you will learn Put SLIs and SLOs into the design, not after it Choose signals: golden signals, RED and USE, and which to alert on Design traces, logs and metrics so they connect Keep monitoring independent and cost under control Start with the SLI For each user journey, pick a small number of service level indicators measured as close to the user as practical: the share of requests that succeed and are fast enough, the freshness of data, the share of jobs finished on time. An SLI measured at the load balancer sees more than one measured inside the service, because a crashed service reports nothing. Write the SLO next to the design and make sure the design can measure it. The signals Golden signals: latency, traffic, errors, saturation. RED for services: rate, errors, duration. USE for resources: utilisation, saturation, errors. Alert on symptoms users feel (error rate and latency against the SLO, with burn-rate alerts), not on every cause. Use cause metrics (CPU, queue age, connection pool use, replica lag, cache hit rate) for dashboards and diagnosis. Saturation signals that predict trouble are the most valuable: queue age, thread pool use, disk and file descriptor use, distance to a hard limit. Metrics, logs and traces together Metrics are cheap and aggregate: use them to detect and to size the problem. Logs are detailed and expensive: use structured fields (request id, tenant, version) so they can be searched. Traces follow one request across services: use them to find where time or errors come from. Connect them by a shared request or trace id in all three, so you can move from a spike on a graph to an example trace to the log lines. Control cardinality: a label with millions of values (user id, full URL) can overwhelm a metrics system. Put high-cardinality detail in logs and traces, and sample traces sensibly, keeping all errors and slow requests. Design for the blast radius Label metrics by cell, region, version and tenant tier so you can see whether a fault is bounded, and compare canary against baseline. A rollout without a per-version error rate cannot be halted automatically. Include change events (deploys, config pushes, flag flips) on the same timeline, because most incidents begin with a change. Monitoring that survives Do not run your only monitoring on the platform it watches. When the platform degrades you lose your view exactly when you need it. Keep a small, simple, independent path for the core signals: external probes from outside your network, a separate metrics store for critical alerts, a separate paging path, and a status page on independent infrastructure. In 2021 Roblox&#39;s monitoring depended on the same Consul cluster that was struggling, which made diagnosis harder; in November 2023 a power failure at the primary site for Cloudflare&#39;s control plane and analytics meant customers could not change settings and logs were lost for about two days. See the case files. Alert quality Every page should be urgent, actionable and linked to a runbook. Review pages weekly and fix or delete noisy alerts. Test alerts: simulate the failure and confirm that the right person is paged in the expected time. An alert that has never fired is untested. Cost of telemetry Telemetry can cost as much as the service. Set retention by value: high-resolution metrics for days, downsampled for months, logs hot for days and cold for compliance. Sample traces, drop debug logs by default, and watch the growth in ingestion volume as a signal in itself. Predict first. Your error-rate alert is calculated from application logs. The application crashes at start-up and writes no logs. What does the alert s"},"system-design/security-and-identity-by-design":{"html":"\u003cp>Security that is added after the design shows up as a list of exceptions. Security that is designed in shows up as boundaries: who can call what, with which credential, for how long, and what a stolen credential can reach. This lesson takes the design view; it does not replace the security and identity modules, it uses them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Draw trust boundaries and design for least privilege\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Prefer short-lived, workload-bound credentials over long-lived keys\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Limit blast radius with isolation and segmentation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Plan for secret and key compromise, and for detection\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"assume-a-component-will-be-compromised\">Assume a component will be compromised\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Design as if any one component, key or account will eventually be misused, and ask what that gives an attacker. If the answer is &quot;everything&quot;, the design has a single point of compromise, just as a shared database is a single point of failure. The goals are the same as reliability: bound the blast radius and shorten recovery.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"trust-boundaries\">Trust boundaries\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Draw the boundaries on your diagram: internet to edge, edge to services, service to service, services to data, admin plane to everything. At each boundary decide who may cross, how they prove who they are, and what is checked. Public access to administration is the commonest mistake: Tesla&#39;s cloud account was used for cryptomining in 2017 after attackers reached a Kubernetes administration console that had no password, and found cloud credentials inside a pod. See the case file.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"least-privilege\">Least privilege\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Give each workload only the permissions it needs, on the resources it needs. Wide roles are convenient and expensive. In 2019 an attacker used a server-side request forgery against a misconfigured web application firewall to reach the cloud metadata service, obtained temporary credentials for a role with broad permissions and read data from storage buckets; the case file for Capital One covers this through a published report. Measures that would have narrowed it: restricting the role to what the application needs, enforcing a metadata service version that is harder to abuse, and blocking server-side requests to internal addresses.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"short-lived-bound-credentials\">Short-lived, bound credentials\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A long-lived key is a standing invitation. In 2014 an Amazon access key posted to a public code-sharing site was used to open Uber&#39;s S3 data store; the company found out months later. Prefer credentials that expire in minutes or hours, are issued to a workload identity rather than copied into configuration, and are scoped to the task. Scan for secrets in repositories and logs, rotate on a schedule, and make revocation fast and tested.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"segmentation-and-isolation\">Segmentation and isolation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Separate environments and accounts (production apart from development), separate data classes, and separate tenants where the risk justifies it. Network policies default to deny. Use separate credentials per cell or per service so one leak does not open everything. Isolation is the security twin of cells and bulkheads.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protect-the-control-and-support-planes\">Protect the control and support planes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Support tools, admin consoles and CI systems often have wide access and weaker controls. Between 28 September and 17 October 2023 an attacker used a service account credential to access Okta&#39;s customer support system and download files, including browser session captures with session tokens; 134 customers had files accessed and 5 had sessions hijacked. Treat support and CI as production: least privilege, strong authentication, short sessions, audit logs, and sanitised uploads.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"secrets-keys-and-recovery\">Secrets, keys and recovery\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Store secrets in a manager, not in code, images or logs. Use envelope encryption so keys are separate from data and can be rotated. Plan the compromise runbook before you need it: how to find where a secret is used, how to rotate it without downtime, how to revoke sessions, how to confirm no one else is using it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"detection-and-audit\">Detection and audit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Log authentication, authorisation changes, secret access and data export, to a store the application cannot alter. Alert on impossible behaviour: a service account used from a new place, a sudden large read, a new admin. Detection shortens the time attackers have; Uber&#39;s key was exposed for months before discovery.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"supply-chain\">Supply chain\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Dependencies and build systems are part of the design. Pin versions, verify sources, keep a software bill of materials, restrict who can publish, and isolate build credentials. Several case files show how one compromised package or build step reached many victims.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A service needs to read one bucket. It is given a role that allows full access to all storage in the account. An attacker exploits a flaw in the service. What can they reach, and what is the fix?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Every bucket in the account. Scope the role to read only that bucket (and prefix if possible), use short-lived credentials, and add detection for unusual reads.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The permission set is the blast radius.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Long-lived keys in code. One role for everything. Admin interfaces exposed. Support and CI with more access than production. Secrets that cannot be rotated without an outage. Logs that the attacker can edit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-key-that-opened-the-data-store\">Incident: A key that opened the data store\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A long-lived access key was published in a public repository and later used to read a data store.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws sts get-caller-identity --profile found-key\naws s3 ls s3://driver-data --profile found-key\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws sts get-caller-identity --profile found-key\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{ &quot;UserId&quot;: &quot;AKIA...&quot;, &quot;Account&quot;: &quot;123456789012&quot;, &quot;Arn&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:iam::123456789012:user/deploy&quot; }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> s3://driver-data --profile found-key\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2014-05-12 09:14:07   1388190 drivers.csv\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is a simulated illustration with invented identifiers. The real Uber incident in May 2014 is in the case file. A design with short-lived workload credentials, scoped permissions, secret scanning and access logging would have limited both the exposure and the time to detect.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;How would you secure service-to-service access in a new platform?&quot; Workload identity with short-lived tokens, mutual authentication, per-service least-privilege policies, default-deny network policy, audit logs, and a rotation and revocation runbook tested in advance.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Long-lived keys in code or images\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One broad role shared by many services\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Exposed admin consoles\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Support and CI tools with wider access than production\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"assume-a-component-will-be-compromised","text":"Assume a component will be compromised"},{"id":"trust-boundaries","text":"Trust boundaries"},{"id":"least-privilege","text":"Least privilege"},{"id":"short-lived-bound-credentials","text":"Short-lived, bound credentials"},{"id":"segmentation-and-isolation","text":"Segmentation and isolation"},{"id":"protect-the-control-and-support-planes","text":"Protect the control and support planes"},{"id":"secrets-keys-and-recovery","text":"Secrets, keys and recovery"},{"id":"detection-and-audit","text":"Detection and audit"},{"id":"supply-chain","text":"Supply chain"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-a-key-that-opened-the-data-store","text":"Incident: A key that opened the data store"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Security pillar","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/security-pillar/welcome.html"},{"t":"Azure Well-Architected security","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/well-architected/security/"}],"text":"Security that is added after the design shows up as a list of exceptions. Security that is designed in shows up as boundaries: who can call what, with which credential, for how long, and what a stolen credential can reach. This lesson takes the design view; it does not replace the security and identity modules, it uses them. What you will learn Draw trust boundaries and design for least privilege Prefer short-lived, workload-bound credentials over long-lived keys Limit blast radius with isolation and segmentation Plan for secret and key compromise, and for detection Assume a component will be compromised Design as if any one component, key or account will eventually be misused, and ask what that gives an attacker. If the answer is \"everything\", the design has a single point of compromise, just as a shared database is a single point of failure. The goals are the same as reliability: bound the blast radius and shorten recovery. Trust boundaries Draw the boundaries on your diagram: internet to edge, edge to services, service to service, services to data, admin plane to everything. At each boundary decide who may cross, how they prove who they are, and what is checked. Public access to administration is the commonest mistake: Tesla&#39;s cloud account was used for cryptomining in 2017 after attackers reached a Kubernetes administration console that had no password, and found cloud credentials inside a pod. See the case file. Least privilege Give each workload only the permissions it needs, on the resources it needs. Wide roles are convenient and expensive. In 2019 an attacker used a server-side request forgery against a misconfigured web application firewall to reach the cloud metadata service, obtained temporary credentials for a role with broad permissions and read data from storage buckets; the case file for Capital One covers this through a published report. Measures that would have narrowed it: restricting the role to what the application needs, enforcing a metadata service version that is harder to abuse, and blocking server-side requests to internal addresses. Short-lived, bound credentials A long-lived key is a standing invitation. In 2014 an Amazon access key posted to a public code-sharing site was used to open Uber&#39;s S3 data store; the company found out months later. Prefer credentials that expire in minutes or hours, are issued to a workload identity rather than copied into configuration, and are scoped to the task. Scan for secrets in repositories and logs, rotate on a schedule, and make revocation fast and tested. Segmentation and isolation Separate environments and accounts (production apart from development), separate data classes, and separate tenants where the risk justifies it. Network policies default to deny. Use separate credentials per cell or per service so one leak does not open everything. Isolation is the security twin of cells and bulkheads. Protect the control and support planes Support tools, admin consoles and CI systems often have wide access and weaker controls. Between 28 September and 17 October 2023 an attacker used a service account credential to access Okta&#39;s customer support system and download files, including browser session captures with session tokens; 134 customers had files accessed and 5 had sessions hijacked. Treat support and CI as production: least privilege, strong authentication, short sessions, audit logs, and sanitised uploads. Secrets, keys and recovery Store secrets in a manager, not in code, images or logs. Use envelope encryption so keys are separate from data and can be rotated. Plan the compromise runbook before you need it: how to find where a secret is used, how to rotate it without downtime, how to revoke sessions, how to confirm no one else is using it. Detection and audit Log authentication, authorisation changes, secret access and data export, to a store the application cannot alter. Alert on impossible behaviour: a service account used from a new place, a su"},"system-design/capacity-planning-and-cost-trade-offs":{"html":"\u003cp>Capacity and cost are two views of one decision: how much to run, and how much margin to carry. Too little and a launch or a failure becomes an outage. Too much and the bill hides the problem. This lesson gives a working method to plan capacity from measured numbers and to make cost a visible trade-off in the design.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Plan capacity from load tests, peak factors and redundancy rules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Account for N+1, zone loss and the cold-start problem\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Spot limits that are not CPU: threads, connections, quotas and rate limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Make cost trade-offs explicit and avoid hidden reliability cuts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measure-then-plan\">Measure, then plan\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Capacity planning starts with measured throughput per unit at the SLO: load test one instance (or one cell) until latency or errors cross your target, and record the requests per second it handles at, say, 60 percent of that point. Then take the forecast peak (average times a peak factor, plus known events such as launches, sales and year-end), and divide. Add redundancy: N+1 so one instance can fail, and zone loss so the remaining zones carry the load. With three zones, losing one means the other two must carry 100 percent, so each can run at most about 66 percent of its capacity before the loss.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits-that-are-not-cpu\">Limits that are not CPU\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Systems hit other ceilings first: thread counts, file descriptors, database connections, network packets per second, load balancer limits, API rate limits and cloud quotas, connection tracking tables, DNS query rates. Slack&#39;s outage on 4 January 2021 followed a surge that overloaded an AWS Transit Gateway; AWS Kinesis on 25 November 2020 failed when a small capacity addition pushed servers over an operating system thread limit; Toyota&#39;s plants stopped in August 2023 after a disk space error. List every limit on the request path and watch the distance to each. See the case files.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"headroom-and-autoscaling\">Headroom and autoscaling\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Autoscaling reacts in minutes; a spike arrives in seconds. New instances start cold (empty caches, no warmed connections, long image pulls). Keep headroom for the time it takes to scale, scale on leading signals (queue age, request rate) instead of CPU alone, set sensible minimums, and use limits and load shedding to hold the line meanwhile. Test scale-up under load, not only on paper. When Pokemon GO launched in 2016, traffic reached 50 times the initial target and ten times the worst case estimate; scaling worked because the design could add capacity horizontally and the provider could supply it, which is not something to assume of every dependency.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"forecasting\">Forecasting\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use growth trends and business events, and review monthly. Record the assumptions and compare forecast with actual to improve them. Keep a list of lead times: quota increases, hardware, committed spend, procurement. A forecast that is right but needs a six-week lead time is already late.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cost-as-a-design-input\">Cost as a design input\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cost per request or per customer is a number to estimate in the design, as you estimate latency. The big levers are: right-sizing, using committed or discounted capacity for the steady base and on-demand for the peak, storage tiering and lifecycle rules, data transfer (cross-zone and cross-region traffic often costs more than compute), and retention of logs and metrics. Tag resources by team and service so the bill can be read.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"savings-that-spend-reliability\">Savings that spend reliability\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Some savings quietly cut resilience: one zone instead of three, no spare capacity, shorter retention that removes your ability to investigate, dropping a standby, removing the second DNS provider, skipping restore tests. These are legitimate choices only when stated: write the saving and the risk it takes, and have the owner of the SLO agree. A cheaper design that misses the SLO is not cheaper.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cost-spikes-are-incidents-too\">Cost spikes are incidents too\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A runaway job, a retry storm, a crypto-mining intrusion or an unbounded log can multiply the bill in hours. Set budgets and anomaly alerts, quotas per team, and limits on autoscaling maximums. Tesla&#39;s 2017 case, where attackers ran mining software in a cloud account, is a cost incident that began as a security one.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Three zones, each running at 80 percent of capacity. One zone is lost. What happens to the other two, and what should the design have done?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> They need to carry 120 percent of one zone's share each (3 zones at 80 percent is 240 percent total, spread over two zones is 120 percent each), so they overload. Run each zone at about 60 to 66 percent, or shed low priority load deliberately.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Redundancy is only real if the survivors have the capacity.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A forecast made from average, not peak. A capacity test on a single component while the database is the limit. Autoscaling max set below the real need. A cost cut that removed the margin. A quota hit during a launch.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-limit-nobody-listed\">Incident: The limit nobody listed\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A small capacity addition pushed every server over an operating system limit and the service failed for hours.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cat /proc/sys/kernel/threads-max\nps -eLf | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /proc/sys/kernel/threads-max\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">62000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ps -eLf | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">62113\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The console output is a simulated illustration. In the real AWS Kinesis outage on 25 November 2020 each front-end server kept a thread for every other server, so adding servers pushed each one past the operating system&#39;s thread limit. The design response is a list of every limit on the path with an alert on the distance to each, and capacity changes that are tested and staged.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;How do you size a service for a launch?&quot; Forecast peak with a margin, load test per unit at the SLO, add N+1 and zone-loss headroom, list non-CPU limits, plan scale-up time and cold start, and agree what is shed when demand exceeds the plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Planning from averages\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Watching CPU only\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running every zone near its limit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cost cuts with no recorded reliability trade\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"measure-then-plan","text":"Measure, then plan"},{"id":"limits-that-are-not-cpu","text":"Limits that are not CPU"},{"id":"headroom-and-autoscaling","text":"Headroom and autoscaling"},{"id":"forecasting","text":"Forecasting"},{"id":"cost-as-a-design-input","text":"Cost as a design input"},{"id":"savings-that-spend-reliability","text":"Savings that spend reliability"},{"id":"cost-spikes-are-incidents-too","text":"Cost spikes are incidents too"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-the-limit-nobody-listed","text":"Incident: The limit nobody listed"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Software engineering in SRE (capacity planning)","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/software-engineering-in-sre/"},{"t":"Cost optimization pillar","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/cost-optimization-pillar/welcome.html"}],"text":"Capacity and cost are two views of one decision: how much to run, and how much margin to carry. Too little and a launch or a failure becomes an outage. Too much and the bill hides the problem. This lesson gives a working method to plan capacity from measured numbers and to make cost a visible trade-off in the design. What you will learn Plan capacity from load tests, peak factors and redundancy rules Account for N+1, zone loss and the cold-start problem Spot limits that are not CPU: threads, connections, quotas and rate limits Make cost trade-offs explicit and avoid hidden reliability cuts Measure, then plan Capacity planning starts with measured throughput per unit at the SLO: load test one instance (or one cell) until latency or errors cross your target, and record the requests per second it handles at, say, 60 percent of that point. Then take the forecast peak (average times a peak factor, plus known events such as launches, sales and year-end), and divide. Add redundancy: N+1 so one instance can fail, and zone loss so the remaining zones carry the load. With three zones, losing one means the other two must carry 100 percent, so each can run at most about 66 percent of its capacity before the loss. Limits that are not CPU Systems hit other ceilings first: thread counts, file descriptors, database connections, network packets per second, load balancer limits, API rate limits and cloud quotas, connection tracking tables, DNS query rates. Slack&#39;s outage on 4 January 2021 followed a surge that overloaded an AWS Transit Gateway; AWS Kinesis on 25 November 2020 failed when a small capacity addition pushed servers over an operating system thread limit; Toyota&#39;s plants stopped in August 2023 after a disk space error. List every limit on the request path and watch the distance to each. See the case files. Headroom and autoscaling Autoscaling reacts in minutes; a spike arrives in seconds. New instances start cold (empty caches, no warmed connections, long image pulls). Keep headroom for the time it takes to scale, scale on leading signals (queue age, request rate) instead of CPU alone, set sensible minimums, and use limits and load shedding to hold the line meanwhile. Test scale-up under load, not only on paper. When Pokemon GO launched in 2016, traffic reached 50 times the initial target and ten times the worst case estimate; scaling worked because the design could add capacity horizontally and the provider could supply it, which is not something to assume of every dependency. Forecasting Use growth trends and business events, and review monthly. Record the assumptions and compare forecast with actual to improve them. Keep a list of lead times: quota increases, hardware, committed spend, procurement. A forecast that is right but needs a six-week lead time is already late. Cost as a design input Cost per request or per customer is a number to estimate in the design, as you estimate latency. The big levers are: right-sizing, using committed or discounted capacity for the steady base and on-demand for the peak, storage tiering and lifecycle rules, data transfer (cross-zone and cross-region traffic often costs more than compute), and retention of logs and metrics. Tag resources by team and service so the bill can be read. Savings that spend reliability Some savings quietly cut resilience: one zone instead of three, no spare capacity, shorter retention that removes your ability to investigate, dropping a standby, removing the second DNS provider, skipping restore tests. These are legitimate choices only when stated: write the saving and the risk it takes, and have the owner of the SLO agree. A cheaper design that misses the SLO is not cheaper. Cost spikes are incidents too A runaway job, a retry storm, a crypto-mining intrusion or an unbounded log can multiply the bill in hours. Set budgets and anomaly alerts, quotas per team, and limits on autoscaling maximums. Tesla&#39;s 2017 case, where attackers ran mining software in a clou"},"system-design/worked-design-global-api-at-four-nines":{"html":"\u003cp>Four nines allows about 4.3 minutes of full unavailability in a 30 day month (0.01 percent of 43,200 minutes). One slow human response uses the whole budget. So the design must prevent most failures from reaching users and must recover automatically for the rest. This lesson walks the six-step method on a concrete problem.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Turn an SLO into design requirements\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Compose cells, regions, traffic steering, limits and rollout into one design\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Write down the failure modes and the budget each consumes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Review the design against real outages\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"1-clarify\">1. Clarify\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A public REST API for payments-style requests, global customers, 99.99 percent monthly availability measured at the edge as the share of valid requests answered successfully within 1 second. Writes must never be duplicated. Reads may be up to 5 seconds stale. Out of scope: the dashboard and the batch exports.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"2-estimate\">2. Estimate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Assume 20,000 requests per second at peak, 70 percent reads, 3x peak to average. Average payload 2 KB. Data growth 500 GB per month. One cell sized for 5,000 requests per second at 60 percent utilisation gives 3,000 comfortable. Four cells give 12,000 which is not enough for peak with one lost, so plan 8 cells of 3,000 at steady state: peak 20,000 uses 7 cells, losing one still leaves headroom. Assumptions written down for review.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"3-sketch\">3. Sketch\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Two or three regions, each with several cells; each customer has a home cell in a home region.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A thin global router (anycast plus health-checked DNS from two providers) sends a request to a region; a thin cell router sends it to the customer&#39;s cell.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A cell: gateway with rate limits, stateless API services, a database primary with a synchronous replica in a second zone, an asynchronous replica in another region, a queue for non-critical work, a cache.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Idempotency keys on every write.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"4-deep-dive-on-the-risks\">4. Deep-dive on the risks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Data:\u003c/strong> home-region writes with synchronous replication across zones, asynchronous across regions. RPO inside a region is zero; across regions is seconds. Failover for a cell promotes the zone replica automatically with fencing; promotion to another region needs a human decision because it can lose seconds of writes and splits traffic.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Rollout:\u003c/strong> the biggest outage source. Code and configuration go cell by cell: one canary cell, 15 minute bake with automatic halt on error rate or latency, then cells in groups, one region at a time. No global fast path. Configuration changes use the same pipeline.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Overload:\u003c/strong> token bucket per customer at the gateway, concurrency limits per service, priority classes (payments critical, exports shed first), load shedding with 429 and Retry-After. Retries only from the edge client with a budget.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Degradation:\u003c/strong> reads fall back to the replica or cache when the primary is slow; the fraud scorer fails closed for large amounts and fails open with a flag for small ones; webhooks queue.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"5-failure-modes-and-the-budget\">5. Failure modes and the budget\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Failure\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Blast radius\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Expected effect\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Instance crash\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>one pod\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>none, retried\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Zone loss\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>one third of a cell\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>brief errors, automatic promotion\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cell database failure\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>one cell, about 12 percent of customers\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>seconds to minutes for those customers\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Bad code release\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>canary cell first\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>caught in bake, under 1 percent for 15 minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Bad config push\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>canary cell first\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>same, staged\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Region loss\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>up to half of users\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>decision-based failover, minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Shared dependency (DNS, identity)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>all\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>two DNS providers, cached tokens, static stability\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Weighted by frequency, the sum must stay under 4.3 minutes. The honest answer is that a 12 percent cell outage of 20 minutes costs 2.4 minutes of effective global unavailability, so frequency matters; a small cell count and well-tested recovery keep it in budget.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"6-trade-offs\">6. Trade-offs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cells cost more (duplicate infrastructure, harder cross-customer queries). Home regions add latency for far customers. A synchronous replica adds write latency of a millisecond or so within a region. No global transactions across customers. State what the business gave up for the SLO.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"review-against-real-outages\">Review against real outages\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Cloudflare bot-management file (2025) and Azure Front Door (2025): one change to everything. Your staged rollout and fail-static loaders answer these.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Google Service Control (2025): replicated policy data crashing every region. Your configuration goes cell by cell.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>AWS DynamoDB DNS (2025): an empty regional DNS record from automation. Your static stability and dependence map apply.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>GoCardless (2017): failover that did not run. Your drills and fencing apply.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A bad release is detected after 12 minutes in the canary cell, which serves 12 percent of traffic and was failing half its requests. How much of the monthly error budget did that consume, roughly?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> About 6 percent of requests failed for 12 minutes, which is 12 minutes times 0.06 equal to about 0.72 minutes of full outage, around 17 percent of the 4.3 minute budget. A smaller first stage would cost less.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Blast radius times duration is the budget cost.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The design passes review and decays: the router grows features, someone adds a global cache, a fast path appears. Re-run the dependency map and a failover drill every quarter, and track budget burn.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-canary-that-was-too-big\">Incident: A canary that was too big\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The first stage of a rollout was 25 percent of all traffic. A fault there used most of the quarterly error budget in one afternoon.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"slo report --window 30d --service api\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>slo report --window 30d --service api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">availability 99.962%  target 99.990%  budget_remaining -180%\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This console output is a simulated illustration of budget arithmetic, not a named company. The fix was a first stage of one cell (under 5 percent), a bake time with automatic halt, and a policy that a depleted budget freezes risky changes. See the SRE module for error budget policy.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Try the budget arithmetic yourself in the \u003ca href=\"#/system-design-lab\">System design lab\u003c/a>: estimate the load, then fail each part of a design and watch the budget.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Design an API with 99.99 percent availability.&quot; Use the structure above and state the budget math out loud.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Two zones and a load balancer, called four nines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No staged rollout\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Failover plans never drilled\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No written trade-offs\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"1-clarify","text":"1. Clarify"},{"id":"2-estimate","text":"2. Estimate"},{"id":"3-sketch","text":"3. Sketch"},{"id":"4-deep-dive-on-the-risks","text":"4. Deep-dive on the risks"},{"id":"5-failure-modes-and-the-budget","text":"5. Failure modes and the budget"},{"id":"6-trade-offs","text":"6. Trade-offs"},{"id":"review-against-real-outages","text":"Review against real outages"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-a-canary-that-was-too-big","text":"Incident: A canary that was too big"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Implementing SLOs","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/"},{"t":"Automating safe, hands-off deployments","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/automating-safe-hands-off-deployments/"}],"text":"Four nines allows about 4.3 minutes of full unavailability in a 30 day month (0.01 percent of 43,200 minutes). One slow human response uses the whole budget. So the design must prevent most failures from reaching users and must recover automatically for the rest. This lesson walks the six-step method on a concrete problem. What you will learn Turn an SLO into design requirements Compose cells, regions, traffic steering, limits and rollout into one design Write down the failure modes and the budget each consumes Review the design against real outages 1. Clarify A public REST API for payments-style requests, global customers, 99.99 percent monthly availability measured at the edge as the share of valid requests answered successfully within 1 second. Writes must never be duplicated. Reads may be up to 5 seconds stale. Out of scope: the dashboard and the batch exports. 2. Estimate Assume 20,000 requests per second at peak, 70 percent reads, 3x peak to average. Average payload 2 KB. Data growth 500 GB per month. One cell sized for 5,000 requests per second at 60 percent utilisation gives 3,000 comfortable. Four cells give 12,000 which is not enough for peak with one lost, so plan 8 cells of 3,000 at steady state: peak 20,000 uses 7 cells, losing one still leaves headroom. Assumptions written down for review. 3. Sketch Two or three regions, each with several cells; each customer has a home cell in a home region. A thin global router (anycast plus health-checked DNS from two providers) sends a request to a region; a thin cell router sends it to the customer&#39;s cell. A cell: gateway with rate limits, stateless API services, a database primary with a synchronous replica in a second zone, an asynchronous replica in another region, a queue for non-critical work, a cache. Idempotency keys on every write. 4. Deep-dive on the risks Data: home-region writes with synchronous replication across zones, asynchronous across regions. RPO inside a region is zero; across regions is seconds. Failover for a cell promotes the zone replica automatically with fencing; promotion to another region needs a human decision because it can lose seconds of writes and splits traffic. Rollout: the biggest outage source. Code and configuration go cell by cell: one canary cell, 15 minute bake with automatic halt on error rate or latency, then cells in groups, one region at a time. No global fast path. Configuration changes use the same pipeline. Overload: token bucket per customer at the gateway, concurrency limits per service, priority classes (payments critical, exports shed first), load shedding with 429 and Retry-After. Retries only from the edge client with a budget. Degradation: reads fall back to the replica or cache when the primary is slow; the fraud scorer fails closed for large amounts and fails open with a flag for small ones; webhooks queue. 5. Failure modes and the budget Failure Blast radius Expected effect Instance crash one pod none, retried Zone loss one third of a cell brief errors, automatic promotion Cell database failure one cell, about 12 percent of customers seconds to minutes for those customers Bad code release canary cell first caught in bake, under 1 percent for 15 minutes Bad config push canary cell first same, staged Region loss up to half of users decision-based failover, minutes Shared dependency (DNS, identity) all two DNS providers, cached tokens, static stability Weighted by frequency, the sum must stay under 4.3 minutes. The honest answer is that a 12 percent cell outage of 20 minutes costs 2.4 minutes of effective global unavailability, so frequency matters; a small cell count and well-tested recovery keep it in budget. 6. Trade-offs Cells cost more (duplicate infrastructure, harder cross-customer queries). Home regions add latency for far customers. A synchronous replica adds write latency of a millisecond or so within a region. No global transactions across customers. State what the business gave up for the SLO. Review again"},"system-design/worked-design-the-status-page-that-survives":{"html":"\u003cp>The status page is the simplest system with the hardest requirement: it must be up exactly when the rest is down. Designing it shows every principle in the module in a small space. After that, we use the same lens to review a real outage design, before and after.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Derive independence requirements from a failure scenario\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Design a communication path that does not share fate with the product\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Run a before and after design review of an outage using a case file\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Write follow-ups that change the design, not only the runbook\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"part-1-the-status-page\">Part 1. The status page\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Clarify.\u003c/strong> Customers and staff must see current state and updates during any outage of our platform, DNS, identity or cloud account. Readers: tens of thousands during an incident. Writers: a few on-call staff. Updates can take a minute to appear. It must work if our SSO is down.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Estimate.\u003c/strong> Reads at 50,000 requests per second would be a surge; a static page of 50 KB behind a CDN serves that at trivial cost. Writes: a handful an hour.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Sketch.\u003c/strong>\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A static site generated from a small data file, published to object storage on a different provider and account, behind a CDN on a different DNS zone from the product.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Updates through a simple authenticated tool with its own accounts (not company SSO) with multi-factor, plus a break-glass path.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A second publishing path (an API and a documented manual upload) so one tool outage does not stop updates.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Email and chat notifications from a separate provider.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Automated probes from outside the platform update the page when checks fail, but a human can override.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Failure modes.\u003c/strong>\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Scenario\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Design answer\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Our cloud account unavailable\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>page is on another provider\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Our DNS down\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>status domain on a different registrar and DNS provider\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>SSO down\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>separate accounts for publishers, tested quarterly\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Publisher tool down\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>manual upload path documented and drilled\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Page itself attacked\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>static content on a CDN, rate limits\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Automated probes wrong\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>human override, probes labelled as automatic\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Trade-offs.\u003c/strong> A second provider costs a little and adds a process to maintain. Static pages cannot show personalised state. Accept both.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>The test.\u003c/strong> Every quarter, shut off the product&#39;s DNS and SSO in a staging copy and publish an update. Time it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"part-2-reviewing-an-outage-design\">Part 2. Reviewing an outage design\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A design review after an outage asks the same questions as a design review before one. Use this frame on a case file:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Before design:\u003c/strong> draw what existed and the dependencies, including build, operate and recover graphs.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Trigger and latent cause:\u003c/strong> what changed, and what weakness did it meet?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Why it spread:\u003c/strong> which failure domain, shared dependency or missing limit?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Why it lasted:\u003c/strong> detection time, diagnosis tools, recovery dependencies, tested rollback?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>After design:\u003c/strong> the changes, each mapped to a principle (cell, stage, limit, fail static, independence).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>What is still open:\u003c/strong> residual risk, honestly.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch3 id=\"example-facebook-4-october-2021\">Example: Facebook, 4 October 2021\u003c/h3>\n\u003cp>The case file uses Cloudflare&#39;s outside view. Before: DNS servers announced through the company&#39;s own backbone; a configuration change on that backbone. Trigger: the change. Latent cause: the DNS servers withdrew themselves from routing when they could not reach the data centres, so the name stopped resolving globally. Why it lasted: tools that depended on the same network. After-design lessons: stage backbone changes with a pre-check that proves access remains, keep out-of-band access, and make DNS survive the loss of its backend. Open: any such choice remains a trade between withdrawing a broken server and keeping a stale one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3 id=\"example-s3-in-us-east-1-28-february-2017\">Example: S3 in us-east-1, 28 February 2017\u003c/h3>\n\u003cp>Trigger: a debugging command removed more capacity than intended. Latent: two subsystems needed a full restart that nobody had practised at that scale. Why it lasted: restart took longer than expected. After: the tool limits how fast capacity can be removed and a minimum capacity floor; restart paths were practised. A further twist: the status dashboard itself depended on S3, which is exactly the design flaw in Part 1.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> In an outage review you list &quot;engineer ran the wrong command&quot; as the root cause. What is missing?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The design that allowed a single command to remove too much, the missing guardrail or limit, and why recovery was slow. People make errors; the review should change the system so the error is bounded.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A cause that ends at a person is not a design change.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Reviews that stop at the trigger. Follow-ups that are only &quot;be more careful&quot;. A list of 40 actions with no owner and no ordering. Rank by blast radius reduced per effort and track completion.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-dashboard-that-needed-the-broken-service\">Incident: The dashboard that needed the broken service\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>During a major storage outage, the page that should have reported it was itself slow to update because it depended on the failing service.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\\n' https://status.example.com\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s -o /dev/null -w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;%{http_code}\\n&#x27;\u003c/span> https://status.example.com\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">503\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is a simulated illustration. In the real S3 incident of 28 February 2017, AWS reported that the status dashboard&#39;s own images were stored in S3, so it could not show colour changes and the team used text updates instead. See the case file. The design answer is the independent page in Part 1.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Practise this with a clock in the \u003ca href=\"#/design-interview\">mock design interview\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;You are asked to review a postmortem. What do you look for?&quot; Whether actions reduce blast radius, whether detection and recovery dependencies are addressed, whether owners and dates exist, and whether the fixes get tested.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Status page on the same stack\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Review ends at human error\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Actions that add procedure but no system change\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never testing the communication path\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"part-1-the-status-page","text":"Part 1. The status page"},{"id":"part-2-reviewing-an-outage-design","text":"Part 2. Reviewing an outage design"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-the-dashboard-that-needed-the-broken-service","text":"Incident: The dashboard that needed the broken service"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Postmortem culture (SRE book)","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/"},{"t":"Postmortem culture (SRE workbook)","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/postmortem-culture/"}],"text":"The status page is the simplest system with the hardest requirement: it must be up exactly when the rest is down. Designing it shows every principle in the module in a small space. After that, we use the same lens to review a real outage design, before and after. What you will learn Derive independence requirements from a failure scenario Design a communication path that does not share fate with the product Run a before and after design review of an outage using a case file Write follow-ups that change the design, not only the runbook Part 1. The status page Clarify. Customers and staff must see current state and updates during any outage of our platform, DNS, identity or cloud account. Readers: tens of thousands during an incident. Writers: a few on-call staff. Updates can take a minute to appear. It must work if our SSO is down. Estimate. Reads at 50,000 requests per second would be a surge; a static page of 50 KB behind a CDN serves that at trivial cost. Writes: a handful an hour. Sketch. A static site generated from a small data file, published to object storage on a different provider and account, behind a CDN on a different DNS zone from the product. Updates through a simple authenticated tool with its own accounts (not company SSO) with multi-factor, plus a break-glass path. A second publishing path (an API and a documented manual upload) so one tool outage does not stop updates. Email and chat notifications from a separate provider. Automated probes from outside the platform update the page when checks fail, but a human can override. Failure modes. Scenario Design answer Our cloud account unavailable page is on another provider Our DNS down status domain on a different registrar and DNS provider SSO down separate accounts for publishers, tested quarterly Publisher tool down manual upload path documented and drilled Page itself attacked static content on a CDN, rate limits Automated probes wrong human override, probes labelled as automatic Trade-offs. A second provider costs a little and adds a process to maintain. Static pages cannot show personalised state. Accept both. The test. Every quarter, shut off the product&#39;s DNS and SSO in a staging copy and publish an update. Time it. Part 2. Reviewing an outage design A design review after an outage asks the same questions as a design review before one. Use this frame on a case file: Before design: draw what existed and the dependencies, including build, operate and recover graphs. Trigger and latent cause: what changed, and what weakness did it meet? Why it spread: which failure domain, shared dependency or missing limit? Why it lasted: detection time, diagnosis tools, recovery dependencies, tested rollback? After design: the changes, each mapped to a principle (cell, stage, limit, fail static, independence). What is still open: residual risk, honestly. Example: Facebook, 4 October 2021 The case file uses Cloudflare&#39;s outside view. Before: DNS servers announced through the company&#39;s own backbone; a configuration change on that backbone. Trigger: the change. Latent cause: the DNS servers withdrew themselves from routing when they could not reach the data centres, so the name stopped resolving globally. Why it lasted: tools that depended on the same network. After-design lessons: stage backbone changes with a pre-check that proves access remains, keep out-of-band access, and make DNS survive the loss of its backend. Open: any such choice remains a trade between withdrawing a broken server and keeping a stale one. Example: S3 in us-east-1, 28 February 2017 Trigger: a debugging command removed more capacity than intended. Latent: two subsystems needed a full restart that nobody had practised at that scale. Why it lasted: restart took longer than expected. After: the tool limits how fast capacity can be removed and a minimum capacity floor; restart paths were practised. A further twist: the status dashboard itself depended on S3, which is exactly the design flaw in "},"system-design/worked-design-a-url-shortener":{"html":"\u003cp>A URL shortener looks trivial and is a good test of method: it is read-heavy, needs unique short keys without collisions, has a few extremely hot links, and attracts abuse. The goal here is not the shortest answer; it is a design you can defend with numbers and failure modes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Run the six steps on a read-heavy service\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Compare key generation strategies and their failure modes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Design the cache, the store and the redirect path for hot links\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Add abuse controls and an availability story\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"1-clarify\">1. Clarify\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Create a short link for a long URL; redirect on request. Optional custom aliases, expiry and click counts. Redirects must be fast (under 100 ms at the edge) and highly available, because every shared link depends on them. Creation can be slower and can fail briefly. Links must not collide, and a redirect must never go to the wrong target. SLO: 99.95 percent of redirects succeed within 300 ms.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"2-estimate\">2. Estimate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Assume 100 million new links a month and 100 reads per write. Writes: 100 million divided by about 2.6 million seconds in a month is about 40 per second. Reads: 4,000 per second on average, about 12,000 at a 3x peak. Storage: 100 million links a month at roughly 500 bytes each is 50 GB a month, 600 GB a year, a few TB over five years with replication. The write path is small; the read path and its availability are the design.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A 7 character key in base 62 gives 62 to the power 7, about 3.5 trillion keys, far more than the roughly 6 billion links in five years. Six characters (56 billion) would also do.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"3-sketch\">3. Sketch\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Clients, a CDN or edge cache, redirect service (stateless), a cache, a key-value store partitioned by short key, and a separate create service with a key generator. Redirects return HTTP 301 or 302: 301 is cached by browsers (fewer requests, harder to change or count), 302 is not cached (more requests, easier to control). Pick on purpose: 302 if you count clicks or allow edits.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"4-deep-dive\">4. Deep-dive\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Key generation.\u003c/strong> Options: hash the URL and truncate (collisions possible, same URL gives same key, needs collision handling); a counter encoded in base 62 (no collisions, predictable keys, needs a safe distributed counter); pre-generated random keys handed out in blocks from a key service (no collisions, no coordination on the hot path). A common safe choice is each create server leasing a block of ids from a central allocator, then encoding with a keyed permutation so keys are not guessable. If the allocator is down, servers continue until their block runs out; make blocks large enough to outlast an outage.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Store.\u003c/strong> Lookup by short key only, so a partitioned key-value store with hashed keys fits, with three replicas. Reads dominate and are single-key. Writes are idempotent with a client token so a retry does not create a second link.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Cache.\u003c/strong> Most traffic goes to a small set of links. A cache in front of the store, plus the CDN for very hot links, takes most reads. Use jittered TTLs, request coalescing and negative caching for missing keys, so a scanner guessing keys does not hit the store. Plan for a viral link: a single key may carry a large share of traffic, so replicate hot keys across cache nodes or serve them at the edge.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Counts.\u003c/strong> Counting clicks on the redirect path would slow it. Emit an event to a queue and aggregate asynchronously, accepting that counts lag by seconds.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Abuse.\u003c/strong> Short links hide destinations, which attackers use. Scan targets against block lists at creation and periodically afterwards, rate limit creation per account and address, offer a preview page for suspicious links, and provide takedown that works through every cache layer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"5-failure-modes\">5. Failure modes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Failure\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Effect\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Design answer\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cache node lost\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>misses rise\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>replicas, coalescing, store sized for cold cache\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Store partition down\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>some keys fail\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>replicas in other zones, retries with budget\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Allocator down\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>creation stops after block\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>large blocks, fallback allocator, alert on remaining ids\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Viral link\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>one key hot\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>edge cache, key replication, per-key shedding\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Regional outage\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>redirects fail\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>multi-region read replicas, DNS failover, static fallback for hottest links\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Bad deploy\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>wrong redirects\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>staged rollout, per-version error and redirect checks\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Abuse wave\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>cost and reputation\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>creation limits, scanning, takedown path\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"6-trade-offs\">6. Trade-offs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Random keys avoid guessing but lose sortability. 302 costs more traffic but keeps control. Asynchronous counts are cheap but slightly stale. Multi-region replicas cost money and need a plan for writes (one home region for creates, reads everywhere). Say what you chose and what you would revisit at 10x scale.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A link goes viral and one short key receives 40 percent of all redirects. What breaks first, and what helps?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The single cache or store partition that holds that key becomes a hot spot. Serve it from the CDN or replicate the key across cache nodes, and shed or rate limit per key if needed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Hashing spreads keys, not the traffic to one key.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Collisions that send users to the wrong target. A counter that two servers share without coordination. A hot key that overloads one node. A takedown that does not purge the CDN. A cache that was sized for the warm case.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-traffic-fifty-times-the-plan\">Incident: Traffic fifty times the plan\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A launch brought far more traffic than anyone had planned for, and the backend scaled only because it could add machines quickly.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get hpa redirect\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get hpa redirect\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME       REFERENCE             TARGETS    MINPODS   MAXPODS   REPLICAS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">redirect   Deployment/redirect   95%/60%    10        10        10\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The console output is a simulated illustration of autoscaling pinned at its maximum. The real related case is the Pokemon GO launch in 2016, where traffic reached 50 times the initial target and ten times the worst case estimate; see the case file. For the shortener, the lesson is to set maximums from the worst case, keep the CDN absorbing hot links, and test scale-up.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Design a URL shortener for 100 million links a month.&quot; Use the numbers above, choose block-leased random keys, a partitioned key-value store, a cache and CDN, asynchronous counts, abuse controls and multi-region reads, then walk the failure table.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Spending the interview on key encoding and none on availability\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A single counter that is a single point of failure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>301 without understanding browser caching\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No story for a viral key\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"1-clarify","text":"1. Clarify"},{"id":"2-estimate","text":"2. Estimate"},{"id":"3-sketch","text":"3. Sketch"},{"id":"4-deep-dive","text":"4. Deep-dive"},{"id":"5-failure-modes","text":"5. Failure modes"},{"id":"6-trade-offs","text":"6. Trade-offs"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-traffic-fifty-times-the-plan","text":"Incident: Traffic fifty times the plan"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Caching challenges and strategies","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/caching-challenges-and-strategies/"},{"t":"Cache-aside pattern","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/cache-aside"}],"text":"A URL shortener looks trivial and is a good test of method: it is read-heavy, needs unique short keys without collisions, has a few extremely hot links, and attracts abuse. The goal here is not the shortest answer; it is a design you can defend with numbers and failure modes. What you will learn Run the six steps on a read-heavy service Compare key generation strategies and their failure modes Design the cache, the store and the redirect path for hot links Add abuse controls and an availability story 1. Clarify Create a short link for a long URL; redirect on request. Optional custom aliases, expiry and click counts. Redirects must be fast (under 100 ms at the edge) and highly available, because every shared link depends on them. Creation can be slower and can fail briefly. Links must not collide, and a redirect must never go to the wrong target. SLO: 99.95 percent of redirects succeed within 300 ms. 2. Estimate Assume 100 million new links a month and 100 reads per write. Writes: 100 million divided by about 2.6 million seconds in a month is about 40 per second. Reads: 4,000 per second on average, about 12,000 at a 3x peak. Storage: 100 million links a month at roughly 500 bytes each is 50 GB a month, 600 GB a year, a few TB over five years with replication. The write path is small; the read path and its availability are the design. A 7 character key in base 62 gives 62 to the power 7, about 3.5 trillion keys, far more than the roughly 6 billion links in five years. Six characters (56 billion) would also do. 3. Sketch Clients, a CDN or edge cache, redirect service (stateless), a cache, a key-value store partitioned by short key, and a separate create service with a key generator. Redirects return HTTP 301 or 302: 301 is cached by browsers (fewer requests, harder to change or count), 302 is not cached (more requests, easier to control). Pick on purpose: 302 if you count clicks or allow edits. 4. Deep-dive Key generation. Options: hash the URL and truncate (collisions possible, same URL gives same key, needs collision handling); a counter encoded in base 62 (no collisions, predictable keys, needs a safe distributed counter); pre-generated random keys handed out in blocks from a key service (no collisions, no coordination on the hot path). A common safe choice is each create server leasing a block of ids from a central allocator, then encoding with a keyed permutation so keys are not guessable. If the allocator is down, servers continue until their block runs out; make blocks large enough to outlast an outage. Store. Lookup by short key only, so a partitioned key-value store with hashed keys fits, with three replicas. Reads dominate and are single-key. Writes are idempotent with a client token so a retry does not create a second link. Cache. Most traffic goes to a small set of links. A cache in front of the store, plus the CDN for very hot links, takes most reads. Use jittered TTLs, request coalescing and negative caching for missing keys, so a scanner guessing keys does not hit the store. Plan for a viral link: a single key may carry a large share of traffic, so replicate hot keys across cache nodes or serve them at the edge. Counts. Counting clicks on the redirect path would slow it. Emit an event to a queue and aggregate asynchronously, accepting that counts lag by seconds. Abuse. Short links hide destinations, which attackers use. Scan targets against block lists at creation and periodically afterwards, rate limit creation per account and address, offer a preview page for suspicious links, and provide takedown that works through every cache layer. 5. Failure modes Failure Effect Design answer Cache node lost misses rise replicas, coalescing, store sized for cold cache Store partition down some keys fail replicas in other zones, retries with budget Allocator down creation stops after block large blocks, fallback allocator, alert on remaining ids Viral link one key hot edge cache, key replication, per-key shedding Regional outa"},"system-design/worked-design-a-rate-limiter-service":{"html":"\u003cp>A rate limiter protects services and enforces fairness. It sits on every request, so it must be fast and must never become the reason requests fail. The design question is less &quot;which algorithm&quot; than &quot;what do we do when the limiter itself is wrong, slow or down&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choose limits, keys and algorithm for a gateway limiter\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Design shared counters and accept approximate accuracy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Decide fail-open or fail-closed and protect the limiter from itself\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Communicate limits to clients and watch the system\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"1-clarify\">1. Clarify\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Limit requests per API key per second and per minute, with different tiers, plus a global protection limit per service. It runs in the gateway on every call. Limit decisions must add under 2 ms. Slightly inaccurate limits are acceptable (a customer allowed 105 instead of 100 for a moment). A limiter outage must not block traffic. Limits are changed by operators and must roll out safely.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"2-estimate\">2. Estimate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Gateway traffic 50,000 requests per second at peak across 20 gateway nodes, 200,000 active keys. A counter per key and window is small: 200,000 keys by a few counters by about 64 bytes is a few tens of MB, easily in memory. The cost is the network call: asking a shared store for every request is 50,000 operations per second of added latency and a new dependency on the hot path.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"3-sketch\">3. Sketch\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Local limiter in each gateway node (token bucket in memory), plus a shared store (replicated in-memory store) holding counts per key for accurate global limits, synchronised in batches. A configuration service distributes rules. A metrics path records allow, deny and error decisions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"4-deep-dive\">4. Deep-dive\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Algorithm.\u003c/strong> A token bucket per key: tokens refill at the sustained rate up to the burst size; each request spends one. It allows controlled bursts and is cheap. For protecting a service, add a concurrency limit that caps in-flight requests, because slow requests hold capacity.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Where state lives.\u003c/strong> Three choices: purely local (each node enforces limit divided by nodes; cheap, inaccurate when traffic is uneven); a central store consulted per request (accurate, adds latency and a dependency); or hybrid. The hybrid is common: each node holds a local allowance, and refills it in chunks from the shared store (for example 10 percent of the key&#39;s limit at a time). Accuracy is within a chunk, store calls drop by an order of magnitude, and if the store is unreachable the node keeps enforcing its local allowance.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Atomicity.\u003c/strong> The shared update must be atomic (increment and compare in one operation) to avoid races between gateway nodes. Use the store&#39;s atomic operations or a short script, not read then write.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Failure behaviour.\u003c/strong> If the store is down, fail open to local limits with a conservative floor. Blocking all traffic because the limiter cannot count turns a minor fault into an outage. For sensitive operations such as login attempts, you may choose fail-closed or stricter local limits; decide per rule and write it down.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Protecting the limiter.\u003c/strong> Cache denies for a short time, apply a cheap pre-filter for obviously abusive sources before the keyed lookup, and bound the work per request. Treat rules as configuration with validation, staged rollout and a floor: an automated or mistaken rule that sets a limit to zero is an outage. In December 2020 an automated quota system cut the quota of a core Google authentication service to zero because an old system reported its usage as zero, and authentication failed for almost every Google service for about 50 minutes. See the case file.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Client contract.\u003c/strong> Return 429 with a Retry-After header and rate limit headers showing the limit and remaining count. Document backoff with jitter. Clients that ignore it are limited harder. In December 2021 an automated scaling activity made clients on AWS&#39;s internal network flood the devices linking it to the main network, and clients did not back off; congestion lasted hours. A clear contract and client-side backoff matter even inside one company.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"5-failure-modes\">5. Failure modes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Failure\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Effect\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Design answer\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Shared store down\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>accuracy lost\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>local allowance continues, alert\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Store slow\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>added latency\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>tight timeout, circuit breaker, skip to local\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Bad rule pushed\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>mass rejections\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>validation, floor, staged rollout, kill switch\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Clock skew\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>windows misaligned\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>token bucket with monotonic time\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Hot key\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>one node overloaded\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>local caching of denies, per-node limits\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Gateway restart\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>local state lost\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>brief extra allowance acceptable\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Retry storm\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>load amplified\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>429 with Retry-After, retry budgets\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"6-trade-offs\">6. Trade-offs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Accuracy against latency: chunked allowances are fast and approximate. Fail-open protects availability and risks abuse during the outage. Per-key limits need many counters; a few tiers with shared limits are cheaper. State these choices.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> The shared counter store fails. The gateway is set to fail closed. What happens, and what would you change for most traffic?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Every request is rejected because the limiter cannot count, so a limiter fault becomes a full outage. Fail open with a conservative local limit for most rules, and keep fail-closed only for the few sensitive ones.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A protective component must not become the single point of failure.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A limit of zero. A store that becomes the slowest call on the path. A distributed counter updated with read-then-write. Clients with no backoff. Operators unable to see which rule denied a request.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-quota-that-read-zero\">Incident: A quota that read zero\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An automated process set a limit to zero from a bad reading, and the limiter obeyed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ratectl show rule identity-core\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ratectl show rule identity-core\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">limit_per_minute: 0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">source: automated-quota-sync\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">last_usage_reading: 0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is a simulated illustration of the pattern. The real event is the Google authentication quota outage of 14 December 2020, in the case file. The guards are a floor under every limit, a sanity check against the previous value, staged application and a kill switch.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Design a rate limiter for an API gateway.&quot; Ask for keys and limits, choose token bucket plus a concurrency limit, use local allowances refilled in chunks from a shared atomic store, fail open with a floor, return 429 with Retry-After, validate and stage rule changes, and monitor decisions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A shared store call on every request with no fallback\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Read then write counters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Limits that can drop to zero\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No client guidance on backoff\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"1-clarify","text":"1. Clarify"},{"id":"2-estimate","text":"2. Estimate"},{"id":"3-sketch","text":"3. Sketch"},{"id":"4-deep-dive","text":"4. Deep-dive"},{"id":"5-failure-modes","text":"5. Failure modes"},{"id":"6-trade-offs","text":"6. Trade-offs"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-a-quota-that-read-zero","text":"Incident: A quota that read zero"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Using load shedding to avoid overload","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/using-load-shedding-to-avoid-overload/"},{"t":"Rate limiting pattern","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/rate-limiting-pattern"}],"text":"A rate limiter protects services and enforces fairness. It sits on every request, so it must be fast and must never become the reason requests fail. The design question is less \"which algorithm\" than \"what do we do when the limiter itself is wrong, slow or down\". What you will learn Choose limits, keys and algorithm for a gateway limiter Design shared counters and accept approximate accuracy Decide fail-open or fail-closed and protect the limiter from itself Communicate limits to clients and watch the system 1. Clarify Limit requests per API key per second and per minute, with different tiers, plus a global protection limit per service. It runs in the gateway on every call. Limit decisions must add under 2 ms. Slightly inaccurate limits are acceptable (a customer allowed 105 instead of 100 for a moment). A limiter outage must not block traffic. Limits are changed by operators and must roll out safely. 2. Estimate Gateway traffic 50,000 requests per second at peak across 20 gateway nodes, 200,000 active keys. A counter per key and window is small: 200,000 keys by a few counters by about 64 bytes is a few tens of MB, easily in memory. The cost is the network call: asking a shared store for every request is 50,000 operations per second of added latency and a new dependency on the hot path. 3. Sketch Local limiter in each gateway node (token bucket in memory), plus a shared store (replicated in-memory store) holding counts per key for accurate global limits, synchronised in batches. A configuration service distributes rules. A metrics path records allow, deny and error decisions. 4. Deep-dive Algorithm. A token bucket per key: tokens refill at the sustained rate up to the burst size; each request spends one. It allows controlled bursts and is cheap. For protecting a service, add a concurrency limit that caps in-flight requests, because slow requests hold capacity. Where state lives. Three choices: purely local (each node enforces limit divided by nodes; cheap, inaccurate when traffic is uneven); a central store consulted per request (accurate, adds latency and a dependency); or hybrid. The hybrid is common: each node holds a local allowance, and refills it in chunks from the shared store (for example 10 percent of the key&#39;s limit at a time). Accuracy is within a chunk, store calls drop by an order of magnitude, and if the store is unreachable the node keeps enforcing its local allowance. Atomicity. The shared update must be atomic (increment and compare in one operation) to avoid races between gateway nodes. Use the store&#39;s atomic operations or a short script, not read then write. Failure behaviour. If the store is down, fail open to local limits with a conservative floor. Blocking all traffic because the limiter cannot count turns a minor fault into an outage. For sensitive operations such as login attempts, you may choose fail-closed or stricter local limits; decide per rule and write it down. Protecting the limiter. Cache denies for a short time, apply a cheap pre-filter for obviously abusive sources before the keyed lookup, and bound the work per request. Treat rules as configuration with validation, staged rollout and a floor: an automated or mistaken rule that sets a limit to zero is an outage. In December 2020 an automated quota system cut the quota of a core Google authentication service to zero because an old system reported its usage as zero, and authentication failed for almost every Google service for about 50 minutes. See the case file. Client contract. Return 429 with a Retry-After header and rate limit headers showing the limit and remaining count. Document backoff with jitter. Clients that ignore it are limited harder. In December 2021 an automated scaling activity made clients on AWS&#39;s internal network flood the devices linking it to the main network, and clients did not back off; congestion lasted hours. A clear contract and client-side backoff matter even inside one company. 5. Failure modes Failure "},"system-design/worked-design-a-notification-fan-out-system":{"html":"\u003cp>A notification system turns one event (&quot;new message&quot;, &quot;payment received&quot;, &quot;outage&quot;) into many deliveries over email, push and chat. The hard parts are fan-out, uneven load, third-party limits and not sending the same message twice or a million at once to a recovering dependency.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Design fan-out on write and fan-out on read, and when to mix them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use priorities, queues and per-provider limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Make delivery idempotent and avoid duplicate sends\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Protect providers and the system from retry storms\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"1-clarify\">1. Clarify\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Producers publish events with a type, an audience and a payload. The system delivers to each recipient&#39;s chosen channels. Critical notices (security alerts, payment failures) are time sensitive; marketing can wait. Users set preferences and quiet hours. Duplicates are annoying, and for some notices harmful. SLO: 99 percent of critical notifications are handed to a provider within 10 seconds, bulk within 30 minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"2-estimate\">2. Estimate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>50 million users, 5 notifications per user per day: 250 million a day, about 2,900 per second average, bursts to 10x on events (a flash sale or an outage notice). A single broadcast to 20 million users must complete in minutes: 20 million over 600 seconds is about 33,000 per second sustained, far above the average. Provider limits apply: email and push providers cap per-second sends and per-recipient rates, so the egress rate is set by the providers, not by our fleet.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"3-sketch\">3. Sketch\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Event intake API, an event stream, a fan-out service that expands audiences into per-recipient messages, priority queues per channel, channel workers that call providers, a preference and deduplication store, a delivery status store, and a dead-letter queue.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"4-deep-dive\">4. Deep-dive\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Fan-out strategy.\u003c/strong> For targeted notices (one user), expand immediately. For huge audiences (all users), expanding to 20 million messages at intake would flood the queue and delay critical traffic. Expand in batches by partition of the user table, as background jobs that are rate limited, and keep them off the critical lane. That is fan-out on write for most cases, with controlled batching for broadcasts. Where a recipient pulls their feed (an in-app inbox), fan-out on read avoids storing millions of copies; hybrids store for normal users and read for celebrity-sized audiences.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Priority lanes.\u003c/strong> Separate queues and workers for critical, normal and bulk traffic, with their own capacity so a marketing blast cannot delay a security alert. That is a bulkhead. Workers pull from critical first.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Provider limits and backpressure.\u003c/strong> Each channel worker has a rate limiter matched to the provider&#39;s limits and a circuit breaker. If the provider slows or returns errors, the breaker opens, messages wait in the queue, and bulk is delayed before critical. Use a second provider for critical channels when the cost justifies it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Idempotency and dedup.\u003c/strong> Give each notification a unique id (event id plus recipient plus channel). Before sending, record the id in the dedup store in the same step as claiming the send; a redelivery from the queue finds the id and skips. Providers that accept an idempotency key should receive it. Accept that some paths are at-least-once and make duplicates harmless where possible.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Retries.\u003c/strong> Retry transient failures with exponential backoff and jitter, a cap, and a retry budget per provider. Invalid addresses and unsubscribed users are not retried; failures go to a dead-letter queue with an owner. In January 2019 a retry storm from one application overloaded CoreDNS in Zalando&#39;s Kubernetes clusters, pods ran out of memory and DNS went down for over an hour: the same pattern applies to workers retrying a slow provider while also overloading name resolution. See the case file.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Preferences and quiet hours.\u003c/strong> Check preferences at send time, not only at intake, because they change. Defer non-critical messages during quiet hours using a delayed queue, and spread the morning wake-up burst with jitter.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"5-failure-modes\">5. Failure modes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Failure\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Effect\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Design answer\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Provider outage\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>deliveries stall\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>breaker, queue, secondary provider for critical\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Broadcast floods queue\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>critical delayed\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>separate lanes, rate-limited expansion\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Worker crash mid-send\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>possible duplicate\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>dedup record, idempotency key\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Poison message\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>worker loops\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>attempt cap and dead-letter queue\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Queue backlog\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>stale notices\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>age alerts, expire old bulk messages\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Preference store down\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>wrong sends\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>cached preferences, fail safe to critical only\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Retry storm\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>provider and DNS overload\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>jitter, budget, limited concurrency\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"6-trade-offs\">6. Trade-offs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>At-least-once delivery with dedup is simpler than exactly-once and occasionally duplicates. Separate lanes cost idle capacity. A second provider costs money and integration work. Expiring stale bulk messages protects users from a flood of old notices at the cost of undelivered ones. State which you chose.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A marketing broadcast to 20 million users is queued at the same moment as password reset emails. All use one queue. What happens, and what is the fix?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Password resets wait behind millions of marketing messages and arrive late. Use separate priority lanes with their own workers and rate-limited expansion for the broadcast.\u003c/p>\u003cp>One queue means one fate for all traffic.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A broadcast that starves critical alerts. Duplicate sends after a worker restart. A provider limit exceeded so the account is throttled or suspended. An expired credential for a provider. A dead-letter queue nobody reads.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-retry-storm-against-shared-infrastructure\">Incident: A retry storm against shared infrastructure\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>One application retried aggressively and overloaded a shared service that many others needed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl top pod -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns\nkubectl get events -n kube-system | grep OOMKilled | head -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl top pod -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME                       CPU(cores)   MEMORY(bytes)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">coredns-6d4b75cb6d-x7k2p   1998m        1.9Gi\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get events -n kube-system | grep OOMKilled | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Warning  OOMKilled  pod/coredns-6d4b75cb6d-x7k2p  Container exceeded memory limit\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The console output is a simulated illustration. The real case is Zalando on 7 January 2019, in the case file. For a notification system, the response is a per-provider retry budget, jittered backoff, concurrency limits per worker pool, and keeping bulk traffic behind critical traffic so a storm cannot take shared dependencies down.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Design a notification system for 50 million users.&quot; Cover event intake, fan-out strategy, priority lanes, per-provider limits, dedup ids, retries with budgets, preferences, and the failure table.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>One queue for all priorities\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expanding a broadcast at intake\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retrying without a budget\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring provider rate limits and quotas\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"1-clarify","text":"1. Clarify"},{"id":"2-estimate","text":"2. Estimate"},{"id":"3-sketch","text":"3. Sketch"},{"id":"4-deep-dive","text":"4. Deep-dive"},{"id":"5-failure-modes","text":"5. Failure modes"},{"id":"6-trade-offs","text":"6. Trade-offs"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-a-retry-storm-against-shared-infrastructure","text":"Incident: A retry storm against shared infrastructure"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Timeouts, retries and backoff with jitter","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/timeouts-retries-and-backoff-with-jitter/"},{"t":"Making retries safe with idempotent APIs","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/making-retries-safe-with-idempotent-APIs/"}],"text":"A notification system turns one event (\"new message\", \"payment received\", \"outage\") into many deliveries over email, push and chat. The hard parts are fan-out, uneven load, third-party limits and not sending the same message twice or a million at once to a recovering dependency. What you will learn Design fan-out on write and fan-out on read, and when to mix them Use priorities, queues and per-provider limits Make delivery idempotent and avoid duplicate sends Protect providers and the system from retry storms 1. Clarify Producers publish events with a type, an audience and a payload. The system delivers to each recipient&#39;s chosen channels. Critical notices (security alerts, payment failures) are time sensitive; marketing can wait. Users set preferences and quiet hours. Duplicates are annoying, and for some notices harmful. SLO: 99 percent of critical notifications are handed to a provider within 10 seconds, bulk within 30 minutes. 2. Estimate 50 million users, 5 notifications per user per day: 250 million a day, about 2,900 per second average, bursts to 10x on events (a flash sale or an outage notice). A single broadcast to 20 million users must complete in minutes: 20 million over 600 seconds is about 33,000 per second sustained, far above the average. Provider limits apply: email and push providers cap per-second sends and per-recipient rates, so the egress rate is set by the providers, not by our fleet. 3. Sketch Event intake API, an event stream, a fan-out service that expands audiences into per-recipient messages, priority queues per channel, channel workers that call providers, a preference and deduplication store, a delivery status store, and a dead-letter queue. 4. Deep-dive Fan-out strategy. For targeted notices (one user), expand immediately. For huge audiences (all users), expanding to 20 million messages at intake would flood the queue and delay critical traffic. Expand in batches by partition of the user table, as background jobs that are rate limited, and keep them off the critical lane. That is fan-out on write for most cases, with controlled batching for broadcasts. Where a recipient pulls their feed (an in-app inbox), fan-out on read avoids storing millions of copies; hybrids store for normal users and read for celebrity-sized audiences. Priority lanes. Separate queues and workers for critical, normal and bulk traffic, with their own capacity so a marketing blast cannot delay a security alert. That is a bulkhead. Workers pull from critical first. Provider limits and backpressure. Each channel worker has a rate limiter matched to the provider&#39;s limits and a circuit breaker. If the provider slows or returns errors, the breaker opens, messages wait in the queue, and bulk is delayed before critical. Use a second provider for critical channels when the cost justifies it. Idempotency and dedup. Give each notification a unique id (event id plus recipient plus channel). Before sending, record the id in the dedup store in the same step as claiming the send; a redelivery from the queue finds the id and skips. Providers that accept an idempotency key should receive it. Accept that some paths are at-least-once and make duplicates harmless where possible. Retries. Retry transient failures with exponential backoff and jitter, a cap, and a retry budget per provider. Invalid addresses and unsubscribed users are not retried; failures go to a dead-letter queue with an owner. In January 2019 a retry storm from one application overloaded CoreDNS in Zalando&#39;s Kubernetes clusters, pods ran out of memory and DNS went down for over an hour: the same pattern applies to workers retrying a slow provider while also overloading name resolution. See the case file. Preferences and quiet hours. Check preferences at send time, not only at intake, because they change. Defer non-critical messages during quiet hours using a delayed queue, and spread the morning wake-up burst with jitter. 5. Failure modes Failure Effect Design answer "},"system-design/worked-design-a-metrics-and-log-pipeline":{"html":"\u003cp>Observability data is a high-volume, write-heavy workload with a special property: you need it most when everything else is failing, which is when it is hardest to keep running. The design must handle load spikes, bound cost and keep a path for the core signals alive.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Estimate ingest rates and storage for metrics and logs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Design a pipeline with buffering, backpressure and sampling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Control cardinality and retention\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep alerting working when the platform is not\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"1-clarify\">1. Clarify\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Collect metrics (numbers over time), logs (events) and traces from 2,000 services across several clusters. Dashboards for engineers, alerts to on-call, long-term retention for audits. Alerts on core service health must fire within 1 minute of a problem. Dashboards may lag by seconds. Losing some debug logs is acceptable; losing the alerts is not. Cost is a major constraint.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"2-estimate\">2. Estimate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Metrics: 2,000 services times 50 instances times 500 series each is 50 million active series. At a 15 second scrape interval that is about 3.3 million samples per second. At roughly 2 bytes per sample after compression, that is about 6.6 MB per second, 570 GB per day, before replication and indexes. Logs: 100,000 lines per second at 300 bytes is 30 MB per second, 2.6 TB a day. Logs cost far more than metrics, so retention and sampling decide the bill.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"3-sketch\">3. Sketch\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Agents or collectors on each node, a regional buffer (a log or queue), ingest workers that write to a time-series store for metrics, an indexed store for recent logs and cheap object storage for old logs, a query layer, an alert evaluator, and a notification path. Separate, small, independent path for core alerts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"4-deep-dive\">4. Deep-dive\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Buffering and backpressure.\u003c/strong> Put a durable buffer between collectors and storage so a slow store does not drop data or push back into applications. Bound the buffer; when it fills, drop the least valuable data first (debug logs, high-volume low-priority metrics), never application traffic. Collectors must never block the service they observe.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Cardinality control.\u003c/strong> The number of distinct series is the main cost and failure driver. One label with millions of values (user id, request id, full URL) can multiply series and take the store down. Enforce limits per team and per metric, reject or aggregate offending labels at ingest, alert on series growth, and keep high-cardinality detail in logs and traces. Allocate quotas per tenant so one team cannot starve others (a noisy-neighbour bulkhead).\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Sampling and tiering.\u003c/strong> Keep all error traces and slow traces, sample the rest. Downsample metrics (raw for 15 days, 5 minute for 13 months). Keep logs hot for a few days, warm for weeks, and cold in object storage with lifecycle rules. Sampling decisions should be consistent so a trace is complete.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Partitioning.\u003c/strong> Shard series by a hash of the series key across ingest nodes with replicas for durability; shard logs by time and tenant. Plan compaction and index maintenance (see the storage engine lesson): an ingest surge can build compaction debt that slows queries.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Alerting path.\u003c/strong> The alert evaluator should read from a fast, replicated store and be redundant across zones. Provide a minimal independent path: external probes against the user-facing endpoints, a separate small metrics store for a handful of core SLIs, a separate paging provider. Dead-man&#39;s-switch alerts (an alert that fires if the pipeline stops sending its heartbeat) catch silent failure of the monitoring itself. In November 2023 a power failure at the primary site for Cloudflare&#39;s control plane and analytics meant logs were lost for about two days and customers could not change settings; see the case file for the sourced account.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Multi-region.\u003c/strong> Collect locally and ship upward, so a region keeps its own view if the central site is unreachable. Replicate the core alerting data to a second region.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"5-failure-modes\">5. Failure modes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Failure\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Effect\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Design answer\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Ingest surge\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>buffers fill\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>bounded buffer, drop low priority, auto scale\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cardinality explosion\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>store overload\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>per-tenant limits, reject at ingest, alert on growth\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Storage node loss\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>gaps or slow queries\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>replicas, quorum writes, repair\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Query of death\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>store overloaded\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>query limits, timeouts, per-user quotas\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Pipeline silently stops\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>blind on-call\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>dead-man&#39;s switch, external probes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Region loss\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>no central view\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>regional stores, replicated core alerts\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cost overrun\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>budget cut leads to data loss\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>quotas, retention tiers, ingestion alerts\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"6-trade-offs\">6. Trade-offs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Dropping debug logs under pressure saves the pipeline and loses detail. Long raw retention is costly; downsampling loses spikes. Central storage is easy to query and a single point; regional storage is resilient and harder to query globally. Independent core alerting is another system to run. State the choices.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A developer adds a metric label containing the user id. What happens to a store that holds 50 million series, and what should prevent it?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Series count can multiply by millions, memory and index size explode, and ingest or queries fail for everyone. Enforce cardinality limits at ingest, reject or aggregate the label, and move user-level detail to logs or traces.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Cardinality is the multiplier on everything else.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The monitoring platform on the same cluster as the workload. Alerts that depend on the store that is overloaded. No alert for missing data. Logs ingested without limits. Dashboards that run expensive queries on every refresh.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-logs-lost-with-the-site\">Incident: Logs lost with the site\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The primary data centre for a control plane and analytics system lost power, and logs were lost for days.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"logctl status --region primary\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>logctl status --region primary\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">primary:   unreachable\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">secondary: standby (not receiving)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">last_replicated_event: 2023-11-02T08:41:12Z\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The console output is a simulated illustration. In the real incident on 2 November 2023, a power failure at a Flexential data centre in Portland took down the primary site of Cloudflare&#39;s control plane and analytics; customers could not change settings and logs were lost for about two days. See the case file. Design responses are standby capacity that actually receives data, regular failover tests, and keeping the customer data path independent of the control plane.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Design a metrics system for 2,000 services.&quot; Estimate series and samples, buffer and shed, control cardinality, tier retention, keep an independent core alert path with a dead-man&#39;s switch, and discuss multi-region.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No cardinality limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Monitoring that shares the failing platform\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unbounded buffers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No alert for missing data\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"1-clarify","text":"1. Clarify"},{"id":"2-estimate","text":"2. Estimate"},{"id":"3-sketch","text":"3. Sketch"},{"id":"4-deep-dive","text":"4. Deep-dive"},{"id":"5-failure-modes","text":"5. Failure modes"},{"id":"6-trade-offs","text":"6. Trade-offs"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-logs-lost-with-the-site","text":"Incident: Logs lost with the site"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Monitoring distributed systems","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/"},{"t":"OpenTelemetry signals","u":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/"}],"text":"Observability data is a high-volume, write-heavy workload with a special property: you need it most when everything else is failing, which is when it is hardest to keep running. The design must handle load spikes, bound cost and keep a path for the core signals alive. What you will learn Estimate ingest rates and storage for metrics and logs Design a pipeline with buffering, backpressure and sampling Control cardinality and retention Keep alerting working when the platform is not 1. Clarify Collect metrics (numbers over time), logs (events) and traces from 2,000 services across several clusters. Dashboards for engineers, alerts to on-call, long-term retention for audits. Alerts on core service health must fire within 1 minute of a problem. Dashboards may lag by seconds. Losing some debug logs is acceptable; losing the alerts is not. Cost is a major constraint. 2. Estimate Metrics: 2,000 services times 50 instances times 500 series each is 50 million active series. At a 15 second scrape interval that is about 3.3 million samples per second. At roughly 2 bytes per sample after compression, that is about 6.6 MB per second, 570 GB per day, before replication and indexes. Logs: 100,000 lines per second at 300 bytes is 30 MB per second, 2.6 TB a day. Logs cost far more than metrics, so retention and sampling decide the bill. 3. Sketch Agents or collectors on each node, a regional buffer (a log or queue), ingest workers that write to a time-series store for metrics, an indexed store for recent logs and cheap object storage for old logs, a query layer, an alert evaluator, and a notification path. Separate, small, independent path for core alerts. 4. Deep-dive Buffering and backpressure. Put a durable buffer between collectors and storage so a slow store does not drop data or push back into applications. Bound the buffer; when it fills, drop the least valuable data first (debug logs, high-volume low-priority metrics), never application traffic. Collectors must never block the service they observe. Cardinality control. The number of distinct series is the main cost and failure driver. One label with millions of values (user id, request id, full URL) can multiply series and take the store down. Enforce limits per team and per metric, reject or aggregate offending labels at ingest, alert on series growth, and keep high-cardinality detail in logs and traces. Allocate quotas per tenant so one team cannot starve others (a noisy-neighbour bulkhead). Sampling and tiering. Keep all error traces and slow traces, sample the rest. Downsample metrics (raw for 15 days, 5 minute for 13 months). Keep logs hot for a few days, warm for weeks, and cold in object storage with lifecycle rules. Sampling decisions should be consistent so a trace is complete. Partitioning. Shard series by a hash of the series key across ingest nodes with replicas for durability; shard logs by time and tenant. Plan compaction and index maintenance (see the storage engine lesson): an ingest surge can build compaction debt that slows queries. Alerting path. The alert evaluator should read from a fast, replicated store and be redundant across zones. Provide a minimal independent path: external probes against the user-facing endpoints, a separate small metrics store for a handful of core SLIs, a separate paging provider. Dead-man&#39;s-switch alerts (an alert that fires if the pipeline stops sending its heartbeat) catch silent failure of the monitoring itself. In November 2023 a power failure at the primary site for Cloudflare&#39;s control plane and analytics meant logs were lost for about two days and customers could not change settings; see the case file for the sourced account. Multi-region. Collect locally and ship upward, so a region keeps its own view if the central site is unreachable. Replicate the core alerting data to a second region. 5. Failure modes Failure Effect Design answer Ingest surge buffers fill bounded buffer, drop low priority, auto scale Cardinality explosi"},"system-design/worked-design-a-multi-tenant-kubernetes-platform":{"html":"\u003cp>An internal platform offers teams a place to run workloads without each building their own infrastructure. The design questions are isolation (how much can one tenant harm another), operability (how the platform itself is changed safely), and blast radius (how many tenants one mistake reaches).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choose a tenancy model: namespaces, clusters per team, or cells\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Apply quotas, policies and network rules for isolation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Plan cluster upgrades and add-on changes as staged rollouts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Identify the platform&#39;s shared dependencies\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"1-clarify\">1. Clarify\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>150 teams, 3,000 services, mixed criticality (customer-facing and batch). Teams self-serve namespaces, deployments and ingress. The platform team owns clusters, networking, ingress, DNS, observability agents and policies. Requirements: one team cannot starve or read another; platform upgrades cause no tenant downtime; cluster loss affects a bounded share; SLO for the control plane API 99.9 percent, for tenant workloads set by tenants.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"2-estimate\">2. Estimate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>3,000 services at an average of 6 pods is 18,000 pods. A single cluster is practical up to a few thousand nodes and tens of thousands of pods, but limits (API server load, etcd size, DNS, network policies, rate limits) tighten earlier. Design for about 8 clusters of 400 to 600 nodes in 2 regions, not one very large cluster: failure of one cluster affects roughly an eighth of workloads and upgrades can be staged.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"3-sketch\">3. Sketch\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Several clusters per region, each with the same platform layer (ingress, DNS, policy engine, observability agents, declarative delivery of platform config). A fleet controller assigns tenants to clusters. A global traffic layer routes by hostname. Shared services (identity, registry, secrets) are outside the clusters.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"4-deep-dive\">4. Deep-dive\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Tenancy model.\u003c/strong> Namespace-per-team in shared clusters is cheap and has weaker isolation; cluster-per-team gives strong isolation at high cost and operational load; a middle path groups tenants into a small number of clusters by risk tier (critical, standard, batch), so a noisy batch tenant cannot share a failure domain with payments.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Isolation controls.\u003c/strong> Resource quotas and limit ranges per namespace; requests and limits on every pod; priority classes so critical workloads preempt batch; pod security standards enforced by admission policy; network policies default deny with explicit allows; separate service accounts with least-privilege RBAC; node pools or taints for sensitive or noisy workloads; per-tenant limits on the number of objects (a tenant that creates a million objects can overload the API server and etcd).\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Platform dependencies.\u003c/strong> Cluster DNS, the CNI network plugin, the ingress controller, the policy webhooks, the registry and the identity provider are shared by every tenant in a cluster. A failing admission webhook can block all deployments; a broken CNI breaks all networking. Reddit&#39;s 2023 outage came from a Kubernetes upgrade from 1.23 to 1.24 that removed a node label the Calico route reflectors depended on; cluster networking broke and the site was down for about 314 minutes. Zalando&#39;s 2019 DNS overload and Preply&#39;s 2020 DNS and conntrack problems show the same theme: platform add-ons are single points per cluster. Give each a dependency review, redundancy, resource limits and a rollback.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Upgrades.\u003c/strong> Treat upgrades as staged rollouts: a canary cluster with synthetic and real low-risk tenants, a bake time, then clusters in groups across regions. Read deprecation notes and run checks for removed APIs and labels before each upgrade, and test the add-ons against the new version in a copy of production. Keep the ability to rebuild a cluster from declarative config quickly instead of repairing in place, and keep the previous control plane version rollable where the platform supports it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Control plane safety.\u003c/strong> Back up etcd (or the managed equivalent) and test restores; protect the API server from unbounded clients; keep admission webhooks fail-safe with timeouts and exclusions for system namespaces.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Cost and capacity.\u003c/strong> Use cluster autoscaling with maximums, per-team showback, and headroom for losing a zone (see the capacity lesson). Spread each service across zones with topology constraints and disruption budgets so node drains during upgrades do not take a service down.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"5-failure-modes\">5. Failure modes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Failure\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Effect\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Design answer\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Noisy tenant\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>neighbours starved\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>quotas, limits, priority, node pools\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Admission webhook fails\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>deployments blocked\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>timeouts, fail-safe policy, exclusions, redundancy\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Bad cluster upgrade\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>cluster-wide outage\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>canary cluster, checks, staged groups, rebuild path\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>DNS or CNI overload\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>cluster networking fails\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>sized replicas, limits, node-local caches, load tests\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>API server overload\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>control plane unusable\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>client limits, per-tenant object caps, priority and fairness\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Registry rate limit or outage\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>pods cannot start\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>internal mirror, cached images\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cluster loss\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>up to an eighth of workloads\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>multi-cluster deployment for critical services\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"6-trade-offs\">6. Trade-offs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>More clusters mean smaller blast radius and more operational work (more upgrades, more config). Shared clusters are efficient and demand strong policy. Strict network policy slows onboarding and prevents lateral movement. A mirror of images costs storage and avoids third-party rate limits (Docker Hub limits pulls by source address). State the tiering you chose.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A cluster upgrade removes a label that the network plugin's route reflectors need. What would have caught it before production, and what limits the damage?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Testing the add-ons against the new version in a copy of production and a canary cluster. Staged rollout across clusters limits the damage to one cluster, and a rebuild path restores it faster.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Upgrade the platform the way you would ship a risky release.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>One shared cluster for everything. Add-ons without redundancy or limits. Upgrades done everywhere in a weekend. Admission policies that block recovery actions. Tenants with no quotas.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-an-upgrade-that-broke-networking\">Incident: An upgrade that broke networking\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A control plane upgrade changed a label that the network layer depended on, and cluster networking failed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get nodes -l kubernetes.io/role=control-plane\ncalicoctl node status\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes -l kubernetes.io/role=control-plane\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">No resources found\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>calicoctl node status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">BGP peers: 0 established, 12 down\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The console output is a simulated illustration. The real Reddit incident on 14 March 2023 is described in the case file with a sourced timeline: an upgrade of an older cluster from 1.23 to 1.24, a removed node label, broken Calico route reflection, about 314 minutes of downtime. The design answers are pre-upgrade checks for removed labels and APIs, canary clusters, and a faster rebuild.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Design a multi-tenant Kubernetes platform.&quot; Choose tenancy tiers and several clusters per region, apply quotas, policies and network isolation, treat add-ons as single points, stage upgrades, back up and restore, and keep critical services spread across clusters.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>One giant shared cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating add-ons as invisible\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No tenant quotas or object limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading every cluster at once\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"1-clarify","text":"1. Clarify"},{"id":"2-estimate","text":"2. Estimate"},{"id":"3-sketch","text":"3. Sketch"},{"id":"4-deep-dive","text":"4. Deep-dive"},{"id":"5-failure-modes","text":"5. Failure modes"},{"id":"6-trade-offs","text":"6. Trade-offs"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-an-upgrade-that-broke-networking","text":"Incident: An upgrade that broke networking"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Multi-tenancy","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/multi-tenancy/"},{"t":"Resource quotas","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/policy/resource-quotas/"}],"text":"An internal platform offers teams a place to run workloads without each building their own infrastructure. The design questions are isolation (how much can one tenant harm another), operability (how the platform itself is changed safely), and blast radius (how many tenants one mistake reaches). What you will learn Choose a tenancy model: namespaces, clusters per team, or cells Apply quotas, policies and network rules for isolation Plan cluster upgrades and add-on changes as staged rollouts Identify the platform&#39;s shared dependencies 1. Clarify 150 teams, 3,000 services, mixed criticality (customer-facing and batch). Teams self-serve namespaces, deployments and ingress. The platform team owns clusters, networking, ingress, DNS, observability agents and policies. Requirements: one team cannot starve or read another; platform upgrades cause no tenant downtime; cluster loss affects a bounded share; SLO for the control plane API 99.9 percent, for tenant workloads set by tenants. 2. Estimate 3,000 services at an average of 6 pods is 18,000 pods. A single cluster is practical up to a few thousand nodes and tens of thousands of pods, but limits (API server load, etcd size, DNS, network policies, rate limits) tighten earlier. Design for about 8 clusters of 400 to 600 nodes in 2 regions, not one very large cluster: failure of one cluster affects roughly an eighth of workloads and upgrades can be staged. 3. Sketch Several clusters per region, each with the same platform layer (ingress, DNS, policy engine, observability agents, declarative delivery of platform config). A fleet controller assigns tenants to clusters. A global traffic layer routes by hostname. Shared services (identity, registry, secrets) are outside the clusters. 4. Deep-dive Tenancy model. Namespace-per-team in shared clusters is cheap and has weaker isolation; cluster-per-team gives strong isolation at high cost and operational load; a middle path groups tenants into a small number of clusters by risk tier (critical, standard, batch), so a noisy batch tenant cannot share a failure domain with payments. Isolation controls. Resource quotas and limit ranges per namespace; requests and limits on every pod; priority classes so critical workloads preempt batch; pod security standards enforced by admission policy; network policies default deny with explicit allows; separate service accounts with least-privilege RBAC; node pools or taints for sensitive or noisy workloads; per-tenant limits on the number of objects (a tenant that creates a million objects can overload the API server and etcd). Platform dependencies. Cluster DNS, the CNI network plugin, the ingress controller, the policy webhooks, the registry and the identity provider are shared by every tenant in a cluster. A failing admission webhook can block all deployments; a broken CNI breaks all networking. Reddit&#39;s 2023 outage came from a Kubernetes upgrade from 1.23 to 1.24 that removed a node label the Calico route reflectors depended on; cluster networking broke and the site was down for about 314 minutes. Zalando&#39;s 2019 DNS overload and Preply&#39;s 2020 DNS and conntrack problems show the same theme: platform add-ons are single points per cluster. Give each a dependency review, redundancy, resource limits and a rollback. Upgrades. Treat upgrades as staged rollouts: a canary cluster with synthetic and real low-risk tenants, a bake time, then clusters in groups across regions. Read deprecation notes and run checks for removed APIs and labels before each upgrade, and test the add-ons against the new version in a copy of production. Keep the ability to rebuild a cluster from declarative config quickly instead of repairing in place, and keep the previous control plane version rollable where the platform supports it. Control plane safety. Back up etcd (or the managed equivalent) and test restores; protect the API server from unbounded clients; keep admission webhooks fail-safe with timeouts and exclusions for s"},"system-design/worked-design-an-authoritative-dns-service":{"html":"\u003cp>DNS is the dependency under every other dependency. If your names stop resolving, nothing else matters, and your tools to fix it may depend on DNS too. Designing an authoritative service means designing for extreme availability, abuse and careful change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Estimate query load and design anycast serving\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep zone data consistent and changes safe\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Resist floods and avoid a single provider\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Plan for the failure of the service that serves its own names\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"1-clarify\">1. Clarify\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Serve authoritative answers for customer zones (or a company&#39;s internal and public zones). Queries are small UDP requests, with TCP and DNSSEC as required. Customers update records through an API and expect changes to appear globally within a minute. Availability target: 100 percent of queries answered in practice; a published SLO of 99.999 percent. A wrong answer is worse than no answer for many records.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"2-estimate\">2. Estimate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>100 million zones, 5 billion queries per day on average is about 58,000 per second, with attacks capable of 100x that. Each response is 100 to 500 bytes. A single modern server can answer hundreds of thousands of simple queries per second, so ordinary load is small; the design is driven by attack traffic, geographic latency and availability. Zone data: 100 million zones at about 1 KB each is about 100 GB, which fits in memory per node with compact encoding.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"3-sketch\">3. Sketch\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A control plane (API, validation, change pipeline, zone database) and a data plane (name servers in many locations announcing the same addresses by anycast). Zone data flows one way from control to data plane through a replicated distribution system, and each name server serves from a local copy so it can keep answering if the control plane is gone.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"4-deep-dive\">4. Deep-dive\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Anycast.\u003c/strong> Many sites announce the same IP addresses; routing sends each client to a nearby healthy site, and a failing site withdraws its routes. This absorbs floods (attack traffic spreads across sites) and cuts latency. Publish several name server addresses from different network prefixes and different providers so one routing problem or one provider cannot remove them all. In October 2016 an attack on Dyn, partly from devices infected with the Mirai botnet, made many popular sites unreachable for users on the US East Coast; sites that used only that provider had no alternative. See the case file.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Static stability.\u003c/strong> Each site keeps its last known good zone data and keeps answering when the control plane or distribution is down. Health checks withdraw a site from anycast only when it cannot answer correctly, and never withdraw everything: a rule that withdraws a site if it cannot reach its backend can withdraw every site when the backend fails. Facebook&#39;s outage on 4 October 2021 removed routes to its DNS servers after a configuration change on the backbone, and the name stopped resolving; the case file uses Cloudflare&#39;s outside view.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Zone data safety.\u003c/strong> Treat zone changes as deployments. Validate syntax and semantics before publishing, run checks for dangerous changes (deleting an apex record, large deletions, a change that would remove most records), stage by site or percentage, watch for answer anomalies, and keep versioned snapshots for instant rollback. In July 2021 a software configuration update triggered a bug in a DNS component of Akamai&#39;s Secure Edge CDN, and some customer websites were unavailable for up to about an hour until it was rolled back. See the case file.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Software diversity and safe releases.\u003c/strong> A bug in name server software hits every site that runs it. Run releases through canary sites, bake time and automatic rollback on answer error rate; consider running two independent implementations at different sites so one bug cannot take all of them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Abuse and floods.\u003c/strong> Rate limit per source with response rate limiting to blunt reflection abuse, drop malformed queries early, size for peak attack traffic with headroom, and use scrubbing for volumetric attacks. Keep TTLs on your own critical records sensible: short TTLs speed failover and increase query load; long TTLs hide problems for hours.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Consistency and propagation.\u003c/strong> Choose the freshness promise: changes visible within a minute at all sites. Distribute through a versioned log, show the version each site serves, and alert on sites that lag. Secondary DNS providers receive transfers or API updates; make sure the second provider is genuinely independent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Its own dependencies.\u003c/strong> The control plane, its authentication, and the tools used to repair the service must not depend on the service&#39;s own names. Keep break-glass access by IP address.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"5-failure-modes\">5. Failure modes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Failure\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Effect\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Design answer\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Volumetric attack\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>sites saturated\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>anycast spread, headroom, scrubbing, rate limits\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Bad zone change\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>wrong or empty answers\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>validation, staged rollout, snapshots, rollback\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Control plane down\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>no updates\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>sites serve last good data\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Bad software release\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>all sites crash\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>canary, bake, rollback, second implementation\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Site withdrawal cascade\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>no sites left\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>never withdraw all, health rules reviewed\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Provider outage\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>some addresses dark\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>multiple prefixes and providers\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Lag between sites\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>inconsistent answers\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>version tracking, lag alerts\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"6-trade-offs\">6. Trade-offs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Short TTLs help failover and increase load. Two software implementations double maintenance and cut correlated failure. Aggressive health-based withdrawal removes sick sites quickly and risks cascades. A second provider costs money and demands careful zone sync. Choose and write it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A health rule withdraws a site's anycast route if it cannot reach the zone database. The database fails. What happens?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Every site fails the check and withdraws its route, so no site is left to answer and the names stop resolving. Sites should serve their last good local data and never withdraw all at once.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Health checks can turn a backend fault into a total outage.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A single provider or single prefix. A health check that depends on the control plane. Zone changes pushed everywhere at once. The repair tools depending on the names that are down.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-when-the-name-servers-disappear\">Incident: When the name servers disappear\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The routes to the DNS servers were withdrawn and the name stopped resolving anywhere.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"dig +short NS example-service.net\nbgpctl show ip bgp 198.51.100.0/24\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dig +short NS example-service.net\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">;; connection timed out; no servers could be reached\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>bgpctl show ip bgp 198.51.100.0/24\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">network not in table\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The console output is a simulated illustration with documentation addresses. The real events in the case files are Facebook on 4 October 2021 (routes to its DNS servers withdrawn after a backbone configuration change), the Dyn attack of 21 October 2016, and the Akamai edge DNS bug of 22 July 2021. The shared design lessons are static stability, staged change, independent providers and recovery tools that do not need the broken names.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Design an authoritative DNS service.&quot; Use anycast across diverse prefixes and providers, local zone copies, validated and staged zone changes, rate limiting, canary software releases, static stability, and an independent control plane and break-glass access.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>All name servers on one provider or prefix\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Health checks that withdraw every site together\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Zone changes applied everywhere at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Repair tools that need the broken service\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"1-clarify","text":"1. Clarify"},{"id":"2-estimate","text":"2. Estimate"},{"id":"3-sketch","text":"3. Sketch"},{"id":"4-deep-dive","text":"4. Deep-dive"},{"id":"5-failure-modes","text":"5. Failure modes"},{"id":"6-trade-offs","text":"6. Trade-offs"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-when-the-name-servers-disappear","text":"Incident: When the name servers disappear"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"RFC 1034 Domain names: concepts","u":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1034"},{"t":"RFC 1035 Domain names: implementation","u":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1035"}],"text":"DNS is the dependency under every other dependency. If your names stop resolving, nothing else matters, and your tools to fix it may depend on DNS too. Designing an authoritative service means designing for extreme availability, abuse and careful change. What you will learn Estimate query load and design anycast serving Keep zone data consistent and changes safe Resist floods and avoid a single provider Plan for the failure of the service that serves its own names 1. Clarify Serve authoritative answers for customer zones (or a company&#39;s internal and public zones). Queries are small UDP requests, with TCP and DNSSEC as required. Customers update records through an API and expect changes to appear globally within a minute. Availability target: 100 percent of queries answered in practice; a published SLO of 99.999 percent. A wrong answer is worse than no answer for many records. 2. Estimate 100 million zones, 5 billion queries per day on average is about 58,000 per second, with attacks capable of 100x that. Each response is 100 to 500 bytes. A single modern server can answer hundreds of thousands of simple queries per second, so ordinary load is small; the design is driven by attack traffic, geographic latency and availability. Zone data: 100 million zones at about 1 KB each is about 100 GB, which fits in memory per node with compact encoding. 3. Sketch A control plane (API, validation, change pipeline, zone database) and a data plane (name servers in many locations announcing the same addresses by anycast). Zone data flows one way from control to data plane through a replicated distribution system, and each name server serves from a local copy so it can keep answering if the control plane is gone. 4. Deep-dive Anycast. Many sites announce the same IP addresses; routing sends each client to a nearby healthy site, and a failing site withdraws its routes. This absorbs floods (attack traffic spreads across sites) and cuts latency. Publish several name server addresses from different network prefixes and different providers so one routing problem or one provider cannot remove them all. In October 2016 an attack on Dyn, partly from devices infected with the Mirai botnet, made many popular sites unreachable for users on the US East Coast; sites that used only that provider had no alternative. See the case file. Static stability. Each site keeps its last known good zone data and keeps answering when the control plane or distribution is down. Health checks withdraw a site from anycast only when it cannot answer correctly, and never withdraw everything: a rule that withdraws a site if it cannot reach its backend can withdraw every site when the backend fails. Facebook&#39;s outage on 4 October 2021 removed routes to its DNS servers after a configuration change on the backbone, and the name stopped resolving; the case file uses Cloudflare&#39;s outside view. Zone data safety. Treat zone changes as deployments. Validate syntax and semantics before publishing, run checks for dangerous changes (deleting an apex record, large deletions, a change that would remove most records), stage by site or percentage, watch for answer anomalies, and keep versioned snapshots for instant rollback. In July 2021 a software configuration update triggered a bug in a DNS component of Akamai&#39;s Secure Edge CDN, and some customer websites were unavailable for up to about an hour until it was rolled back. See the case file. Software diversity and safe releases. A bug in name server software hits every site that runs it. Run releases through canary sites, bake time and automatic rollback on answer error rate; consider running two independent implementations at different sites so one bug cannot take all of them. Abuse and floods. Rate limit per source with response rate limiting to blunt reflection abuse, drop malformed queries early, size for peak attack traffic with headroom, and use scrubbing for volumetric attacks. Keep TTLs on your own critical records se"}});